2. Phase I Technical Objectives.
The overall objective of this Phase I effort is to determine the feasibility of adapting the current HALAR
laser system into a light-based counter-personnel Warn / Dazzle Gate Defense for perimeter security of
a base. The system must provide a visible warning and temporary visual disruption effect while remaining
within conservative eye-safety standards and employing robust safety interlocks. Specific objectives are: System Architecture Definition – Conduct a trade study of optical methods, beam modulation strategies, and integration approaches that enable a perceptible warning/dazzle effect while remaining
eye-safe. Identify and down-select candidate optical methods and integration strategies based on
performance, safety compliance, and retrofit feasibility.
2. Efficacy vs. Safety Characterization – Perform laboratory experiments and modeling to determine
which laser array pattern or combination of patterns is most effective. The team will quantify visual
disruption effects against ANSI/IEC safety thresholds, accounting for distance, ambient light levels,
and geometry.
3. Safety Architecture Specification – Define hardware and software safety interlocks, target
discrimination methods, and logging requirements to prevent accidental exposure or misuse.
4. Laboratory characterization – Build a conservative bench-scale prototype incorporating
recommended architecture and safety features. Measure beam and pattern characteristics under
controlled lab and outdoor conditions. . Compare results against safety standards to map the feasible
operating envelope.
5. Metrics and Phase II Transition Plan – Define quantitative criteria for success and establish
quantitative go/no-go metrics (safety compliance, perceptual effect, false activation rate, reliability)
