5. Relationship with Future Research or Research and Development
The Phase I effort is designed to establish the technical feasibility, safety parameters, and operational
value of a laser-based non-lethal warning and dazzle system for perimeter defense at military
installations. Results from modeling, simulation, and controlled laboratory testing will provide the data
necessary to determine effective laser powers, wavelengths, optical elements, irradiance thresholds, siting
geometry, and integration protocols within existing access control point (ACP) procedures.
In Phase II, this foundation will enable the design, development, and field demonstration of a prototype
system at an operationally relevant military base. Tasks will include hardware integration of an eye-safe
laser dazzle source, ruggedization for outdoor deployment, real-time control software, and fail-safe safety
interlocks. The Phase II prototype will be evaluated in partnership with installation security forces to
validate detection, deterrence, and escalation-of-force benefits under realistic traffic conditions.
Beyond Phase II, follow-on research will address system scaling (multi-gate coverage, mobile variants),
integration with automated surveillance and edge decision systems, and refinement of tactics, techniques,
and procedures (TTPs) to support widespread adoption. The long-term objective is to transition the
technology into a program of record as a non-lethal escalation layer for base security, with the aim of
providing security forces with more decision time and reducing reliance on barriers or lethal force while
enhancing deterrence and compliance at military entry control facilities.
6. Commercialization Strategy
The primary commercialization pathway for this technology is through transition into DoD security
programs, critical facility security and borer protection operations. Current physical security measures
rely on passive/active barriers and armed guards, with limited non-lethal escalation options. The proposed
system addresses this gap by providing a visible, controllable, and eye-safe deterrent layer that adds an
effective warning and enhances compliance. The long-term commercialization goal is to transition the
system into acquisition channels as a Program of Record for base security, with deployment across Army,
Navy, and Air Force installations.
The DoD manages ~4,790 sites worldwide and hundreds to thousands of individual facilities (bases,
ranges, depots, posts). That represents a large potential buyer base for military customers. The total
available market (TAM) for laser dazzlers is estimated at roughly USD 1.2 billion in 2024 with an
expected CAGR of 8%. The serviceable available market (SAM), focused on military bases and critical-
infrastructure facilities, is conservatively in the USD 750M-4B range depending on site count and units
per site. The Immobileyes team is partnering with Tinker AFB Security Forces to develop a laser-based a
C-UAS system and can leverage that relationship to pilot the LEOPARD system for AFB security.
Beyond the defense sector, the system has broad dual-use potential in critical
infrastructure protection. For example, the energy/power sector has about ~12,500 utility-
scale power plants in the U.S. alone (plus thousands of substations, distribution nodes,
and control centers). A warning and deterrence system at nuclear power plants, airports,
seaports, oil refineries, data centers, and other high-value facilities, represents significant
opportunities for commercialization in parallel with DoD transition.
The transition strategy will rely on partnering with established DoD primes and security
integrators to accelerate fielding, while pursuing direct engagements with
installation commands for pilot deployments. The team at Immobileyes have pre-
existing relationships with several Prime Contractors (Azimuth, UES, UTC and Booz Allen) that can
facilitate and successfully transition the technology to DoD customers. -- 15 of 28 -- Proposal Number N254-P01-0421 Immobileyes Inc.
