Sep 24, 2025 Sep 24, 2026 -- 28 of 28 --', '[{"id":"509177b5-6b98-4a82-a8df-4839e41290e2","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 1 of 28 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.850Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.850Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"71900f8d-a710-4635-83bd-d2b27623fcca","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Small Business Innovation Research (SBIR) Program - Proposal Cover Sheet\nDisclaimer\nKnowingly and willfully making any false, fictitious, or fraudulent statements or representations may be a\nfelony under the Federal Criminal False Statement Act (18 USC Sec 1001), punishable by a fine of up to $10,000,\nup to five years in prison, or both.\nSBIR Phase I Proposal\nProposal Number: N254-P01-0421\nProposal Title: LEOPARD: Light-based Electro-Optic Perimeter Active Response Defense\nAgency Information\nAgency Name: NAVY\nCommand: MCSC\nTopic Number: N254-P01\nFirm Information\nFirm Name: Immobileyes Inc.\nAddress: 1950 State Route 59, Kent, OH 44240-4118\nWebsite: www.immobileyes.com\nUEI: KL3MJVGD9XZ9\nDUNS: 117497611\nCAGE: 8KQ82\nSBA SBC Identification Number: 001832313\nFirm Certificate\nOFFEROR CERTIFIES THAT:"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.850Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.850Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"b37ca9e9-5dfe-4a50-8a7c-3eb1267c5c9f","type":"numbered_list","style":{},"content":{"items":[{"text":"It has no more than 500 employees, including the employees of its affiliates. YES"},{"text":"Number of employees including all affiliates (average for preceding 12 months) 39"},{"text":"The business concern meets the ownership and control requirements set forth in 13 C.F.R. Section"}]},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.850Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.850Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"90dc36ef-fcad-4394-9659-ca82cd48540d","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"121.702.\nYES\n4. Verify that your firm has registered in the SBAS Company Registry at www.sbir.gov by providing the\nSBC Control ID# and uploading the registration confirmation PDF:\nSBC_001832313\nSupporting Documentation:\nSBC_001832313.pdf\t•"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.850Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.850Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"778e241e-752a-43aa-9bc9-187b3507368f","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 2 of 28 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.850Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.850Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"00b2e1bf-caad-4f98-8326-d67c9b8e6eab","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"5. It has more than 50% owned by a single Venture Capital Owned Company (VCOC), hedge fund, or\nprivate equity firm\nNO\n6. It has more than 50% owned by multiple business concerns that are VOCs, hedge funds, or private\nequity firms?\nNO\n7. The birth certificates, naturalization papers, or passports show that any individuals it relies upon to\nmeet the eligibility requirements are U.S. citizens or permanent resident aliens in the United States.\nYES\n8. Is 50% or more of your firm owned or managed by a corporate entity? NO\n9. Is your firm affiliated as set forth in 13 CFR Section 121.103? YES\n10. It has met the performance benchmarks as listed by the SBA on their website as eligible to\nparticipate\nYES\n11. Firms PI, CO, or owner, a faculty member or student of an institution of higher education NO\n12. The offeror qualifies as a:\n[ ] Socially and economically disadvantaged SBC\n[X] Women-owned SBC\n[ ] HUBZone-owned SBC\n[ ] Veteran-owned SBC\n[ ] Service Disabled Veteran-owned SBC\n[ ] None Listed\n13. Race of the offeror:\n[ ] American Indian or Alaska Native\n[ ] Native Hawaiian or Other Pacific Islander\n[ ] Asian\n[ ] White\n[ ] Black or African American\n[X] Do not wish to Provide\n14. Ethnicity of the offeror: NON-\nHISPANIC\n15. It is a corporation that has some unpaid Federal tax liability that has been assessed, for which all\njudicial and administrative remedies have not been exhausted or have not lapsed, and that is not being\npaid in a timely manner pursuant to an agreement with the authority responsible for collecting the tax\nliability:\nFALSE\n16. Firm been convicted of a fraud-related crime involving SBIR and/or STTR funds or found civilly liable\nfor a fraud-related violation involving federal funds:\nNO\n17. Firms Principal Investigator (PI) or Corporate Official (CO), or owner been convicted of a fraud-related\ncrime involving SBIR and/or STTR funds or found civilly liable for a fraud-related violation involving federal\nfunds:\nNO\nFirm Affiliates:\nName Address Number of Employees\nAlphaMicron Inc. bahman@alphamicron.com\nKent, OH\n34"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.850Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.850Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"aea7b776-5959-45f2-9765-87c2f6aea28e","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 3 of 28 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.850Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.850Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"227f4282-06f9-485d-88bb-d135e7318538","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Signature:\nPrinted Name Signature Title Business Name Date\nAtossa Alavi Atossa M Alavi CEO Immobileyes Inc. 10/21/2020\nAudit Information\nSummary:\nHas your Firm ever had a DCAA review?NO\nVOL I - Proposal Summary\nSummary:\nProposed Base Duration (in months): 6\nOption 1 Duration (in months): 6\nTechnical Abstract:\nThis Phase I effort evaluates the feasibility of adapting the current IMMOBILEYES laser system into a light-based, non-lethal\nWarn/Dazzle Gate Defense for perimeter security. Addressing the need for layered deterrence at military access points, the\nproposed LEOPARD system (Light-based Electro-Optic Perimeter Active Response Defense) employs adaptive laser responses\nacross three zones: Warn, Dazzle, and Deny. Utilizing multiple wavelengths (green and blue), beam divergence control, and\nlaser splitting, LEOPARD maximizes effectiveness while maintaining eye safety. This approach allows for scalable, non-kinetic\nescalation against personnel or vehicles, with visible warnings and temporary visual disruption tailored to threat intent and\nproximity. Compared to conventional dazzlers, the system achieves wider area coverage, greater effective range, and reduced\npower requirements—demonstrating a 40× reduction in laser power while enhancing safety and performance.\nAnticipated Benefits/Potential Commercial Applications of the Research or Development:\nThe LEOPARD system offers a non-lethal, adaptive deterrence solution designed to enhance security at military bases, forward\noperating locations, and critical homeland infrastructure. By providing a visible warning and scalable visual disruption while\nmaintaining strict eye-safety, it addresses a key capability gap at access control points—reducing reliance on barriers or lethal\nforce. Potential applications include fixed site defense, expeditionary perimeter security, and protection of airfields, ports, and\nenergy facilities. The system’s low-power, beam-splitting laser architecture supports flexible deployment with minimal\nlogistical burden. LEOPARD’s ability to safely and effectively deter personnel or vehicles at range enables rapid threat"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.850Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.850Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"3752740f-4e62-43d0-b767-9cc769bad65b","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 4 of 28 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.850Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.850Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"2fd05b69-f757-4ce7-9436-5cc87aed525e","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"discrimination, de-escalation, and force protection in both CONUS and OCONUS environments. Its dual-use potential aligns\nwith DHS and DoD modernization priorities for non-kinetic layered defense.\nAttention:\nDisclaimer: For any purpose other than to evaluate the proposal, this data except proposal cover sheets shall not\nbe disclosed outside the Government and shall not be duplicated, used or disclosed in whole or in part, provided\nthat if a contract is awarded to this proposer as a result of or in connection with the submission of this data, the\nGovernment shall have the right to duplicate, use or disclose the data to the extent provided in the funding\nagreement. This restriction does not limit the Government''s right to use information contained in the data if it is\nobtained from another source without restriction. This restriction does not apply to routine handling of proposals\nfor administrative purposes by Government support contractors. The data subject to this restriction is contained\non the pages of the proposal listed on the line below.\nAddition:\nEnter the page numbers separated by a space of the pages in the proposal that are considered proprietary:\n5 7 9\nList a maximum of 8 Key Words or phrases, separated by commas, that describe the Project:\nNon-lethal deterrence, Directed Energy, Perimeter security, Electro-optical defense, Adaptive threat response,\nLayered defense, Optical warning, Low collateral defeat\nVOL I - Proposal Certification\nSummary:"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.850Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.850Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"d45779a3-0140-459d-aa4e-1630cd3f72c3","type":"numbered_list","style":{},"content":{"items":[{"text":"At a minimum, two thirds of the work in Phase I will be carried out by your small business as defined by 13 C.F.R"}]},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.850Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.850Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"ff3428bf-437e-456c-b44e-ef0077493e20","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Section 701-705. The numbers for this certification are derived from the budget template. To update these\nnumbers, review and revise your budget data. If the minimum percentage of work numbers are not met, then a\nletter of explanation or written approval from the funding officer is required.\nPlease note that some components will not accept any deviation from the Percentage of Work (POW) minimum\nrequirements. Please check your component instructions regarding the POW requirements.\nYES\nFirm POW 100%\nSubcontractor POW 0%\n2. Is primary employment of the principal investigator with your firm as defined by 13 C.F.R Section 701-705? YES\n3. During the performance of the contract, the research and development will be performed in the\nUnited States?\nYES\n4. During the performance of the contract, the research and development will be performed at the\nofferors facilities by the offerors employees except as otherwise indicated in the technical\nproposal?\nYES"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.850Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.850Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"7c68e377-7274-461b-9c0d-775ea9ba9e6f","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 5 of 28 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.850Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.850Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"14319a75-7f7f-4e09-b760-121ce2ade0b3","type":"heading","style":{},"content":{"text":"5. Do you plan to use Federal facilities, laboratories, or equipment? NO","level":1},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.850Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.850Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"34ae8432-0c59-4960-9fcb-28a7c6ef4873","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"6. The offeror understands and shall comply with export control regulations. YES\n7. There will be ITAR/EAR data in this work and/or deliverables. NO\n8. Has a proposal for essentially equivalent work been submitted to other US government agencies or DoD\ncomponents?\nNO\n9. Has a contract been awarded for any of the proposals listed above? NO\n10. Firm will notify the Federal agency immediately if all or a portion of the work authorized and funded\nunder this proposal is subsequently funded by another Federal agency?\nYES\n11. Are you submitting assertions in accordance with DFARS 252.227-7017 Identification and assertions use,\nrelease, or disclosure restriction?\nNO\n12. Are you proposing research that utilizes human/animal subjects or a recombinant DNA as described in DoDI\n3216.01, 32 C.F.R. Section 219, and National Institutes of Health Guidelines for Research Involving Recombinant DNA\nof the solicitation?\nNO\n13. Are teaming partners or subcontractors proposed? NO\n14. Are you proposing to use foreign nationals as defined in 22 CFR 120.16 for work under the proposed effort? YES\n15. What percentage of the principal investigators total time will be on the project? 25%\n16. Is the principal investigator socially/economically disadvantaged? NO\n17. Does your firm allow for the release of its contact information to Economic Development Organizations? YES\nVOL I - Contact Information\nPrincipal Investigator\nName: Dr. Atossa Alavi\nPhone: (330) 819-5157\nEmail: atossa@immobileyes.com\nAddress: 1950 State Route 59, Kent, OH 44240 - 4376\nCorporate Official\nName: Dr. Kamalika Ghatak\nPhone: (734) 548-2812\nEmail: programs@immobileyes.com\nAddress: 1950 State Route 59, Kent, OH 44240 - 4118\nAuthorized Contract Negotiator\nName: Dr. Atossa Alavi\nPhone: (330) 819-5157\nEmail: atossa@immobileyes.com"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.850Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.850Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"25386357-80bb-4e04-b907-d7df329d84b5","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 6 of 28 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.850Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.850Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"af568430-7768-4002-9869-45d23e772a83","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Address: 1950 State Route 59, Kent, OH 44240 - 4376\nForm Generated on 09/24/2025 11:01:21 AM"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.850Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.850Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"bd098e11-2c3b-483a-b60a-6cf58edf0f09","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 7 of 28 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.850Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.850Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"b1fbd42c-bb91-43d9-a5ef-1e6fe4786350","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Proposal Number N254-P01-0421 Immobileyes Inc.\nTopic Number N254-P01\nUse or disclosure of data contained on this page is subject to the restriction on the first page of this volume.\nPage 1 of 10\nTitle: LEOPARD: Light-based Electro-Optic Perimeter Active Response Defense\nVolume 2: Technical Volume\nThis proposal includes data that must not be disclosed outside the Government and must not be duplicated, used, or\ndisclosed-in whole or in part-for any purpose other than to evaluate this proposal. If, however, a contract is\nawarded to this proposer as a result of-or in connection with-the submission of this data, the Government has the\nright to duplicate, use, or disclose the data to the extent provided in the resulting contract. This restriction does not\nlimit the Government''s right to use information contained in this data if it is obtained from another source without\nrestriction. The data subject to this restriction are contained in pages 1-10."},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.850Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.850Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"c61899e5-2ab6-4aeb-bd16-f287ec4dd095","type":"numbered_list","style":{},"content":{"items":[{"text":"Identification and Significance of the Problem or Opportunity."},{"text":"Problem"}]},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.850Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.850Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"b271598f-7fbf-4b5b-884b-1ce14744c99b","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Military installations face a persistent challenge at access control points: some individuals who approach\nare careless or unaware, while others may be adversarial. Distinguishing between the two in real time is\ndifficult. Current reliance on barriers and armed response forces commanders to choose between\nunderreacting to a genuine threat or overreacting to a benign incident, both of which carry operational,\nlegal, and public safety risks. A layered, non-lethal response option is needed to manage this uncertainty\nand reduce reliance on force escalation.\nThe central challenge is to create a graduated deterrence system that can stop adversarial movement\nearly and safely before it escalates, while minimizing permanent harm\nTo this end, conventional laser dazzlers, or\nnon-lethal optical distractors, have been used to\ncause temporary visual disruption and impair\nhuman performance without causing permanent\neye damage. These devices have been used\nsuccessfully in conflict zones to hail, warn and\nsuppress individuals from a distance, providing\nnon-lethal area denial, disrupting continuous\nvisual observation, and providing the target\nwith an opportunity to clarify intent.\nHowever, their use in CONUS has been limited\ndue to legal and regulatory restrictions and the\nrisk of eye-damage. This risk is heightened\nbecause conventional dazzlers rely on a single laser beam diverging to deliver the required dazzling\nintensity at a great distance, delivering unsafe levels of laser intensity closer to the system. For example,\nto achieve a 0.1mW/cm2 irradiance distributed over a 3m spot at a distance of 300m, the Distributed\nSound and Light Array (DSLA) needs to use a 10W Coherent laser. 1 Since the area coverage is achieved\nby simply diverging a single laser beam, bystanders at distances closer to the source will experience\nsignificantly high laser powers that are greater than the Maximum Permissible Exposure (MPE) limit,\n1 Distributed Sound and Light Array (DSLA), https://jifco.defense.gov/Press-Room/Fact-Sheets/Article-View-\nFact-sheets/Article/577990/distributed-sound-and-light-array/"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.850Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.850Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"e6d54ae6-1e21-4de1-8d8d-9e6d9c0930b4","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 8 of 28 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.850Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.850Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"b82c5d3d-5586-4d96-a3f1-c463b6c2387c","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Proposal Number N254-P01-0421 Immobileyes Inc.\nTopic Number N254-P01\nUse or disclosure of data contained on this page is subject to the restriction on the first page of this volume.\nPage 2 of 10\nthereby exposing bystanders to permanent eye damage. Table 2 below shows the illuminance threshold\nlevels for eye-damage vs dazzle, glare and distraction. 2\nB. Proposed Solution\nImmobileyes is developing a low size, weight, power and cost (SWAP-C) laser system, HALAR, that\nuses multiple laser wavelengths, divergences and laser beam splitting to creates three operational zones:"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.850Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.850Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"b7f02e3f-3f87-41ac-aaed-d07d77cc3da9","type":"bulleted_list","style":{},"content":{"items":[{"text":"Zone 1 – Warn/Deter,"},{"text":"Zone 2 – Dazzle / Disrupt,"},{"text":"Zone 3 – Deny/Disable,"}]},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.851Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.851Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"f9359049-2d2b-4433-8670-ba3cdcfe7ced","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"giving operators an adaptive response tailored to the threat, distance\nand intent. The current system is optimized for C-sUAS defense.\nFor this Phase I effort, Immobileyes proposes adapting its existing\ntechnology to create LEOPARD, Light-based Electro-Optic\nPerimeter Active Response Defense to create Warn, Dazzle and\nDeny zones.\nThe Warn response uses eye-safe lasers to deliver a clear visual warning to approaching individuals or\nvehicles. If the subject / vehicle continues to advance, the system escalates to a Dazzle / Glare response,\nemploying controlled irradiance to disrupt direct observation and hinder continuous surveillance.\nShould the threat persist, the system can “paint” the target with an eye-safe laser to aid in escalating to a\nDeny response, utilizing additional non-kinetic or kinetic measures. This adaptive escalation framework\nis effective against both personnel and vehicles. Green and blue laser wavelengths are particularly\neffective in producing temporary glare, visual disorientation, or complete obstruction, compelling a driver\nto halt without inflicting permanent harm.\nOur team has discovered that in certain circumstances, a blue (450 nm) laser is more effective at causing\nglare effects. In addition, using both blue and green wavelengths defeats standard laser eye protection,\n2 Alexander Toet, et al, \"Optical countermeasures against CLOS weapon systems,\" Proc. SPIE 8898,\nTechnologies for Optical Countermeasures X; and High-Power Lasers 2013: Technology and Systems, 88980L (15\nOctober 2013); https://doi.org/10.1117/12.2028342\nPTZ\nCamer\na\nLasers\nThree Zones of defense\nMulti WL dazzle effects Blue (450nm) laser dazzle effect on drone camera at 200 m"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.851Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.851Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"4bc4c525-7c8b-4516-9974-f73b79678380","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 9 of 28 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.851Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.851Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"1b638726-2626-44de-b7b7-0c4968cc2d92","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Proposal Number N254-P01-0421 Immobileyes Inc.\nTopic Number N254-P01\nUse or disclosure of data contained on this page is subject to the restriction on the first page of this volume.\nPage 3 of 10\nsince protective lenses cannot block both colors simultaneously.\nAt the core of the system is the patented HALAR system, developed by Immobileyes under an Air Force\nSTTR (now TACFI) contract with the support of Tinker Af Security Forces Squadron, delivering a low-\nSWAP-C counter UAV solution that includes multiple lasers integrated into an EO/IR camera for\ndetection & tracking. The system enables a scalable, low-collateral defeat Warn and Dazzle response\nprior to kinetic engagement, desirable for a base in an urban setting.\nIn addition to the 2 lasers emitters, the system employs a laser range finder\nfor precise ranging and can include any number of fixed cameras to\nprovide up to 360o surveillance at any point of entry.\nC: Innovation / Differentiation from conventional dazzlers\nI. “SHOT GUN approach:\nThe Immobileyes laser system is uniquely capable of switching from a\nsingle laser to a cluster of low-divergence laser beams (or beamlets).\nThe number of beamlets and the projected pattern is highly adaptable and\ncan be designed to cover a specified area at various distances. The low\ndivergence of each beamlet means it can deliver the required intensity at\ngreater distances. Using both diverging optics and splitting optics makes\nthe system superior to conventional dazzlers. Beam splitting/shaping is highly adaptable and results in:\n(1) increased effective range,\n(2) wider area cover,\n(3) reduced laser power,\n(4) increased eye-safety over a longer range,\n(5) reduced SWAP-C of the system,\n(6) an adaptable system that can provide\ndifferent responses.\nFor example, using the DSLA as comparison, a cluster of low divergence beams from a 250mW laser can\ndeliver the same intensity over a 3m spot at 300m as compared to the DSLA’s 10W laser. This is an X40\nreduction in power.\nLow SWAP-C Laser +\nEO/IR detection &\ntracking C-sUAS\n360 Fixed\nCameras"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.851Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.851Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"3658c050-61c9-4ea8-b091-78b5c34cd7c8","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 10 of 28 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.851Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.851Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"deae91cb-9ee1-41b4-8e63-57206f821386","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Proposal Number N254-P01-0421 Immobileyes Inc.\nTopic Number N254-P01\nUse or disclosure of data contained on this page is subject to the restriction on the first page of this volume.\nPage 4 of 10\nII. Real-time beam shaping:\nFor effective real-time adaptation, Immobileyes is developing an opto-electronic beam shaping element\nthat uses liquid crystals for rapid transformation of an input laser beam into a variety of beam profiles.\nThis provides electrically tunable beam shaping that provides instant laser beam adjustment based on\ntarget distance and desired response, giving operators greater flexibility.\nIII. Laser eye dazzle safety model\nThe team has experience developing an eye-safe laser-based deterrence for civilian law enforcement\nagencies in response to active shooter incidents. This work focused on optimizing safety levels based on\nthe ANSI and IEC metrics for MPE and Nominal Ocular Hazard Distance or NOHD (the distance used to\nquantify the safe distance to operate from a laser system to avoid any risk of eye damage). Further\ndevelopment will incorporate the additional Maximum Dazzle Exposure (MDE) model put forward by\nWilliamson and McLin3 to derive the “effective range” – i.e. the distance where the dazzler effect is seen\nwithout posing any danger of eye damage.\nMDE is defined as the laser irradiance at the\neye above which an object cannot be visually\ndetected through the dazzle field. At laser\nirradiances higher than the MDE, the dazzle\nfield prevents the observer from detecting the\nobject. MDE levels depend on ambient\nlighting conditions, wavelength of the laser,\nsize and contrast of the object being observed\nand the observer. This model is based on a\nstatic, single wavelength laser and we believe\nusing two different wavelength lasers will\nincrease dazzle and glare effects.\nNevertheless, this model can be used to quantify dazzle levels with some predictability. The MDE Model\n(shown here in Table VI) uses human experimental data and extrapolated numbers and can be used to\ndevelop the LEOPARD Model.\nIV. Use of flashing color LED light for effective warning / distraction\nIn addition to its C-UAS laser dazzler, Immobileyes has developed and commercialized an\nLED-based optical disrupter for civilian law enforcement. The handheld model is a\ncompact, law-enforcement-designed LED system that adds a patented “Optical Stun\nMode” to tactical lights by alternating intense red and green beams. A non-pyro diversion\ndevice is under development.\nOur team’s research during device development showed that alternating red/green flashing\nlights exploit three key effects: (1) human visual physiology, where green appears\nbrighter and mixed colors disrupt depth perception; (2) photic or flicker-induced\ndisruption, in which rapid flashing interferes with the brain’s ability to process\ncontinuous visual input; and (3) color-flicker interference, where alternating\ncolors overload the visual cortex and slow reaction times. Together, these\nmechanisms create rapid, non-lethal disorientation that helps security personnel\nregain control with less force. Building on these findings, we will evaluate\nwhether high-intensity red/green LEDs can be integrated into the system as an\ninitial warning layer prior to activating laser dazzler effects.\n3 Craig A. Williamson & Leon Mclin, “Determination of a laser eye dazzle safety framework” (2018)\nJournal of Laser Applications 30(3):032010. https://doi.org/10.2351/1.5029384\nLED-based optical\ndisruptors for law\nenforcement"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.851Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.851Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"469c6203-e3b8-417b-9abf-9b65dd31b082","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 11 of 28 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.851Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.851Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"4c054372-abad-4775-a8e9-ac2f94d2c7ae","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Proposal Number N254-P01-0421 Immobileyes Inc.\nTopic Number N254-P01\nUse or disclosure of data contained on this page is subject to the restriction on the first page of this volume.\nPage 5 of 10\n2. Phase I Technical Objectives.\nThe overall objective of this Phase I effort is to determine the feasibility of adapting the current HALAR\nlaser system into a light-based counter-personnel Warn / Dazzle Gate Defense for perimeter security of\na base. The system must provide a visible warning and temporary visual disruption effect while remaining\nwithin conservative eye-safety standards and employing robust safety interlocks. Specific objectives are:"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.851Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.851Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"1cff6919-4fd1-400a-8333-b312c52b1929","type":"numbered_list","style":{},"content":{"items":[{"text":"System Architecture Definition – Conduct a trade study of optical methods, beam modulation"}]},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.851Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.851Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"a2da36fa-094a-421d-8af6-0e23fb963c3d","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"strategies, and integration approaches that enable a perceptible warning/dazzle effect while remaining\neye-safe. Identify and down-select candidate optical methods and integration strategies based on\nperformance, safety compliance, and retrofit feasibility.\n2. Efficacy vs. Safety Characterization – Perform laboratory experiments and modeling to determine\nwhich laser array pattern or combination of patterns is most effective. The team will quantify visual\ndisruption effects against ANSI/IEC safety thresholds, accounting for distance, ambient light levels,\nand geometry.\n3. Safety Architecture Specification – Define hardware and software safety interlocks, target\ndiscrimination methods, and logging requirements to prevent accidental exposure or misuse.\n4. Laboratory characterization – Build a conservative bench-scale prototype incorporating\nrecommended architecture and safety features. Measure beam and pattern characteristics under\ncontrolled lab and outdoor conditions. . Compare results against safety standards to map the feasible\noperating envelope.\n5. Metrics and Phase II Transition Plan – Define quantitative criteria for success and establish\nquantitative go/no-go metrics (safety compliance, perceptual effect, false activation rate, reliability)\nObjective 1:\nSystem architecture:\nHALAR is flexible\nand can be designed\nto have 10 to 10,000\ndirected beams with\ncustomizable energy\nlevels, individual\nbeam divergence, and\noverall pattern\ndivergence (distance\nbetween spots), and\nthe component\ncombinations can\ndeliver a different\neffective range.\nObjective 1 is to\ndetermine the best\ncombination of beam\nsplitting / divergence\nto deliver the desired\neffectiveness."},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.851Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.851Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"e0943a6f-3eb3-4181-8390-0cf95fe6a136","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 12 of 28 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.851Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.851Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"e4599b48-5f49-46b1-a29f-16f561e1e873","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Proposal Number N254-P01-0421 Immobileyes Inc.\nTopic Number N254-P01\nUse or disclosure of data contained on this page is subject to the restriction on the first page of this volume.\nPage 6 of 10\nand develop a detailed Phase II plan with milestones and risk mitigation strategies addressing\nintegration, ruggedization, and operational evaluation.\n3. Phase I (Base and Option) Statement of Work\nSOW\nTask\nNo. Title Description Performer Deliverable &\nSchedule\nBase Period\n1 Kickoff meeting Meet with TPOC to review project objectives,\nnon-lethal warning system concept,\nsafety/ethical boundaries, and anticipated\noutcomes.\nFinalize and agree upon the Statement of Work\n(SOW), deliverables, and approval milestones.\nIMM A + 15 days\nKickoff\nBriefing\n2 System Architecture\nTrade Study\nIdentify and down-select candidate\noptical/beam modulation approaches that\nprovide perceptible warning/dazzle effects\nwhile meeting ANSI/IEC eye-safety standards.\nProduce an architecture recommendation report.\nIMM A + 90 days\nProgress\nReport\n3 Laboratory\nCharacterization\nEstablish a matrix of expected visual disruption\neffects based on laboratory testing. Analyze\ndata across distance, geometry, and ambient\nlight variables.\nIMM\n4 Go/No-Go Metrics &\nPhase II Transition\nPlan,\nDefine quantitative metrics for feasibility\n(safety compliance, perceptual effect, false\nactivation rates, reliability). Deliver a Phase II\ndevelopment plan with milestones and risk\nmitigation strategies.\nIMM A+ 180 days\nFinal Report,\nQuad Chart,\nPII Draft\nProposal\nOption Period\n5 Bench-Scale Prototype\nDemonstration\nBuild and test a conservative demonstrator\nincorporating down-selected architecture and\nsafety features. Conduct controlled lab and\nlimited outdoor demonstrations.\nIMM +\nRLI\nsubcontr\nactor\nA + 270 days\nProgress\nReport\n6 Engineering control\ndetermination to\nmaximize utility for a\ngiven laser source\nDetermine optimized divergence / beam\nsplitting for desired effects based on distance to\ntarget using integrated laser range finder output\nIMM\n7 Safety Architecture\nSpecification, Progress\nReport\nDefine system-level interlocks, software\nsafeguards, and operational constraints to\nprevent accidental exposure or misuse.\nDocument requirements and verification plan.\nIMM A+ 360 days\nFinal Report,\nFinal Phase II\nProposal\n4. Related Work.\nTitle: Innovative Directed Energy Counter UAS solution Integrated into Proven Detection, Tracking and\nTargeting System, AF STTR P II (4/3/2024-1/3/2026) & TACFI AF PY25.2 (11/5/2025-11/4/2027)"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.851Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.851Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"c7c1a2cb-6625-4f63-89be-d791f337ad80","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 13 of 28 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.851Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.851Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"e56c9fca-6e60-4e23-b5fc-a198a4cf2271","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Proposal Number N254-P01-0421 Immobileyes Inc.\nTopic Number N254-P01\nUse or disclosure of data contained on this page is subject to the restriction on the first page of this volume.\nPage 7 of 10\nPrimary End-User Points of Contact Robert N.J. Clouse, Colonel, USAF Commander, 72d Mission\nSupport Group, robert.clouse@us.af.mil\nPrimary TPOC: Timothy Culp, TSgt/E-6, 72D SFS/S6, E-6, NCOIC, sUAS/Comm, 72d Security Forces\nSquadron, Tinker AFB, timothy.culp@us.af.mil, DSN: (405)-734-1901\nSummary: The main objective of the AF STTR effort is to develop HALAR-L, an innovative\nelectromagnetic optical disruption solution designed to effectively deny intelligence collection and\nintrusion by small Unmanned Aerial Systems (sUAS) within restricted areas. This system employs a\nmulti-beam laser approach to Warn, Dazzle and Deny sUAS sensors. HALAR-L integrates with a\nsophisticated detect and track unit, enabling both standalone and mobile platform deployment for\nmaximum operational flexibility. Critically, the system''s architecture is scalable, accommodating both\nlow-power, eye-safe lasers for urban operations and high-power lasers for more robust protection\nagainst advanced sUAS threats. A key objective of the HALAR-L program is to minimize size, weight,\npower (SWaP), complexity, and cost per unit. This focus on SWaP-C reduction will ensure HALAR-L''s\nversatility and adaptability across diverse operational environments, including complex urban settings\nwith civilian infrastructure and air traffic considerations with an effort to minimize collateral effects.\nTitle: “Enhanced OPSEC for Air Force Mission Functions Utilizing a Networkable Nonlethal Directed\nEnergy Solution” AF STTR Phase I (5/5/2023- 8/7/2023) RI Partner- The Ohio State University\nSummary: Immobileyes HALAR solutions, in conjunction with computer vision systems, can provide an\nimmediate non-lethal deterrent and counter-intelligence capability in areas deemed sensitive. The system\ndistracts and disrupts visual perception in the affected area. The same system can also be used to mitigate\nthe threat of active shooters or other engagements which would benefit from disrupting visual acuity. The\nsolutions utilize 100% eye-safe certified LEDs which are programmable to allow for patterns of effect.\nTargeted End Users – Any Security Forces organization tasked with controlling sensitive areas and/or\ninformation. Defenders with responsibility for base security and access denial.\nTitle: “Throwable Portable HALAR Visual Disrupter for Air Force Rapid Tactical & Security Operations”\nAF STTR Phase I (5/5/2023- 8/7/2023) RI - Kent State University\nSummary: HALAR is an innovative patented platform technology representing an innovative leap\nforward in optical distraction, disorientation, diversion, and dazzling non-lethal threat mitigation\nsolutions. The HALAR solutions represent the first non-laser eye-safe non-lethal system designed for\nMilitary Security Forces and Law Enforcement missions. The HALAR utilizes high-power multi-color\nLEDs that are programable allowing them to flash in a particular sequence known to cause physiological\neffects such as: dizziness, disorientation, glare, and dazzle/flash blind effects.\nTitle: “Small UAS Detection and Classification for Rapid Multiple C-UAS Operations using a Novel\nMulti-Wavelength Directed Energy Technology” AF STTR Phase I (5/5/2023- 8/7/2023) RI - UT\nSummary: HALAR Laser Storm is a patented, scalable, and programmable laser-emitting device\ncapable of projecting a “shower” of multiple wavelength laser beams adapted to overwhelm multiple\nsensors. It offers a novel approach in the use of multiple laser lights as a force multiplier for counter\nsurveillance, counter unmanned aerial systems, and other value-added Air Force applications. In this\nproject, Immobileyes will to test the feasibility of adapting its technology to “Track and Blind” multiple\nUAVs in a low C-SwaP system.\nTitle: “Intelligent Air Laser System for Mission Support w/ Multi UAVs Equipped with Laser Beams”\nAF STTR Phase I (4/24/2021-7/23/2021) RI: Kent State University\nUnder this project, Immobileyes evaluated developing a rapidly deployable UAS-mounted non-lethal\nIntelligent Air Laser (AIL) System to provide USAF Security Forces with a rapid & safe technology\ncapable of dazzling, distracting, and disrupting hostile threats utilizing hundreds of multicolored moving"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.851Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.851Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"dd06afa9-1b84-42a2-964f-65bba989d1d5","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 14 of 28 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.851Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.851Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"5a82b31f-d9a2-49c6-a0a6-cb24fc88ac4f","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Proposal Number N254-P01-0421 Immobileyes Inc.\nTopic Number N254-P01\nUse or disclosure of data contained on this page is subject to the restriction on the first page of this volume.\nPage 8 of 10\nlaser beams programmed for targeted visual disruption. This project successfully demonstrated the ability\nof directed energy optical solutions to provide valuable tactical and operational advantages to the Air\nForce while ensuring that engagement of any potential threats was non-lethal and non-harmful.\n5. Relationship with Future Research or Research and Development\nThe Phase I effort is designed to establish the technical feasibility, safety parameters, and operational\nvalue of a laser-based non-lethal warning and dazzle system for perimeter defense at military\ninstallations. Results from modeling, simulation, and controlled laboratory testing will provide the data\nnecessary to determine effective laser powers, wavelengths, optical elements, irradiance thresholds, siting\ngeometry, and integration protocols within existing access control point (ACP) procedures.\nIn Phase II, this foundation will enable the design, development, and field demonstration of a prototype\nsystem at an operationally relevant military base. Tasks will include hardware integration of an eye-safe\nlaser dazzle source, ruggedization for outdoor deployment, real-time control software, and fail-safe safety\ninterlocks. The Phase II prototype will be evaluated in partnership with installation security forces to\nvalidate detection, deterrence, and escalation-of-force benefits under realistic traffic conditions.\nBeyond Phase II, follow-on research will address system scaling (multi-gate coverage, mobile variants),\nintegration with automated surveillance and edge decision systems, and refinement of tactics, techniques,\nand procedures (TTPs) to support widespread adoption. The long-term objective is to transition the\ntechnology into a program of record as a non-lethal escalation layer for base security, with the aim of\nproviding security forces with more decision time and reducing reliance on barriers or lethal force while\nenhancing deterrence and compliance at military entry control facilities.\n6. Commercialization Strategy\nThe primary commercialization pathway for this technology is through transition into DoD security\nprograms, critical facility security and borer protection operations. Current physical security measures\nrely on passive/active barriers and armed guards, with limited non-lethal escalation options. The proposed\nsystem addresses this gap by providing a visible, controllable, and eye-safe deterrent layer that adds an\neffective warning and enhances compliance. The long-term commercialization goal is to transition the\nsystem into acquisition channels as a Program of Record for base security, with deployment across Army,\nNavy, and Air Force installations.\nThe DoD manages ~4,790 sites worldwide and hundreds to thousands of individual facilities (bases,\nranges, depots, posts). That represents a large potential buyer base for military customers. The total\navailable market (TAM) for laser dazzlers is estimated at roughly USD 1.2 billion in 2024 with an\nexpected CAGR of 8%. The serviceable available market (SAM), focused on military bases and critical-\ninfrastructure facilities, is conservatively in the USD 750M-4B range depending on site count and units\nper site. The Immobileyes team is partnering with Tinker AFB Security Forces to develop a laser-based a\nC-UAS system and can leverage that relationship to pilot the LEOPARD system for AFB security.\nBeyond the defense sector, the system has broad dual-use potential in critical\ninfrastructure protection. For example, the energy/power sector has about ~12,500 utility-\nscale power plants in the U.S. alone (plus thousands of substations, distribution nodes,\nand control centers). A warning and deterrence system at nuclear power plants, airports,\nseaports, oil refineries, data centers, and other high-value facilities, represents significant\nopportunities for commercialization in parallel with DoD transition.\nThe transition strategy will rely on partnering with established DoD primes and security\nintegrators to accelerate fielding, while pursuing direct engagements with\ninstallation commands for pilot deployments. The team at Immobileyes have pre-\nexisting relationships with several Prime Contractors (Azimuth, UES, UTC and Booz Allen) that can\nfacilitate and successfully transition the technology to DoD customers."},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.851Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.851Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"9e40100e-5a1a-471e-b5da-cd7645ed1a6b","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 15 of 28 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.851Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.851Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"9baa54e9-f682-4aa0-8262-5b8af8781eca","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Proposal Number N254-P01-0421 Immobileyes Inc.\nTopic Number N254-P01\nUse or disclosure of data contained on this page is subject to the restriction on the first page of this volume.\nPage 9 of 10\nPhase II will focus on delivering a prototype and demonstrating operational value at a military base,\nwhich will provide the validation necessary to support Phase III transition funding.\n7. Key Personnel\nAtossa Alavi, CEO (PI – US Citizen) is an entrepreneur, intellectual property (IP) lawyer, and\ntechnology executive with over 15 years of experience at the intersection of law, business strategy, and\nadvanced product development. As Chief Executive Officer of Immobileyes, she has successfully raised\n$2 million in non-dilutive SBIR funding from the U.S. Air Force, assembled and led cross-disciplinary\nengineering teams, and driven the development, fielding, and commercialization of novel non-lethal\noptical disruptor technologies for law enforcement and Air Force Security Forces. She has also served as\nChief Counsel of AlphaMicron, guiding IP strategy and portfolio development, negotiating joint\ndevelopment and licensing agreements, and managing corporate legal functions that supported the\ntransition of adaptive liquid crystal technologies into Army eyewear, automotive, motorsports, and\narchitectural applications. She is a named inventor on three patents for HALAR optical disruption\ntechnology and has built extensive partnerships with DoD, DHS, and commercial stakeholders to advance\nnon-lethal defense and security solutions.\nMs. Alavi has made significant contributions as an educator and community leader. She served as an\nAdjunct Professor at Case Western Reserve University School of Law. Before entering law, she trained\nand practiced in neuroscience and oral surgery in the UK, publishing peer-reviewed research on\nneuroreceptors and serving as Assistant Professor at the Eastman Dental Institute, University of London.\nShe holds a Juris Doctor (2003) from Case Western Reserve University, an MPhil in Neuroscience (2000)\nfrom University College London, and a BDS and MSc in Endodontics from the University of London.\nBahman Taheri, CTO (US Citizen, Secret Clearance) is the CEO of AlphaMicron and CTO of\nImmobileyes. With over 25 years of experience in optics and materials science, he has led cross-\ndisciplinary teams that transitioned advanced electro-optic and liquid crystal innovations from research to\nfielded systems. Under his leadership, AlphaMicron developed the world’s first switchable-tint liquid\ncrystal eyewear, meeting U.S. Army protective eyewear standards and is currently the only electronically\nswitchable eyewear on the Approved Protective Eyewear List (APEL). Under his leadership, the team has\ndelivered light-management solutions for defense, automotive, and commercial partners. He has managed\nmultimillion-dollar R&D programs with the Air Force, Navy, Army, DOE, NSF, and Fortune 500\ncompanies.\nDr. Taheri is an inventor on more than 90 patents and author of over 70 peer-reviewed scientific articles,\nas well as a technical book on liquid crystal optics. His innovations include the first demonstration of\ncontinuous-wave lasing in liquid crystals, development of fast-switching electro-optic systems for laser\nand nuclear flash protection, and commercialization of photochromic-dichroic lens technologies. He has\nreceived multiple innovation awards, served as a frequent invited speaker at defense and industry\nconferences, and has extensive experience in transitioning advanced optical technologies to DoD and\ndual-use markets. He earned his PhD and MS in Physics from Oklahoma State University and a BS in\nPhysics from Cal Poly San Luis Obispo.\n8. Foreign Citizens\nName Foreign\nNational\nCountry\nof Origin\nType of Visa or\nWork Permit\nLevel of Involvement\nJasper Leikens\n(employee)\nYes Belgium Optional\nPractical\nTraining (OPT)\n20% level of effort, primarily in\nMonths 2–6 to support development\nof software used to evaluate system\nperformance."},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.851Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.851Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"e179d71d-ae74-49ad-a56e-c3f4413b9e5e","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 16 of 28 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.851Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.851Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"93a0395a-ad4c-471c-8b17-5838bc2ac618","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Proposal Number N254-P01-0421 Immobileyes Inc.\nTopic Number N254-P01\nUse or disclosure of data contained on this page is subject to the restriction on the first page of this volume.\nPage 10 of 10\nAdditional notes: The engineer will have no access to export-controlled information, ITAR data, or DoD\nsensitive operational procedures. All work will be supervised by the PI, and only sanitized datasets and\npublicly available specifications will be used. The engineer’s contribution will support Tasks 2-4 only,\nand all final reports and analyses will be authored and reviewed by U.S. citizen project staff.\n9. Facilities/Equipment\nThe Immobileyes team possesses a 30-year track record of successful R&D and product development for\nthe Air Force, Army and Navy , including the successful commercialization of SBIR-funded technologies\nfor consumer and military\nmarkets. This experience\nextends to the establishment\nof a new ISO compliant\nmanufacturing facility,\nensuring the delivery of\nhigh-quality, Made in USA\nproducts to DoD customers.\nThe company’s 30,000\nsquare-foot ISO 9001-2015\nfacility in Kent, Ohio,\nprovides a comprehensive\nand vertically integrated\nenvironment, staffed and\nequipped to execute all\nprogram deliverables in-\nhouse. The facility\nencompasses seven\nspecialized laboratories,\nincluding: fully equipped optical characterization, electro-optic, and laser labs; a material characterization\nlab; an additive manufacturing lab featuring multiple 3-D printers; and a dedicated MIL-SPEC testing\nsuite, encompassing ballistic, impact, environmental, and optical testing capabilities. Complementing\nthese resources are dedicated quality control (QC), manufacturing and assembly spaces, and a fully\nequipped machine shop capable of fabricating any necessary tooling or custom equipment. This robust\ninfrastructure and skilled personnel ensure the capability to successfully complete the proposed and future\nefforts entirely in-house.\nSince its inception, Immobileyes has strategically expanded its core capabilities, culminating in the\ndevelopment of HALAR, a versatile platform technology with demonstrated applications across diverse\nsectors, including security, law enforcement, and military. All R&D activities are conducted by a\ndedicated team of engineers and scientists at its Ohio facility. The company’s strategic focus on domestic\nmanufacturing positions Immobileyes to become a leading provider of directed energy hailing and\ndisruption systems for perimeter security applications.\n10. Subcontractors/Consultants: None\n11. Prior, Current, or Pending Support of Similar Proposals or Awards\nThis proposal is not substantially the same as any other proposal that has been funded, is currently being\nfunded, or is pending with another Federal Agency or with any DoD Service or Component.\n12. Identification and Assertion of Restrictions on the Government''s Use, Release, or Disclosure of\nTechnical Data or Computer Software: None"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.851Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.851Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"45f9cb61-3ac9-4a7c-9a4b-e48931b43e3d","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 17 of 28 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.851Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.851Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"502e34a4-7de1-4d6e-b3bd-d0553c4c7ea0","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"SBIR Phase I Proposal\nProposal Number N254-P01-0421\nTopic Number N254-P01\nProposal Title LEOPARD: Light-based Electro-Optic Perimeter Active Response Defense\nDate Submitted 09/24/2025 11:01:18 AM\nFirm Information\nFirm Name Immobileyes Inc.\nMail Address 1950 State Route 59, Kent, Ohio, 44240\nWebsite Address www.immobileyes.com\nUEI KL3MJVGD9XZ9\nDuns 117497611\nCage 8KQ82\nTotal Dollar Amount for this Proposal $239,573.67\nBase Year $139,893.37\nOption $99,680.30\nTechnical and Business Assistance(TABA)- Base $0.00\nTABA- Option $0.00\nBase Year Summary\nTotal Direct Labor (TDL) $118,855.88\nTotal Direct Material Costs (TDM) $0.00\nTotal Direct Supplies Costs (TDS) $0.00\nTotal Direct Equipment Costs (TDE) $0.00\nTotal Direct Travel Costs (TDT) $0.00\nTotal Other Direct Costs (TODC) $0.00\nG&A (rate 10%) x Base (TDL+TOH) $11,885.59\nTotal Firm Costs $130,741.47\nSubcontractor Costs\nTotal Subcontractor Costs (TSC) $0.00\nCost Sharing -$0.00\nProfit Rate (7%) $9,151.90\nTotal Estimated Cost $139,893.37\nTABA $0.00\nOption Summary\nTotal Direct Labor (TDL) $84,690.14\nTotal Direct Material Costs (TDM) $0.00"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.851Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:53.851Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"librar