  VALUES ('58e22fa1-0828-41d4-815d-d1ec8474a725'::uuid, tid, 'primitive', 'p10 · Proposal Number N254-P01-0421 Immobileyes Inc.
Topic N', 'Proposal Number N254-P01-0421 Immobileyes Inc.
Topic Number N254-P01
Use or disclosure of data contained on this page is subject to the restriction on the first page of this volume.
Page 4 of 10
II. Real-time beam shaping:
For effective real-time adaptation, Immobileyes is developing an opto-electronic beam shaping element
that uses liquid crystals for rapid transformation of an input laser beam into a variety of beam profiles.
This provides electrically tunable beam shaping that provides instant laser beam adjustment based on
target distance and desired response, giving operators greater flexibility.
III. Laser eye dazzle safety model
The team has experience developing an eye-safe laser-based deterrence for civilian law enforcement
agencies in response to active shooter incidents. This work focused on optimizing safety levels based on
the ANSI and IEC metrics for MPE and Nominal Ocular Hazard Distance or NOHD (the distance used to
quantify the safe distance to operate from a laser system to avoid any risk of eye damage). Further
development will incorporate the additional Maximum Dazzle Exposure (MDE) model put forward by
Williamson and McLin3 to derive the “effective range” – i.e. the distance where the dazzler effect is seen
without posing any danger of eye damage.
MDE is defined as the laser irradiance at the
eye above which an object cannot be visually
detected through the dazzle field. At laser
irradiances higher than the MDE, the dazzle
field prevents the observer from detecting the
object. MDE levels depend on ambient
lighting conditions, wavelength of the laser,
size and contrast of the object being observed
and the observer. This model is based on a
static, single wavelength laser and we believe
using two different wavelength lasers will
increase dazzle and glare effects.
Nevertheless, this model can be used to quantify dazzle levels with some predictability. The MDE Model
(shown here in Table VI) uses human experimental data and extrapolated numbers and can be used to
develop the LEOPARD Model.
IV. Use of flashing color LED light for effective warning / distraction
In addition to its C-UAS laser dazzler, Immobileyes has developed and commercialized an
LED-based optical disrupter for civilian law enforcement. The handheld model is a
compact, law-enforcement-designed LED system that adds a patented “Optical Stun
Mode” to tactical lights by alternating intense red and green beams. A non-pyro diversion
device is under development.
Our team’s research during device development showed that alternating red/green flashing
lights exploit three key effects: (1) human visual physiology, where green appears
brighter and mixed colors disrupt depth perception; (2) photic or flicker-induced
disruption, in which rapid flashing interferes with the brain’s ability to process
continuous visual input; and (3) color-flicker interference, where alternating
colors overload the visual cortex and slow reaction times. Together, these
mechanisms create rapid, non-lethal disorientation that helps security personnel
regain control with less force. Building on these findings, we will evaluate
whether high-intensity red/green LEDs can be integrated into the system as an
initial warning layer prior to activating laser dazzler effects.
3 Craig A. Williamson & Leon Mclin, “Determination of a laser eye dazzle safety framework” (2018)
Journal of Laser Applications 30(3):032010. https://doi.org/10.2351/1.5029384
LED-based optical
disruptors for law
enforcement', '[{"id":"943b09ca-dada-41d1-833a-9dbdc76f32ac","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Proposal Number N254-P01-0421 Immobileyes Inc.\nTopic Number N254-P01\nUse or disclosure of data contained on this page is subject to the restriction on the first page of this volume.\nPage 4 of 10\nII. Real-time beam shaping:\nFor effective real-time adaptation, Immobileyes is developing an opto-electronic beam shaping element\nthat uses liquid crystals for rapid transformation of an input laser beam into a variety of beam profiles.\nThis provides electrically tunable beam shaping that provides instant laser beam adjustment based on\ntarget distance and desired response, giving operators greater flexibility.\nIII. Laser eye dazzle safety model\nThe team has experience developing an eye-safe laser-based deterrence for civilian law enforcement\nagencies in response to active shooter incidents. This work focused on optimizing safety levels based on\nthe ANSI and IEC metrics for MPE and Nominal Ocular Hazard Distance or NOHD (the distance used to\nquantify the safe distance to operate from a laser system to avoid any risk of eye damage). Further\ndevelopment will incorporate the additional Maximum Dazzle Exposure (MDE) model put forward by\nWilliamson and McLin3 to derive the “effective range” – i.e. the distance where the dazzler effect is seen\nwithout posing any danger of eye damage.\nMDE is defined as the laser irradiance at the\neye above which an object cannot be visually\ndetected through the dazzle field. At laser\nirradiances higher than the MDE, the dazzle\nfield prevents the observer from detecting the\nobject. MDE levels depend on ambient\nlighting conditions, wavelength of the laser,\nsize and contrast of the object being observed\nand the observer. This model is based on a\nstatic, single wavelength laser and we believe\nusing two different wavelength lasers will\nincrease dazzle and glare effects.\nNevertheless, this model can be used to quantify dazzle levels with some predictability. The MDE Model\n(shown here in Table VI) uses human experimental data and extrapolated numbers and can be used to\ndevelop the LEOPARD Model.\nIV. Use of flashing color LED light for effective warning / distraction\nIn addition to its C-UAS laser dazzler, Immobileyes has developed and commercialized an\nLED-based optical disrupter for civilian law enforcement. The handheld model is a\ncompact, law-enforcement-designed LED system that adds a patented “Optical Stun\nMode” to tactical lights by alternating intense red and green beams. A non-pyro diversion\ndevice is under development.\nOur team’s research during device development showed that alternating red/green flashing\nlights exploit three key effects: (1) human visual physiology, where green appears\nbrighter and mixed colors disrupt depth perception; (2) photic or flicker-induced\ndisruption, in which rapid flashing interferes with the brain’s ability to process\ncontinuous visual input; and (3) color-flicker interference, where alternating\ncolors overload the visual cortex and slow reaction times. Together, these\nmechanisms create rapid, non-lethal disorientation that helps security personnel\nregain control with less force. Building on these findings, we will evaluate\nwhether high-intensity red/green LEDs can be integrated into the system as an\ninitial warning layer prior to activating laser dazzler effects.\n3 Craig A. Williamson & Leon Mclin, “Determination of a laser eye dazzle safety framework” (2018)\nJournal of Laser Applications 30(3):032010. https://doi.org/10.2351/1.5029384\nLED-based optical\ndisruptors for law\nenforcement"},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"imported"},"library_eligible":true}]'::jsonb, NULL, 522, 3498, '{}'::jsonb, 'approved', 0.5, 'pending', 0.5, 0, 'upload', 'f5d07400-ac40-491e-9a0e-70b7e76d79f6'::uuid, NULL, NULL, '686842de-ae88-44ac-b724-8e550f079e3e'::uuid, 'tenant', '2026-08-18T22:42:54.204Z'::timestamptz, '2026-08-18T22:42:54.204Z'::timestamptz, 'admin', uid, '[{"blockIds":["b10"],"sourceAtomId":"c4b9486f-79dd-4135-9ad8-3541e505e828"}]'::jsonb, NULL)
