HUBZone-owned SBC\n[ ] Veteran-owned SBC\n[ ] Service Disabled Veteran-owned SBC\n[ ] None Listed\n11. Race of the offeror:\n[ ] American Indian or Alaska Native\n[ ] Native Hawaiian or Other Pacific Islander\n[ ] Asian\n[ ] White\n[ ] Black or African American\n[X] Do not wish to Provide\n12. Ethnicity of the offeror: NON-HISPANIC\n13. It is a corporation that has some unpaid Federal tax liability that has been assessed,\nfor which all judicial and administrative remedies have not been exhausted or have\nnot lapsed, and that is not being paid in a timely manner pursuant to an agreement\nwith the authority responsible for collecting the tax liability:\nFALSE\n14. Firm been convicted of a fraud-related crime involving SBIR and/or STTR funds or found\ncivilly liable for a fraud-related violation involving federal funds:\nNO\n15. Firms Principal Investigator (PI) or Corporate Official (CO), or owner been convicted\nof a fraud-related crime involving SBIR and/or STTR funds or found civilly liable\nfor a fraud-related violation involving federal funds:\nNO\n16. Verify that your firm has registered in the SBAS Company Registry at www.sbir.gov by\nproviding the SBC Control ID# and uploading the registration confirmation PDF:\nSBC_001832313\nSupporting Documentation:\nSBC_001832313.pdf\t•\n17. Is 50% or more of your firm owned or managed by a corporate entity? NO\n18. Is your firm affiliated as set forth in 13 CFR ??121.103? YES\nFirm Affiliates:\nName Address Number of\nEmployees\nbahman@alphamicron.com\tAlphaMicron Inc. 34"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.129Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.129Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"8c1cb82a-37d8-4ad2-91c9-10150908c887","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 3 of 40 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.129Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.129Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"5ee469dc-190e-4838-8617-b6bf4167a174","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Kent, OH\nSignature:\nPrinted Name Signature Title Business Name Date\nAtossa Alavi Atossa M Alavi CEO Immobileyes Inc. 10/21/2020\nAudit Information\nSummary:\nHas your Firm ever had a DCAA review?NO\nVOL I - Proposal Summary\nSummary:\nProposed Base Duration (in months): 3\nTechnical Abstract:\nThe proliferation of low-cost highly capable drone systems creates a critical challenge for the Air Force and Department of\nDefense. These autonomous systems, equipped with commercial cameras and infrared sensor systems, pose a significant\nsurveillance and operational security risk to our military. The Department of Defense has over 750 bases worldwide and each\nof them is under threat from sUAS and drones. This was highlighted when, in early 2021, The Pentagon released a new\nstrategy to counter increasingly complex small drone threats. AFWERX specifically recognized the need for solutions to secure\nairfield operations against the threats and hazards of small UAS systems, stating there is a need for “leveraging automation\nto strengthen the airfield perimeter security.” With hundreds of bases and thousands of operational sites active\nworldwide, the Air Force needs a low-cost, yet highly effective, solution for counteracting the threat of intelligence collection by\nUAS and drone systems.\nImmobileyes has invented an innovative, patented, programmable, scalable directed energy (LED and Laser) disruption and\ndazzling system called HALAR. The solution takes a new “shotgun” approach to laser emission, making it possible to target\nand “blind” several UAS sensors at once, making it a force multiplier for security, OPSEC, counter surveillance, counter\nunmanned aerial systems, and a litany of other value-added Air Force applications. The Immobileyes HALAR solutions are\nbased on over two decades of research by known thought leaders in the fields of physics, optics, and electronics and\nrepresents a first transformative improvement to known existing light-based directed energy technologies.\nFor this project, Immobileyes will work with key Air Force stakeholders led by the AFSFC (Letter of Support) and AFIMSC, to"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.129Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.129Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"f2f17fbc-f6aa-4b84-9898-e97e60d1dc65","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 4 of 40 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.129Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.129Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"92673346-319f-41cc-9eb0-cc02d467b078","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"evaluate the feasibility of utilizing the Immobileyes HALAR Hive solution for base wide counter UAS and drone surveillance\ndefeat. When successful feasibility is demonstrated, Immobileyes will work with the Air Force end-users and stakeholders to\ndevelop a Work Plan for a Phase II effort which culminates in a fieldable and commercially available solution for integration\ninto Air Force base security and OPSEC systems.\nAnticipated Benefits/Potential Commercial Applications of the Research or Development:\nThe Immobileyes HALAR Laser Storm solution, designed specifically to overwhelm, deceive, or disrupt the optical and\ninfrared sensor systems on multiple drones, has the potential to transform c-UAS operations in the Air Force. This has\nbeen validated by Immobileyes’ active supporters at the Security Forces Center.\n“This memorandum is to indicate the AFSFC C&I Branch supports Air Force Security Forces in cooperation with\nImmobileyes to develop and field the base-wide counter-UAS and defeat of drone swarm surveillance utilizing directed\nenergy system. Proposal Number: FX235-CSO1-0859.” – Alexander Erb, Innovations Program Manager, Air Force\nSecurity Forces Center.\nThe HALAR Laser Storm solution has the following benefits over existing counter UAS and counter surveillance solutions.\nHALAR can employee multiple wavelength and variable power applications making it effective against any common\ndrone sensor.\n•\nHALAR enables a “Laser Storm” which showers multiple beams in a “shotgun” approach which defeats surveillance by\ndrone swarms.\n•\nHALAR is versatile and can be adapted into various from factors from a portable unit through a full perimeter defense\nsolution for an Air Force base.\n•\nHALAR is scalable and programmable allowing for customization of effect output based on the desired application,\nrange, and end effect.\n•\nHALAR can be integrated into other counter-UAS and security solutions to provide drone threat migration and\noperational security in any sensitive operational areas.\n•\nHALAR is low-cost, low-weight, and low-power; enabling widespread adoption by the Air Force.\t•\nSuccessful completion of the Phase I feasibility project will demonstrate the value of the HALAR Laser Storm in providing\ncounter UAS and counter surveillance across the Air Force with a focus on base wide security. A sponsored Phase II project will\nresult in a manufacture-ready commercially available counter UAS and counter surveillance solution for the Air Force and\nDepartment of Defense.\nAttention:\nDisclaimer: For any purpose other than to evaluate the proposal, this data except proposal cover sheets shall not\nbe disclosed outside the Government and shall not be duplicated, used or disclosed in whole or in part, provided\nthat if a contract is awarded to this proposer as a result of or in connection with the submission of this data, the\nGovernment shall have the right to duplicate, use or disclose the data to the extent provided in the funding\nagreement. This restriction does not limit the Government''s right to use information contained in the data if it is\nobtained from another source without restriction. This restriction does not apply to routine handling of proposals"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.129Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.129Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"4ec1e8a5-ed5a-4da4-9dea-11f8e1d29a9b","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 5 of 40 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.129Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.129Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"47df561e-2d19-4998-92d6-ebf693a721b2","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"for administrative purposes by Government support contractors. The data subject to this restriction is contained\non the pages of the proposal listed on the line below.\nAddition:\nEnter the page numbers separated by a space of the pages in the proposal that are considered proprietary:\nList a maximum of 8 Key Words or phrases, separated by commas, that describe the Project:\nHALAR, Counter UAS, Optical Dazzler, Security Forces, Secure Base, Counter Surveillance, Directed Energy, Drone\nSwarm Defeat\nVOL I - Proposal Certification\nSummary:"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.129Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.129Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"204fe0b9-dc51-44b7-87be-72d2a3d7f638","type":"numbered_list","style":{},"content":{"items":[{"text":"At a minimum, two thirds of the work in Phase I will be carried out by your small business as defined by 13 C.F.R"}]},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.129Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.129Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"672227bc-2141-4082-bfb6-eb1b97109a7e","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"§§ 701-705. The numbers for this certification are derived from the budget template. To update these numbers,\nreview and revise your budget data. If the minimum percentage of work numbers are not met, then a letter of\nexplanation or written approval from the funding officer is required.\nPlease note that some components will not accept any deviation from the Percentage of Work (POW) minimum\nrequirements. Please check your component instructions regarding the POW requirements.\nYES\nFirm POW 100%\nSubcontractor POW 0%\n2. Is primary employment of the principal investigator with your firm as defined by 13 C.F.R §§ 701-705? YES\n3. During the performance of the contract, the research/research and development will be performed in the\nUnited States.\nYES\n4. During the performance of the contract, the research/research and development will be performed at the\nofferors facilities by the offerors employees except as otherwise indicated in the technical\nproposal.\nYES\n5. Do you plan to use Federal facilities, laboratories, or equipment? NO\n6. The offeror understands and shall comply with export control regulations. YES\n7. There will be ITAR/EAR data in this work and/or deliverables. NO\n8. Has a proposal for essentially equivalent work been submitted to other US government agencies or DoD\ncomponents?\nNO\n9. Has a contract been awarded for any of the proposals listed above? NO\n10. Firm will notify the Federal agency immediately if all or a portion of the work authorized and funded\nunder this proposal is subsequently funded by another Federal agency.\nYES\n11. Are you submitting assertions in accordance with DFARS 252.227-7017 Identification and assertions use,\nrelease, or disclosure restriction?\nNO\n12. Are you proposing research that utilizes human/animal subjects or a recombinant DNA as described in DoDI\n3216.01, 32 C.F.R. §§ 219, and National Institutes of Health Guidelines for Research Involving\nNO"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.129Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.129Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"9231c3c0-3ec6-472f-bf4c-37c801d694f0","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 6 of 40 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.129Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.129Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"f19af3a5-9b96-48f9-becd-a7e99c68e134","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Recombinant DNA of the solicitation:\n13. In accordance with Federal Acquisition Regulation 4.2105, at the time of proposal submission, the required\ncertification template, \"Contractor Certification Regarding Provision of Prohibited Video Surveillance and\nTelecommunications Services and Equipment\" will be completed, signed by an authorized company official, and\nincluded in Volume V: Supporting Documents of this proposal.\nNOTE: Failure to complete and submit the required certifications as a part of the proposal submission process may\nbe cause for rejection of the proposal submission without evaluation.\nYES\n14. Are teaming partners or subcontractors proposed? NO\n15. Are you proposing to use foreign nationals as defined in 22 CFR 120.16 for work under the proposed effort? NO\n16. What percentage of the principal investigators total time will be on the project? 25%\n17. Is the principal investigator socially/economically disadvantaged? NO\n18. Does your firm allow for the release of its contact information to Economic Development Organizations? YES\nVOL I - Contact Information\nPrincipal Investigator\nName: Dr. Atossa Alavi\nPhone: (330) 819-5157\nEmail: atossa@immobileyes.com\nAddress: 1950 State Route 59, Kent, OH 44240 - 4376\nCorporate Official\nName: Eric Wagner\nPhone: (614) 477-0303\nEmail: eric@immobileyes.com\nAddress: 1950 State Route 59, Kent, OH 44240 - 4376\nAuthorized Contract Negotiator\nName: Dr. Atossa Alavi\nPhone: (330) 819-5157\nEmail: atossa@immobileyes.com\nAddress: 1950 State Route 59, Kent, OH 44240 - 4376\nForm Generated on 02/22/2023 21:47:39"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.129Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.129Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"a8a25a79-383d-4fc3-a50d-891a38844f0c","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 7 of 40 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.129Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.129Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"c034e9fc-b9f2-425b-86f4-c1b1c1579246","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Introduction and Summary: Counter-UAS and Defeat of Drone Surveillance Using Directed Energy System\nCompany Name: Immobileyes\nProposal Number: FX235-CSO1-0859 Topic: USAF X23.5_CSO\nIdentified and Engaged USAF End-Users: AF Security Forces Center – AF Installation and Mission Support Center\n1\nTopic: X23.5_CSO \tProposal Number: FX235-CSO1-0859\n“Base-Wide Counter-UAS and Defeat of Drone Surveillance Utilizing Innovative Directed Energy System”\nTechnical Abstract: Immobileyes has invented an innovative, patented, programmable, scalable directed energy (LED and\nLaser) disruption and dazzling system called HALAR. The solution revolutionizes the application of light as a non-lethal force\nmultiplier for security, OPSEC, counter surveillance, counter unmanned aerial systems, and a litany of other value-added Air\nForce applications. For this project, Immobileyes will work with key Air Force stakeholders to evaluate the feasibility of\nadapting Immobileyes’ HALAR Hive dazzler solution to neutralize surveillance threats posed by the emergence of drones.\nOverview: In Phase I, Immobileyes will work with our identified key Air Force stakeholders, led by the AFSFC (Letter of\nSupport) and AFIMSC, to evaluate the feasibility of utilizing the Immobileyes HALAR Hive solution for base-wide\ncounter UAS and drone surveillance defeat. When successful feasibility is demonstrated, Immobileyes will work with the\nAir Force end-users and Phase II partners to develop a Work Plan for a Phase II effort which culminates in a fieldable and\ncommercially available solution for integration into Air Force base security and OPSEC systems.\nTechnical Merit: The Immobileyes HALAR solutions are based on over two decades of research by known thought\nleaders in the fields of physics, optics, and electronics. The HALAR solutions represent the first transformative\nimprovements to known existing light and dazzler technologies in the last two decades. The Hive is programable and\nscalable to address various AF security operational parameters.\nTeam: The Immobileyes is comprised of serial entrepreneurs, optics and electronics experts, product developers,\nengineering, legal, and and electronics manufacturing with a long history of successfully developing and transitioning\ninvention to commercial product. \tAdaptation of Immobileyes’ existing solution significantly de-risks the project.\nCommercialization : The founders of Immobileyes have successfully commercialized numerous electro-optical solutions\nfor various markets over the last several decades. With over 90 granted patents and a proven track record of\ncommercialization, the Immobileyes team is confident in the commercial potential of this solution."},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.129Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.129Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"fa17ca54-4e60-4a66-8cba-3294e86dff46","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 8 of 40 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.129Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.129Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"0233875c-943e-4720-9462-39897adfa969","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Technical Merit Summary\n2\nMulti-target effect: shower of beams in a “shotgun”\nformat targets multiple sensors/UAVs simultaneously\nWide-Coverage: used to warn, disable or paint multiple\nUAVs\nMultiplied effect: multiple wavelengths of light ensure\nmultiple sensor types are affected\nVersatile forms: can be implemented as a hand-held, fixed-\nor mobile-mounted d\nLow C-SWaP: reduced source laser power translates to\nreduced size, weight, power and cost\nScalable and programmable: range, coverage & power can\nbe tailored to specific applications.\nTopic: X23.5_CSO \tProposal Number: FX235-CSO1-0859\n“Base-Wide Counter-UAS and Defeat of Drone Surveillance Utilizing Innovative Directed Energy System”\nHALAR Prototype testing\nWhat do UAS Sensors See When\nEngaged by Immobileyes’ HALAR?\nHALAR Prototype Testing Outside\nHALAR when light hits\ncamera’s sensors\nHALAR with green\nand red light"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.129Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.129Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"9a76319c-1652-468c-8177-36b769cca237","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 9 of 40 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.129Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.129Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"1d0b73f7-2cbe-4285-9733-ae3bd6506f58","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Technical Merit Summary\n3\nTopic: X23.5_CSO \tProposal Number: FX235-CSO1-0859\n“Base-Wide Counter-UAS and Defeat of Drone Surveillance Utilizing Innovative Directed Energy System”\nLaser Storm Multi-Beam System"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"4de85126-0ecd-4909-8c6d-1b08a056d558","type":"bulleted_list","style":{},"content":{"items":[{"text":"Combines multiple laser wavelengths"},{"text":"Splits each laser beam into hundreds of beamlets"}]},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"39ce20ee-596f-4600-9023-e669a4c36ab2","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":" Immobileyes’ HALAR solution has been granted two\nbroad patents and has several patents pending.\n HALAR represents a new approach to optical\ndazzling using directed energy.\n Innovative design allows for multi-beam splitting\nand directional control, a first of its kind.\n HALAR-Laser solution can be scaled to various form\nfactors, from hand-held through vehicle mounted\nsolutions for c-UAS applications.\n Programmable logic enables targeted pattern or\nwide area effects; ensuring that UAS or drone\nswarms can be successfully defeated."},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"14929f0d-821a-493f-8209-68931bcf2916","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 10 of 40 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"a19cb96c-d0a7-4979-93fd-148a2ed59890","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Significance of Problem and Opportunity\n4\nTopic: X23.5_CSO \tProposal Number: FX235-CSO1-0859\n“Base-Wide Counter-UAS and Defeat of Drone Surveillance Utilizing Innovative LED-Based Directed Energy System”\n \tThe consumer drone is over $2.7B annually and expected\nto grow to over $21.6B by 2030. \tThe cost to purchase\nhighly capable autonomous systems is dropping.\n \tEach of these commercial drones represents a clear and\npresent threat to the operational security of the Air Force\nand Department of Defense.\n \tWith high-fidelity optical and infrared camera systems\nonboard, these commercial drones can be used by\nadversaries at any level to conduct intelligence gathering.\n \tWhile counter-UAS and counter-drone technologies are\nadvancing, drone swarms have circumvented existing\ncounter-measures time and again.\n \tDefeating the sensing and information collection\ncapabilities of these drones is paramount to defeating them."},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"aaf00731-ac15-49ee-a74f-0d37fb97e6ac","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 11 of 40 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"1fb5568f-69f2-4fec-a7fd-f7341bdc4996","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Significance of Problem and Opportunity\n5\nTopic: X23.5_CSO \tProposal Number: FX235-CSO1-0859\n“Base-Wide Counter-UAS and Defeat of Drone Surveillance Utilizing Innovative LED-Based Directed Energy System”\n \tThe Department of Defense has over 800 active duty bases\nworldwide. \tEach of them is under threat from sUAS and\ndrones which are used to surveil the bases by adversaries.\n \tIn early 2021, The Pentagon released a new strategy Jan. 7\nto counter increasingly complex small drone threats.\n \tAFWERX recognized the need for solutions to secure\nairfield operations against the threats and hazards of small\nUAS systems, stating there is a need for “leveraging\nautomation to strengthen the airfield perimeter\nsecurity”\n \tWith hundreds of bases and thousands of operational\nsites active worldwide, the Air Force needs a low-cost, yet\nhighly effective solution for counteracting the threat of\nintelligence collection by UAS and drone systems."},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"2c6cef40-e919-4b28-8ff6-034bce6c21da","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 12 of 40 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"98554f88-026c-40ba-ba2b-a407e476f68d","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Summary of Commercial Potential\n6\nTopic: X23.5_CSO \tProposal Number: FX235-CSO1-0859\n“Base-Wide Counter-UAS and Defeat of Drone Surveillance Utilizing Innovative LED-Based Directed Energy System”\n The anti-drone market size is currently $1B\nannually and growing exponentially, expected to\nreach $12.6B by 2030.\n Disabling or “killing” the drone is not always the\ndesired outcome. The largest concern is\ndefeating the drone''s ability to impact security\nor operational effectiveness.\n Immobileyes’ HALAR-Laser solves the challenge\nposed by a drone swarm with hundreds of\ncameras by “blinding” the entire swarm thereby\nmitigating the threat.\n Each of the Air Force bases evaluated for HALAR-\nLaser adoption for c-UAS activities would procure\nhundreds of low-cost systems."},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"fbcb3453-2a97-4b67-bcb8-ef16c3fca93d","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 13 of 40 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"9c3792e2-e3bf-4157-81f9-a53987e68d7e","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Summary of Commercial Potential – Air Force Aligned Mission Priorities\n7\nTopic: X23.5_CSO \tProposal Number: FX235-CSO1-0859\n“Base-Wide Counter-UAS and Defeat of Drone Surveillance Utilizing Innovative LED-Based Directed Energy System”\nImmobileyes HALAR-Laser solution aligns well\nagainst the many of the current Department\nof the Air Force Operational Imperatives"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"f61fc981-4b66-4b15-a26a-ce1290fb5d5e","type":"numbered_list","style":{},"content":{"items":[{"text":"Space Order of Battle; HALAR solutions provide defense"}]},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"10fb8b35-3eb8-43b1-b483-07cbb664823c","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"posture solution to defeat Space-Based ISR threats\n4. (NGAD) Family of Systems; HALAR solutions provide tactical\nsolutions to support defeat of aerial autonomous systems\n5. Resilient Forward Basing; HALAR solutions provide low-cost\neffective deterrents for counter-UAS and hostile actors\n7. Readiness to Mobilize, Deploy, and Fight; HALAR provides\nlow-cost deployable solution to defend supply chains"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"7b7137a0-3657-4db7-ab9f-d1716ea31758","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 14 of 40 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"fd242cb9-4b8b-4583-be11-193e83d00aec","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Team’s Ability to Perform Research and Commercialize the Solution\n8\nThe founders of Immobileyes have a long history of\nsuccessful innovative product commercialization.\nThe team has proven its ability to invent\ntransformative technologies, design and develop\nthose inventions, and deliver value-added solutions\nto a wide range of industries.\nThe Immobileyes team has delivered commercial\nproducts to more than 30 distinguished\norganizations ranging from material suppliers to\nmanufacturing, Tier 1, Tier 2, and OEMs.\nImmobileyes founders have also demonstrated the\nteam’s ability to deliver commercial solutions to the\nDepartment of Defense and have previously\ndelivered products to the Air Force, Navy, and Army.\nImmobileyes Founders Have Delivered Solutions\nTopic: X23.5_CSO \tProposal Number: FX235-CSO1-0859\n“Base-Wide Counter-UAS and Defeat of Drone Surveillance Utilizing Innovative LED-Based Directed Energy System”"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"2cddf597-8cfd-436f-862a-11386afb2570","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 15 of 40 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"61366225-ed64-4060-afcf-1b471fb250c3","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Team’s Ability to Perform Research and Commercialize the Solution – The Right Team\n9\nAtossa Alavi, J.D. - Chief Executive Officer - Atossa is a business/IP attorney with over 15 years of\nexperience in strategy, management and implementation of intellectual property, Government compliance,\nand business development. She specializes in making elite technology available to the appropriate parties.\nAs CEO of Immobileyes, she leads our efforts to bring advanced directed energy-based security solutions to\nthe right partnerships and markets globally. Atossa holds a law degree (JD) from Case Western Reserve\nUniversity and a Master of Philosophy degree in Neuroscience from the University of London.\nBahman Taheri, Ph.D. - Chief Technology Officer - Bahman a liquid crystal and laser physicist with over\n30 years experience conducting research for Department of Defense and others to understand the effects of\nlasers on vision. He is the CEO of our sister company, AlphaMicron, providers of the world’s fastest switchable\nliquid crystal eyewear. He is named inventor on over 90 US and International patents. AlphaMicron’s products\nsell around the world and are used by the military and commercial consumers. He holds a PhD in physics from\nOklahoma State University where he specialized in Nonlinear Optics and Laser Eye Protection.\nAntonio Munoz, Ph.D. - Chief Science Officer - An award-winning scientist with over 30 years of\nexperience in the field of optical physics, spectroscopy, and lasers, Antonio uses his knowledge of ANSI optics\nto characterize device performance of active light management solutions in mission critical situations. Prior\nto his current role, Dr. Munoz was Head of the Laboratory of Magneto-Optics and Spectroscopy (UAM-I,\n1989-2010), and co-chair of the program “Sundays with Science” (AMC, 1995-1999). He received his BS,\nMSc, and Ph.D. in Optical Physics.\nTopic: X23.5_CSO \tProposal Number: FX235-CSO1-0859\n“Base-Wide Counter-UAS and Defeat of Drone Surveillance Utilizing Innovative LED-Based Directed Energy System”"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"e7da6e25-599f-46bc-891c-9628b7247551","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 16 of 40 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"b79eb336-50a5-4adb-bc5e-f467ad487bfa","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Team’s Ability to Perform Research and Commercialize the Solution – The Right Advisors\n10\nScot MacTaggart - Security Strategy Advisor - Scot MacTaggart’s career is uniquely suited to his advisory\nrole with Immobileyes, with extensive management, consulting and advisory experience in both electronic\nsecurity systems and in growing young companies. Prior to cofounding the KRNLS consulting firm in 2017, Scot\nspent 15 years in leadership roles with security systems integrators, and as a member of ASIS International,\nserving corporate and government clients in the adoption of upgraded technologies, including electronic access\ncontrol, video surveillance, intrusion detection, command centers and communications.\nVince O’Malley- Product Advisor - A senior Electronics Design Engineer and Product Innovator with 20 years\nof product design and management experience, Vince supports the team as a product advisor. Vince’s areas of\nexpertise include technology-based protective eyewear, consumer electronics, embedded computer systems\ndevelopment, digital video systems, and solar-powered devices. Experienced in conformance testing for\ncommercial, safety, and military products, Vince brings his expertise to advise on current and future product lines.\nTom Beckett - Design & Manufacturing Partner - Tom is CEO of Dorcy International-Life+Gear, a leading\nmanufacturer of portable lighting products based in Columbus, Ohio with international locations in Canada,\nFlorida, Australia and Hong Kong. Currently focused on Solid State (LED) lighting technologies, Dorcy comes with a\n50-year record of innovation in flashlight development and retail and a global Sales and marketing team. In\naddition, Tom is a founding member of Portable Lights Trade Organization (PLATO), an industry trade organization\nthat established the ANSI/PLATO FL 1 2016 Standard.\nTopic: X23.5_CSO \tProposal Number: FX235-CSO1-0859\n“Base-Wide Counter-UAS and Defeat of Drone Surveillance Utilizing Innovative LED-Based Directed Energy System”"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"b1cfdd14-137d-4820-9dee-085505a7f86c","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 17 of 40 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"f4e8f6f9-c342-4816-a5af-47d212d3b8d3","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Defense Need\n11\nThe Immobileyes HALAR-Laser c-UAS Solutions Address Physical and Operational\nSecurity Challenges Across All Air Force Based Worldwide.\nTopic: X23.5_CSO \tProposal Number: FX235-CSO1-0859\n“Base-Wide Counter-UAS and Defeat of Drone Surveillance Utilizing Innovative LED-Based Directed Energy System”"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"90885cc2-d8da-4a7a-bd64-403629764363","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 18 of 40 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"4502e8f0-b594-48cc-bd33-6d2607ffb3cc","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Defense Need\n12\nTopic: X23.5_CSO \tProposal Number: FX235-CSO1-0853\n“Innovative Patented Dual-Use Handheld LED-Based Distraction, Diversion, and Dazzle for USAF Security Forces Globally”\nImmobileyes has identified the Air Force Security\nForces, Counter-Small Unmanned Aircraft Systems\neffort within the Air Force Materiel Command, as the\nmost beneficial end-user of the Laser Storm solution.\nLetter of Support provided by Security Forces Center.\nThe potential impact of Immobileyes technology on\nphysical and operational security has been validated\nby Immobileyes through engagement with key USAF\npersonnel, including:\nTSgt Dustin Spooner – Security Forces Center\nAlex Erb, GS-13 - Security Forces Center\nTSgt. Andrew Caprio – USAF-CSAF Innovation\nCapt. Garret Custons – USAF-AFWERX\nTSgt. LaRonald Poston – Security Forces Personnel\nDavid Sheely, GS-14 – Air Logistics Center\nImmobileyes has identified USAF stakeholders in the\ntable shown here the and validation of the Defense\nNeed has been provided by other engaged."},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"0c24aaba-23ff-4ca5-8070-e030dc39460b","type":"bulleted_list","style":{},"content":{"items":[{"text":"Wright Patterson AFB,"},{"text":"Logistics Directorate"},{"text":"Civil Engineering Directorate"},{"text":"Force Protection Directorate"},{"text":"Nuclear Integration Directorate and"},{"text":"Air, Space and Cyberspace Operations Directorate"}]},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"21677dd0-480b-4ca4-8e8d-743919270de4","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"All of which work closely to detect friendly or enemy\nactions around Air Force installations worldwide and\nemploy mitigation."},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"0a591409-d27b-40ea-8b99-e96a40fa8a1d","type":"bulleted_list","style":{},"content":{"items":[{"text":"Lt. Col. Ryan Natalini, Security Forces Division deputy"}]},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"16c81588-cd35-4d1e-9fe4-0434a98a6cae","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"chief, and dual-hatted as the Security Forces liaison\nofficer for the C-sUAS"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"225b73a9-3287-4000-a78c-fea68e583e01","type":"bulleted_list","style":{},"content":{"items":[{"text":"Maj. Joseph (Ben) Herwatic, C-sUAS branch chief"},{"text":"Katherine Clarke, C-sUAS lead engineer."}]},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"35666e06-ef0d-4af3-99e4-bd099aee51c4","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Identified USAF Stakeholders in C-UAS"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"288cf87e-bc64-4587-85e0-d93cd8036c6f","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 19 of 40 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"c6a92c31-0b9a-43e2-9f8d-c3a5b5819410","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Phase I Objectives\n13\nOBJECTIVE:\nDemonstrate the ability to adapt Immobileyes HALAR-Laser solution to support the growing need for highly-effective\ncounter-UAS and surveillance threat reduction for Security Forces across the Air Force. This project is endorsed and\nwill be support by the Air Force Security Forces Center (letter of support provided)\nKEY METRICS:\nFeasibility: \tDemonstrate potential ability for significant UAS and drone surveillance threat reduction.\nEffectiveness: \tDemonstrate ability to operate in standard operating environments of Security Forces Personnel.\nProgrammable: \tImplement light output control to tailor performance to mitigate drone and UAS camera systems.\nLow-Cost: \tEvaluate a baseline cost that is acceptable for wide acceptance in the DoD and private sectors.\nKEY OUTCOMES:\nAnalysis: \tWill work with USAF Stakeholders to document the feasibility of adapting the commercial solution.\nWork Plan: \tDevelop the Phase II development plan and requisite milestone plan to develop the system.\nTransition Plan: \tCreate a technology transition plan with its identified Air Force customers.\nTopic: X23.5_CSO \tProposal Number: FX235-CSO1-0859\n“Base-Wide Counter-UAS and Defeat of Drone Surveillance Utilizing Innovative LED-Based Directed Energy System”"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"203bb23f-2d5a-4d77-9fb9-2b1990a0a66c","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 20 of 40 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"7cfde27b-aec0-4200-8dd0-a7c8dbf3a90a","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Phase I Objectives – Feasibility Measures of Success\n14\nTimely Engagement\nDemonstrate that HALAR-Laser can be immediately deployed as soon as threats are identified.\nProgramable Deterrence Zone\nDemonstrate capability to provide wide area effects and non-specific targeting of drone and UAS\nswarms across large geographical footprints.\nDrone Camera and Sensor Defeat\nDemonstrate that HALAR-Laser can effectively saturate common optical and infrared camera sensors.\nDirectional Impact\nDemonstrate HALAR-Laser can be used to paint and target threats with amplified effects.\nMulti-effect against Multiple Drones\nDemonstrate HALAR-Laser can simultaneously engage multiple sensors on multiple drones.\nTopic: X23.5_CSO \tProposal Number: FX235-CSO1-0859\n“Base-Wide Counter-UAS and Defeat of Drone Surveillance Utilizing Innovative LED-Based Directed Energy System”"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"2c764e9a-5a30-4ac8-9652-23552739ae3d","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 21 of 40 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"8aab6a21-75f0-47ee-8405-c537ebcb523b","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Work Plan – Statement of Work\n15\n \tProject Objective: Demonstrate feasibility of adapting Immobileyes HALAR-Laser solution to\neffectively support c-UAS and c-drone surveillance activities across the Air Force.\n \tProject Goal: Quantify the positive operational impact achievable through the adaptation of\nthe HALAR-Laser solution to meet the Air Force’s c-UAS requirements and needs.\n \tDesired Outcome: Development and fielding strategy, complete with Work Plan, for a USAF\nvariant of Immobileyes HALAR-Laser solution for integration into base defense architectures.\nTask \tAction \tOutcome\nStakeholder\nengagement\nImmobileyes will engage with the AFSFC (letter of support) to\nidentify other relevant stakeholders impacted by the project.\nEngaged set of stakeholders (led by SFC) who\nprovide input and feedback on feasibility\nExisting process\nreview and analysis\nImmobileyes will work with stakeholders to evaluate the c-UAS\nchallenges which can be feasibly addressed with HALAR-Laser\nThe team will develop a workflow diagram\nand highlight challenge areas\nFeasibility study and\nimpact report\nImmobileyes and the USAF will evaluate the feasibility of adapting\nHALAR-Laser technology to address USAF-wide c-UAS activities\nImpact report which quantifies the positive\ngains anticipated when implemented\nCommercialization\nstrategy and Phase II\nImmobileyes will work with USAF to develop a development and\ntransition plan which contemplates Phase II and Phase III efforts\nDetailed Phase II Work Plan to be included in\nproposal and transition strategy\nTopic: X23.5_CSO \tProposal Number: FX235-CSO1-0859\n“Base-Wide Counter-UAS and Defeat of Drone Surveillance Utilizing Innovative LED-Based Directed Energy System”"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"15563086-5624-4902-9933-2a32e2cf6fb5","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 22 of 40 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"ab7a3a3c-c6d2-4f63-8cf0-949643779d32","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Transition Plan\n16\nEngage Air Force Key Stake Holders:\nImmobileyes will engage with the Key Stakeholders in Air Force Security Forces Center, Air Force\nInstallation and Mission Support Center, and the Air Logistics Centers, to support the translation of the\ntechnology to a fieldable solution. Letter of Support provided by the Air Force Security Forces Center.\nImmobileyes will engage with the following Air Force stakeholders to support development and\nexecution of the transition plan:\nAir Force End-Users: \tSecurity Forces Squadrons, Air Force Special Operators, Air Logistics\nCenters, Installation and Base Management, and others.\nContracting: \tImmobileyes will meet with the contracting team to discuss support for a\nPhase II project and implementation of a Phase III acquisition contract.\nLeadership: \tThe feasibility study will be briefed to leadership personnel within the\nparticipating Air Force organizations to ensure transition support.\nOther Stakeholders: \tImmobileyes with collaborate with other Air Force stakeholders as\nidentified and referred by our existing partners.\nTopic: X23.5_CSO \tProposal Number: FX235-CSO1-0859\n“Base-Wide Counter-UAS and Defeat of Drone Surveillance Utilizing Innovative LED-Based Directed Energy System”"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"cf8f5951-e20d-4edb-ab70-86d5e8ece4e0","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 23 of 40 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"f06bd7bd-ba8b-4445-9a06-7d81598d0fe3","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Transition Plan\n17\n \tUSAF commercially available HALAR-\nLaser Tactical undergoes certification\nand MIL-SPEC Testing.\n \tCertified system is made available for\npurchase and adoption via GSA.\n \tCommercially available HALAR-Laser\ndistributed by OEM primes such as\nRaytheon and Amentum.\n \tPhase I Feasibility analysis\nconducted with USAF SFC, SFSs\nand other stakeholders.\n \tImpact analysis for adapting\nImmobileyes HALAR-Laser to\nsupport USAF c-UAS operations.\n \tDevelopment of a comprehensive\ndevelopment and transition plan.\nMONTHS 1-3 \tMONTHS 3-24 \tMONTHS 24+\n \t$1.5mm+ Seed investment planned\nwith OGTF and Converge Ventures.\n \tPhase II Air Force contract to develop\nand operationally test proposed\nHALAR-Laser USAF c-UAS solution.\n \tDemonstrated efficacy and reliability\nleads to a fieldable and commercially\navailable USAF HALAR-Laser.\nTopic: X23.5_CSO \tProposal Number: FX235-CSO1-0859\n“Base-Wide Counter-UAS and Defeat of Drone Surveillance Utilizing Innovative LED-Based Directed Energy System”"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"5ee2d548-db96-4505-a3a5-4bfc29e19407","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 24 of 40 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"f4982ff5-f8d4-495d-b711-e7629c6a1d26","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Related Work – Completed\n18\nAF X21.1A- STTR Phase I - Intelligent Air Laser System for Mission Support w/ Multi UAVs Equipped with Laser Beams\nCSO Proposal #: FX21A TCSO1 0321 Research Partner: Kent State University\nUnder this project, Immobileyes evaluated developing a rapidly deployable UAS-mounted\nnon-lethal Intelligent Air Laser (AIL) System to provide USAF Security Forces with a rapid\n& safe technology capable of dazzling, distracting, and disrupting hostile threats utilizing\nhundreds of multicolored moving laser beams programmed for targeted visual disruption.\nThis project successfully demonstrated the ability of directed energy optical solutions to\nprovide valuable tactical and operational advantageous to the Air Force while ensuring that\nengagement of any potential threats was non-lethal and non-harmful.\nCommercial Partner Pilot – Capabilities Demonstration for HALAR Handheld Tactical Solution\nPilot Partners: Dorcy International; Hilliard City Police; Kent Police Department and SWAT\nUnder this project, Immobileyes worked with our commercial partners to design a hybrid\ntactical flashlight and HALAR Dazzler solution. The handheld solution is designed as a\ndrop-in replacement for tactical flashlights with on-demand use of the fully integrated\nHALAR Dazzler capabilities. The team successfully evaluated the technical capabilities of\nthe “dual-mode” solution and determined the operational and tactical advantageous of the\nHALAR Tactical warrant further investigation and investment.\nTopic: X23.5_CSO \tProposal Number: FX235-CSO1-0859\n“Base-Wide Counter-UAS and Defeat of Drone Surveillance Utilizing Innovative LED-Based Directed Energy System”"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"d87daf7a-8eb7-403f-ba16-a393ca4e3495","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 25 of 40 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"135bf879-f55e-4404-87f1-c6f6359dcd44","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Registrations and Certifications\n19\nImmobileyes is 100%\nUS-owned and operated."},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"6bf3f8df-30da-43c9-85a0-2b2fd5072838","type":"bulleted_list","style":{},"content":{"items":[{"text":"SAM Unique Entity"}]},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"3a9d66f2-473d-4b37-82da-60935f533e64","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Identifier:\nKL3MJVGD9XZ9\n(DUNS Number: 117497611)"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"bf0e8b9b-444b-411a-a917-c1bd2c31a1eb","type":"bulleted_list","style":{},"content":{"items":[{"text":"CAGE Code: 8KQ82"},{"text":"Small Business"}]},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"32d04781-3c29-41ab-a1a0-ca506f07749f","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Association ID\nMy SBA SBC ID # is:\nSBC_001832313"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"3f8ba205-3159-4bd8-9234-0b87f90bb4b7","type":"bulleted_list","style":{},"content":{"items":[{"text":"DoD SBIR/STTR ID"}]},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"1414637c-448b-4a8b-baa4-5fcc30d7a755","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"My SBA SBC ID # is:\nSBC_001832313\nImmobileyes will only utilize US Citizens in any aspect of this project.\nTopic: X23.5_CSO \tProposal Number: FX235-CSO1-0859\n“Base-Wide Counter-UAS and Defeat of Drone Surveillance Utilizing Innovative LED-Based Directed Energy System”"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"e2eea0dc-ea83-497b-8e2b-87f6b1b72544","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 26 of 40 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"a747b2ae-5cd6-4317-9b79-edd4d59f74ea","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Facilities and Equipment\n20\nImmobileyes is headquartered within a 30,000 sq ft\ninnovation, research, development and manufacturing\nfacility. The organization operates in compliance with ISO\n9001-2015 and meets all Federal, State and Local\ngovernment laws and regulations, including Laser Safety,\nITAR, Cybersecurity (NIST SP 800-171), and OSHA\nregulations.\nImmobileyes has access to key facility resources, including:\n \tElectro-optical design and development equipment\n \tMechanical design and fabrication work cells\n \t3D printing and additive manufacturing equipment\n \tPhotonics testing and measurement laboratory\n \tQuality control and product testing equipment\n \tCommercial design, modeling, and analysis software\n \tRapid prototyping and light-run manufacturing\nTopic: X23.5_CSO \tProposal Number: FX235-CSO1-0859\n“Base-Wide Counter-UAS and Defeat of Drone Surveillance Utilizing Innovative LED-Based Directed Energy System”"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"e611824e-b3f9-41cd-a1a2-3157ee0b1256","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 27 of 40 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"20bc41df-61b6-4992-a407-38d66d26cbdf","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Facilities and Equipment\n21\nTopic: X23.5_CSO \tProposal Number: FX235-CSO1-0859\n“Base-Wide Counter-UAS and Defeat of Drone Surveillance Utilizing Innovative LED-Based Directed Energy System”\nImmobileyes has full access to the equipment, software, and engineering resources of\nConverge Technologies. The company owns and operates a 28,000sq.ft. mixed-use\nelectronics innovation and manufacturing center at 4621 Lyman Drive in Hilliard, Ohio.\nThe facility has all the required systems to support design, development, prototyping,\nlight manufacturing, test and evaluation of high technology electro-mechanical systems.\nCapabilities accessible include:\nDigital Design: The facility includes high end computing capabilities running the latest\nversions of design software. Solidworks, Catia, Keyshot and other design packages\nare available for physical modeling and analysis. \tAltium, Eagle and other software\npackages are available for electronics and PCB modeling. Matlab and Labview are\navailable for high-end modeling and analysis.\nPCB and Electronics Manufacture and Assembly: The facility includes all the tools\nrequired to manufacture and populate larger quantities of customized printed circuit\nboards (PCB). Equipment includes a PCB stencil work cell; automated component pick\nand place system; automatic reflow oven; environmental testing chambers, spectrum\nanalyzers, oscilloscopes, network analyzers, and other related electronic development\nand testing devices.\nPhysical and Mechanical Fabrication: The facility includes all the tools required for\nprototyping or light manufacturing of physical structures. Typically, this includes\nelectronics housings, casings, mountings, fixtures, integrated mechanical systems and\nother related structures."},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"7010d30d-2bbb-4ba6-91b3-df931c9679be","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 28 of 40 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"37b3e266-0052-4b09-bd48-0f799fa56334","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Commercialization Partners\n22\nTopic: X23.5_CSO \tProposal Number: FX235-CSO1-0853\n“Innovative Patented Dual-Use Handheld LED-Based Distraction, Diversion, and Dazzle for USAF Security Forces Globally”\nAlphaMicron is an award-winning, global leader in liquid crystal-based light reactive\ntechnologies. For over 22 years ago, AlphaMicron has successfully pursued research and\ndevelopment for the Department of Defense. AlphaMicron is an ardent supporter of Immobileyes\nand provides research, design, and engineering support. \tLetter of support provided.\nThe Ohio Gateway Tech Fund (OGTF) is a $10mm pre-seed investment fund targeting Ohio start-\nups. OGTF has performed a deep due diligence on Immobileyes and recently provided a letter of\nintent for a $250,000 pre-seed investment into Immobileyes. The fund is very optimistic about the\npotential of Immobileyes. \tLetter of support provided.\nCompanies with an expressed interest in testing, manufacturing or supporting HALAR C-UAS technology\nOhio-based Drone manufacturer\n– testing partner to Immobileyes\nMakers of “robot dog” deployed\nby USAF for perimeter security."},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"b1bc714b-68c2-4e22-9413-5ab8622b0f1a","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 29 of 40 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"10518fbd-9869-4c72-b66d-c32908b0db02","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Commercialization Partners\n23\nConverge Ventures is a Venture Capital and Start Up support partner for Immobileyes. They\nhave committed to supporting the company’s fund raising, as needed, to launch the\ncommercial products in 2023. Converge Ventures is a strong supporter of Immobileyes and\ntheir engagement with the USAF. \tLetter of support provided.\nThe Entrepreneurs Center (EC) is an Ohio state sponsored technology incubator and support\norganization. Immobileyes is in the EC’s portfolio of supported companies and receives active\nsupport for early-stage business growth challenges. Letter of support provided.\nConverge Technologies is an engineering, product design, and electronics manufacturing\ncompany which supports technology startups in Ohio. Converge is a committed partner to the\ncommercialization of the HALAR solutions and provides access to facilities, equipment, and\nengineering resources to support Immobileyes. Letter of support provided.\nThe Ohio State University is a leader in optometry and physics research and has been a\nsupporter of the development of the HALAR solutions. The university is known for their\ninnovative research with industry and currently ranks #6 in the nation. Through its\npartnership, Immobileyes has access to the research and testing labs of the university.\nTopic: X23.5_CSO \tProposal Number: FX235-CSO1-0859\n“Base-Wide Counter-UAS and Defeat of Drone Surveillance Utilizing Innovative LED-Based Directed Energy System”"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"cabe1c39-2509-43b8-bb97-182bec56e842","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 30 of 40 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"fd90033f-0806-41e9-bda3-1112b006e091","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Intellectual Property\n24\nImmobileyes’ Chief Technology Officer, Bahman Taheri, Ph.D., has over 90 patents where he is a named inventor.\nImmobileyes is the assignees of two broad patents which form the foundation of the HALAR solutions.\nTopic: X23.5_CSO \tProposal Number: FX235-CSO1-0859\n“Base-Wide Counter-UAS and Defeat of Drone Surveillance Utilizing Innovative LED-Based Directed Energy System”"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"2339bd1d-0991-4640-b3e5-2730eae50e2b","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 31 of 40 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"023e5f81-9486-46c8-82cb-72602c587d1d","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Summary of Project and Contact Information\n25\nFor this Phase I project, Immobileyes will work\nwith identified Air Force Stakeholders to evaluate\nthe feasibility of adapting the company’s HALAR-\nLaser solutions to provide high-value c-UAS and c-\ndrone capabilities in support of United States Air\nForce Security Forces (letter of support provided).\nUpon successful feasibility demonstration, the\ncompany will work with USAF to develop the\nfieldable solution under a Phase II and deploy to\nSecurity Force Squadrons across the globe.\nFeasibility of HALAR-Laser Evaluated With These Key Metrics\nImmobileyes Contact Information\nAtossa Alavi\nChief Executive Officer\natossa@immobileyes.com\n330-819-5157\nwww.Immobileyes.com\nTopic: X23.5_CSO \tProposal Number: FX235-CSO1-0859\n“Base-Wide Counter-UAS and Defeat of Drone Surveillance Utilizing Innovative LED-Based Directed Energy System”\nEffective: \tobfuscates the optical and/or infrared sensor\nsystems on common small drone and UAS threats.\nMultiplied effect: multiple wavelengths of light ensure\neffectiveness against common EO/IR camera systems.\nArea effect: solution can be scaled to provide area of effect\noperations against swarm UAS systems. Shotgun approach.\nHard to mitigate: Multi-color patterns cannot be defeated\nwith optical filters on drone cameras.\nScalable and programmable: range, coverage& power can\nbe tailored to support specific applications or operational\nparameters and thresholds.\nSWAP-C: \treduced power lasers allow for significant\nreduction in size, weight, power, and cost."},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:56.130Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"3bdef92f-5ead-4126-99a9-5bbcd58f30a4","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 32 of 40 --"},"h