VOL I - Proposal Summary
Summary:
Proposed Base Duration (in months): 21
Technical Abstract:
The proliferation of low-cost highly capable drone systems creates a critical challenge for the Air Force and Department of
Defense. These autonomous systems, equipped with commercial cameras and infrared sensor systems, pose a significant
surveillance and operational security risk to our military. The Department of Defense has over 750 bases worldwide and each
of them is under threat from sUAS and drones. This was highlighted when, in early 2021, The Pentagon released a new strategy
to counter increasingly complex small drone threats. AFWERX went further and recognized the need for solutions to secure
airfield operations against the threats and hazards of small UAS systems, stating there is a need for “leveraging automation to
strengthen the airfield perimeter security.” With hundreds of bases and thousands of operational sites active worldwide, the Air
Force needs a low-cost, yet highly effective, solution for counteracting the threat of intelligence collection by UAS and drone
systems.
Immobileyes has developed HALAR Laser Storm, a new approach to deny, degrade and deceive multiple sensors using
directed energy. It is a patented, scalable, and programmable laser-emitting device capable of projecting a “shower” of
multiple wavelength laser beams adapted to overwhelm multiple sensors. It offers a novel approach in the use of multiple
laser lights as a force multiplier for counter surveillance, counter unmanned aerial systems, and other value-added Air Force
applications. In this project, Immobileyes will team with University of Central Florida and Lighthouse Avionics to test the -- 4 of 41 --

feasibility of adapting its machine learning identification, classification and targeting technology to “Track and Blind” multiple
UAVs in a low C-SWaP system.
Notably, the proposed effort will incorporate the adaptations and improvements recommended by a panel of USAF experts
engaged during Immobileyes’ Phase I Feasibility Study. Air Force Stakeholders from SFSs, AFSFC, AFRL, AFSOC, AFWERX, and
other interested parties, have formed a collaboration to provide direct support, feedback, and guidance for the development
and execution of this project.
Specifically for this project, Immobileyes has partnered with the 72nd Security Forces Squadron at Tinker AFB as the
project’s representative signatories and TPOC. The 72nd SFS have established the only known Defender Spark Cell in
the Air Force and are building a Security Forces directed technology test, evaluation, and insertion capability. They have
committed to be the representative Customer and End-User for the project and provide a TPOC for the project. They are
closely aligned with the AFWERX , AFSFC, and the AFRL Stakeholders requesting the DAF adaptation of the Immobileyes’ cUAS
solution. Together, this group of Stakeholders represent the champions who will shepherd this project from an R&D Phase II to
an impactful Air Force acquisition-ready solution upon completion of the project.
Anticipated Benefits/Potential Commercial Applications of the Research or Development:
PROBLEM: Despite being aware of the threat of drones for over a decade, to date, most counter drone solutions are ineffective
against multiple drones or swarms. Current kinetic and non-kinetic solutions rely on taking on drones at a time, with serious
imitations when countering multiple drones or swarms. SOLUTION: Immobileyes is developing a new directed energy system
that can target multiple drones at a time – by using optical elements that can split a single laser beam into a shower of
beams, it employes a “shot-gun” approach, and thus is able to paint several targets and deny/defeat several sensors
simultaneously. This solution has been demonstrated using low power lasers. Counterintuitively, this approach simplifies the
operation of the system, requiring less powerful source lasers and less accurate targeting while maintaining effectiveness
at range, As such, this system is simpler to operate, lower-cost and requires less power to run, making a potable solution
entirely possible.
There are over 120 Air Force Security Forces Squadrons currently active in the Air Force comprised of over 38,000 personnel.
These Defenders represent the largest career field in the Air Force and are responsible for the security and safety of operations
and personnel wherever the Air Force operates globally. They are responsible for the deployment and operation of the Air
Force’s counter-UAS systems and, as such, have provided insight into the massive problem facing the DOD.
Defender Counter-UAS Application: The proliferation of low-cost highly capable drone systems creates a critical challenge
for the Air Force and Department of Defense. These autonomous systems, equipped with commercial cameras and infrared
sensor systems, pose a significant surveillance and operational security risk to our military. The Department of Defense has
over 750 bases worldwide and each of them is under threat from sUAS and drones. This was highlighted when, in early
2021, The Pentagon released a new strategy to counter increasingly complex small drone threats. AFWERX specifically
recognized the need for solutions to secure airfield operations against the threats and hazards of small UAS systems, stating
there is a need for “leveraging automation to strengthen the airfield perimeter security.”
“The proliferation of unmanned aerial systems and the ability to operate them in swarms poses a significant threat to -- 5 of 41 -- the Joint Force. The committee applauds the Department of Defense’s focus on rapid development and fielding of
counter-UAS capabilities but seeks to accelerate fielding of capabilities.” – Senate Armed Services Committee
