advantaged SBC\n[X] Women-owned SBC\n[ ] HUBZone-owned SBC\n[ ] Veteran-owned SBC\n[ ] Service Disabled Veteran-owned SBC\n[ ] None Listed\n11. Race of the offeror:\n[ ] American Indian or Alaska Native\n[ ] Native Hawaiian or Other Pacific Islander\n[ ] Asian\n[ ] White\n[ ] Black or African American\n[X] Do not wish to Provide\n12. Ethnicity of the offeror: NON-HISPANIC\n13. It is a corporation that has some unpaid Federal tax liability that has been assessed,\nfor which all judicial and administrative remedies have not been exhausted or have\nnot lapsed, and that is not being paid in a timely manner pursuant to an agreement\nwith the authority responsible for collecting the tax liability:\nFALSE\n14. Firm been convicted of a fraud-related crime involving SBIR and/or STTR funds or found\ncivilly liable for a fraud-related violation involving federal funds:\nNO\n15. Firms Principal Investigator (PI) or Corporate Official (CO), or owner been convicted\nof a fraud-related crime involving SBIR and/or STTR funds or found civilly liable\nfor a fraud-related violation involving federal funds:\nNO\n16. Verify that your firm has registered in the SBAS Company Registry at www.sbir.gov by\nproviding the SBC Control ID# and uploading the registration confirmation PDF:\nSBC_001832313\nSupporting Documentation:\nSBC_001832313.pdf\t•\n17. Is 50% or more of your firm owned or managed by a corporate entity? NO\n18. Is your firm affiliated as set forth in 13 CFR §121.103? YES\nFirm Affiliates:\nName Address Number of\nEmployees"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.926Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.926Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"3c45a49d-be71-48e2-8551-af06999052a4","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 3 of 41 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.926Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.926Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"233105fc-6fb8-4f2e-a5d7-90ca2c9492a8","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"AlphaMicron Inc. bahman@alphamicron.com\nKent, OH\n34\nSignature:\nPrinted Name Signature Title Business Name Date\nAtossa Alavi Atossa M Alavi CEO Immobileyes Inc. 10/21/2020\nAudit Information\nSummary:\nHas your Firm ever had a DCAA review?NO\nVOL I - Proposal Summary\nSummary:\nProposed Base Duration (in months): 21\nTechnical Abstract:\nThe proliferation of low-cost highly capable drone systems creates a critical challenge for the Air Force and Department of\nDefense. These autonomous systems, equipped with commercial cameras and infrared sensor systems, pose a significant\nsurveillance and operational security risk to our military. The Department of Defense has over 750 bases worldwide and each\nof them is under threat from sUAS and drones. This was highlighted when, in early 2021, The Pentagon released a new strategy\nto counter increasingly complex small drone threats. AFWERX went further and recognized the need for solutions to secure\nairfield operations against the threats and hazards of small UAS systems, stating there is a need for “leveraging automation to\nstrengthen the airfield perimeter security.” With hundreds of bases and thousands of operational sites active worldwide, the Air\nForce needs a low-cost, yet highly effective, solution for counteracting the threat of intelligence collection by UAS and drone\nsystems.\nImmobileyes has developed HALAR Laser Storm, a new approach to deny, degrade and deceive multiple sensors using\ndirected energy. It is a patented, scalable, and programmable laser-emitting device capable of projecting a “shower” of\nmultiple wavelength laser beams adapted to overwhelm multiple sensors. It offers a novel approach in the use of multiple\nlaser lights as a force multiplier for counter surveillance, counter unmanned aerial systems, and other value-added Air Force\napplications. In this project, Immobileyes will team with University of Central Florida and Lighthouse Avionics to test the"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.926Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.926Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"fb2695c1-6189-4490-aa65-da5db333f54e","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 4 of 41 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.926Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.926Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"45eaeb04-ea8a-412c-a756-fa1fe0a2eb2a","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"feasibility of adapting its machine learning identification, classification and targeting technology to “Track and Blind” multiple\nUAVs in a low C-SWaP system.\nNotably, the proposed effort will incorporate the adaptations and improvements recommended by a panel of USAF experts\nengaged during Immobileyes’ Phase I Feasibility Study. Air Force Stakeholders from SFSs, AFSFC, AFRL, AFSOC, AFWERX, and\nother interested parties, have formed a collaboration to provide direct support, feedback, and guidance for the development\nand execution of this project.\nSpecifically for this project, Immobileyes has partnered with the 72nd Security Forces Squadron at Tinker AFB as the\nproject’s representative signatories and TPOC. The 72nd SFS have established the only known Defender Spark Cell in\nthe Air Force and are building a Security Forces directed technology test, evaluation, and insertion capability. They have\ncommitted to be the representative Customer and End-User for the project and provide a TPOC for the project. They are\nclosely aligned with the AFWERX , AFSFC, and the AFRL Stakeholders requesting the DAF adaptation of the Immobileyes’ cUAS\nsolution. Together, this group of Stakeholders represent the champions who will shepherd this project from an R&D Phase II to\nan impactful Air Force acquisition-ready solution upon completion of the project.\nAnticipated Benefits/Potential Commercial Applications of the Research or Development:\nPROBLEM: Despite being aware of the threat of drones for over a decade, to date, most counter drone solutions are ineffective\nagainst multiple drones or swarms. Current kinetic and non-kinetic solutions rely on taking on drones at a time, with serious\nimitations when countering multiple drones or swarms. SOLUTION: Immobileyes is developing a new directed energy system\nthat can target multiple drones at a time – by using optical elements that can split a single laser beam into a shower of\nbeams, it employes a “shot-gun” approach, and thus is able to paint several targets and deny/defeat several sensors\nsimultaneously. This solution has been demonstrated using low power lasers. Counterintuitively, this approach simplifies the\noperation of the system, requiring less powerful source lasers and less accurate targeting while maintaining effectiveness\nat range, As such, this system is simpler to operate, lower-cost and requires less power to run, making a potable solution\nentirely possible.\nThere are over 120 Air Force Security Forces Squadrons currently active in the Air Force comprised of over 38,000 personnel.\nThese Defenders represent the largest career field in the Air Force and are responsible for the security and safety of operations\nand personnel wherever the Air Force operates globally. They are responsible for the deployment and operation of the Air\nForce’s counter-UAS systems and, as such, have provided insight into the massive problem facing the DOD.\nDefender Counter-UAS Application: The proliferation of low-cost highly capable drone systems creates a critical challenge\nfor the Air Force and Department of Defense. These autonomous systems, equipped with commercial cameras and infrared\nsensor systems, pose a significant surveillance and operational security risk to our military. The Department of Defense has\nover 750 bases worldwide and each of them is under threat from sUAS and drones. This was highlighted when, in early\n2021, The Pentagon released a new strategy to counter increasingly complex small drone threats. AFWERX specifically\nrecognized the need for solutions to secure airfield operations against the threats and hazards of small UAS systems, stating\nthere is a need for “leveraging automation to strengthen the airfield perimeter security.”\n“The proliferation of unmanned aerial systems and the ability to operate them in swarms poses a significant threat to"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.926Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.926Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"e848ce58-8502-4c1e-9c7b-93dcceb3c48e","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 5 of 41 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.926Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.926Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"ebba6ec6-b8ea-4a97-ad33-8095b1402ef2","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"the Joint Force. The committee applauds the Department of Defense’s focus on rapid development and fielding of\ncounter-UAS capabilities but seeks to accelerate fielding of capabilities.” – Senate Armed Services Committee\nAttention:\nDisclaimer: For any purpose other than to evaluate the proposal, this data except proposal cover sheets shall not\nbe disclosed outside the Government and shall not be duplicated, used or disclosed in whole or in part, provided\nthat if a contract is awarded to this proposer as a result of or in connection with the submission of this data, the\nGovernment shall have the right to duplicate, use or disclose the data to the extent provided in the funding\nagreement. This restriction does not limit the Government''s right to use information contained in the data if it is\nobtained from another source without restriction. This restriction does not apply to routine handling of proposals\nfor administrative purposes by Government support contractors. The data subject to this restriction is contained\non the pages of the proposal listed on the line below.\nAddition:\nEnter the page numbers separated by a space of the pages in the proposal that are considered proprietary:\nList a maximum of 8 Key Words or phrases, separated by commas, that describe the Project:\nHALAR, Counter UAS, Optical Dazzler, Security Forces, Secure Base, Counter Surveillance, Directed Energy, Swarm\nDefeat\nVOL I - Proposal Certification\nSummary:"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.926Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.926Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"00cd5a37-815f-413c-8647-df9cad8e2ff8","type":"numbered_list","style":{},"content":{"items":[{"text":"Is the institution a Research Institution? YES"},{"text":"At a minimum, 40% of the research and/or analytical work in Phase II will be carried out by your small business"}]},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.926Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.926Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"034523b8-f25e-4f4f-af7e-1a997001b353","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"and at least 30% by the research institute as defined in 13 C.F.R §§ 701-705. The numbers for this certification\nquestion are derived from the budget template. To update these numbers, review and revise your budget data.\nThe minimum percentage of work must be met in order to submit the certifications and the proposal.\nYES\nFirm POW 53.22%\nRI POW 35.64%\nSubcontractor POW 11.14%\n3. Is primary employment of the principal investigator with your firm as defined by 13 C.F.R §§ 701-705. YES\n4. During the performance of the contract, the research/research and development will be performed in the\nUnited States.\nYES\n5. During the performance of the contract, the research/research and development will be performed at the\nofferors facilities by the offerors employees except as otherwise indicated in the technical proposal.\nYES\n6. Do you plan to use Federal facilities, laboratories, or equipment? NO\n7. The offeror understands and shall comply with export control regulations. YES"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.926Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.926Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"07f86ec2-f8e6-4d5c-ac86-71229c2d177a","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 6 of 41 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.926Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.926Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"9458568b-a92c-49a7-b857-45f7175cb52a","type":"heading","style":{},"content":{"text":"8. There will be ITAR/EAR data in this work and/or deliverables. NO","level":1},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.926Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.926Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"40ab3faf-e8f7-4241-86c1-d33945a5bcf9","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"9. Proposal for essentially equivalent work been submitted to other US government agencies or DoD\ncomponents.\nNO\n10. Contract been awarded for any of the proposals listed above? NO\n11. Firm will notify the Federal agency immediately if all or a portion of the work authorized and funded\nunder this award is subsequently funded by another Federal agency.\nYES\n12. Submitting assertions in accordance with DFARS 252.227-7017 Identification and assertions use, release, or\ndisclosure restriction.\nNO\n13. The proposing research that utilizes human/animal subjects or a recombinant DNA as described in DoDI\nPhase II 3216.01, 32 C.F.R. §§ 219, and National Institutes of Health Guidelines for Research Involving\nRecombinant DNA of the solicitation:\nNO\n14. In accordance with Federal Acquisition Regulation 4.2105, at the time of proposal submission, the required\ncertification template, \"Contractor Certification Regarding Provision of Prohibited Video Surveillance and\nTelecommunications Services and Equipment\" will be completed, signed by an authorized company official, and\nincluded in Volume V: Supporting Documents of this proposal.\nNOTE: Failure to complete and submit the required certifications as a part of the proposal submission process\nmay be cause for rejection of the proposal submission without evaluation.\nYES\n15. Are teaming partners or subcontractors proposed? NO\n16. Are you proposing to use foreign nationals as defined in 22 CFR 120.16 for work under the proposed effort? NO\n17. Percentage of the principal investigators total time will be on the project: 33%\n18. Is the principal investigator socially/economically disadvantaged? NO\n19. Does your firm allow for the release of its contact information to Economic Development Organizations? YES\nVOL I - Contact Information\nResearch Institution\nName: Dr. Shintson Wu\nPhone: (407) 823-4763\nEmail: swu@creol.ucf.edu\nAddress: 4304 Scorpius Street, Orlando, FL 32816 - 2700\nPrincipal Investigator\nName: Dr. Atossa Alavi\nPhone: (330) 819-5157\nEmail: atossa@immobileyes.com\nAddress: 1950 State Route 59, Kent, OH 44240 - 4376"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.926Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.926Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"8415bf7d-c87e-413d-9dd4-ebde00259492","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 7 of 41 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.926Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.926Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"f1763575-2e5a-401c-87ef-751ab9492a4b","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Corporate Official\nName: Dr. Atossa Alavi\nPhone: (330) 819-5157\nEmail: atossa@immobileyes.com\nAddress: 1950 State Route 59, Kent, OH 44240 - 4376\nAuthorized Contract Negotiator\nName: Eric Wagner\nPhone: (614) 477-0303\nEmail: eric@immobileyes.com\nAddress: 1950 State Route 59, Kent, OH 44240 - 4376\nForm Generated on 11/22/2023 11:31:22"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.927Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.927Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"10fca609-3369-40f5-af3e-390b60ba5716","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 8 of 41 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.927Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.927Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"4f6009a0-80bb-4976-a779-63a734dfdaea","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Topic Number: AFX23D-TCSO1 Proposal Number: F 2 - 1 7 5 2 8\nProposal Title: “Innovative Directed Energy Counter UAS Solution Integrated Into Proven Detection,\nTracking, and Targeting System.”\nSBC: Immobileyes\nVolume 2: Technical Volume\nContents\nGlossary ......................................................................................................................................................... 2\n2.1 Technical Summary .................................................................................................................................. 1\n2.1.1 Identification and Significance of the Problem or Opportunity ................................................. 2\n2.1.2 Air Force Technology Strategic Capabilities .............................................................................. 2\n2.1.3 Non-Defense Commercial Solution ............................................................................................ 3\n2.1.4 Proposed Adaptation of Non-Defense Commercial Solution ..................................................... 3\n2.1.5 Phase I ‘Feasibility Study’ Results ............................................................................................. 4\n2.1.7 Compliance and Regulatory Activities - See Attachment 12 provided in Vol. 5. ........................................5\n2.2 Phase II Technical Objectives and Key Results ....................................................................................... 5\n2.2.1 Required Stakeholders ................................................................................................................ 6\n2.2.2 Phase II Work Plan .................................................................................................................... 6\n2.2.2.1 Milestone Schedule ....................................................................................................................................9\n2.2.2.2 Deliverables .............................................................................................................................................10\n2.2.2.3 Unit Item Identification ............................................................................................................................10\n2.2.2.4 Reporting .................................................................................................................................................10\n2.2.2.5 Safety Related Deliverables .....................................................................................................................10\n2.3 Commercialization Strategy................................................................................................................... 10\n2.3.1 Commercialization Plan .......................................................................................................... 10\n2.3.2 Company Information .............................................................................................................. 10\n2.3.3 Customer and Competition ...................................................................................................... 11\n2.3.4 Market ...................................................................................................................................... 11\n2.3.5 Intellectual Property ................................................................................................................ 12\n2.3.6 Financing........................................................................................................................................................12\n2.3.6 Assistance and Mentoring ........................................................................................................ 12\n2.4 Military Applications and DoD Customers ............................................................................................ 13\n2.5 Foreign Nationals .................................................................................................................................. 13\n2.6 Key Personnel ........................................................................................................................................ 13\n2.8 Investors and Partners........................................................................................................................... 14\n2.9 Related Work.......................................................................................................................................... 15"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.927Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.927Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"191b8eb6-ab84-4a06-acba-32fca67774bb","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 9 of 41 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.927Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.927Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"d1286a08-8b59-4ce3-9bd5-5a85dabd9afa","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Topic Number: AFX23D-TCSO1 Proposal Number: F 2 - 1 7 5 2 8\nProposal Title: “Innovative Directed Energy Counter UAS Solution Integrated Into Proven Detection,\nTracking, and Targeting System.”\nSBC: Immobileyes\nGlossary\nAF – Air Force AFB – Air Force Base AFGSC – Air Force Global Strike\nCommand\nAFIMSC – Air Force Installation\nand Mission Support Center\nAFMC – Air Force Material Command AI – Artificial Intelligence\nAFRL – Air Force Research\nLaboratory\nAFSC – Air Force Sustainment Center AFSFC – Air Force Security\nForces Center\nAGE – Aerospace Ground\nEquipment\nBOM – Bill of Materials BW – Bomb Wing\nCV – Computer Vision DoD – Department of Defense FA – Focus Area\nFAA– Federal Aviation\nAdministration\nIP – Intellectual Property IS – Integrated Systems\nLED – Light Emitting Diode LOS – Letter of Suport MOU – Memorandum of\nUnderstanding\nR&D – Research and\nDevelopment\nSBIR – Small Business Innovation\nResearch\nSF – Security Forces\nSFS – Security Forces Squadron STTR – Small Business Technology\nTransfer\nUL – Underwriters Laboratory\nUN – Unmet Need USB – Universal Serial Bus VCT – Virtual Control Tower\nUNITED STATES AIR FORCE MODERNIZATION PRIORITIES\n5G | Artificial Intelligence/ Machine Learning | Autonomy | Biotechnology | Control and Communications |\nCybersecurity | Directed Energy | General Warfighting Requirements (GWR) | Microelectronics | Network Command\n| Nuclear | Quantum Sciences\nAir Force Technology Strategic Capabilities: Air Superiority; Intelligence, Surveillance and Reconnaissance;\nRapid Global Strike; Global Strike; Command and Control; Responsibilities to Organize, Train, and Equip the Force;\nAir Force Blue Sky"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.927Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.927Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"ad9b2bdd-b8e0-47e8-8f8b-3fd7498a927b","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 10 of 41 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.927Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.927Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"4bc7c253-fc92-47fe-a397-bd69a7b1ee73","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Page 1 of 15\nTopic Number: AFX23D-TCSO1 Proposal Number: F 2 - 1 7 5 2 8\nProposal Title: “AFSFC Requested Innovative Directed Energy Counter UAS Solution Integrated Into\nProven Detection, Tracking, and Targeting System.”\nSBC: Immobileyes\n2.1 Technical Summary\nAs drone technology advances and swarm tactics become more\nsophisticated, the need for effective countermeasures has become\nincreasingly urgent. Military installations and critical infrastructure are\nfacing a growing threat from both state and non-state actors employing\ndrone swarms for reconnaissance, offensive operations, and even acts of\nterrorism. In addition to the threat to human life, the potential economic\nimpact of successful drone swarm attacks on critical infrastructure is\nstaggering. A 2020 study by Oxford Economics estimated that a single\ndrone attack on a European airport could cause economic losses of\nup to $69 million. While the DoD (and Air Force specifically) have\ninvested significant resources into C-UAS solutions, currently deployed\nsystems fall short of the mark and the ability to deny multiple drones and\nswarms operating fully autonomously and asymmetrically is identified as\none of the top remaining technology challenges for the DoD.\nDuring robust customer engagement between the Immobileyes Team and\nAir Force, the need for a low-cost easy-to-deploy scalable counter-drone\nand drone swarm detect, track, characterize, target, and denial solution\ncontinues to be voiced as a critical mission priority. Specifically, the\nSecurity Forces Squadrons tasked with operating the fielded C-UAS\nsystems, and therefore the most knowledgeable on the shortcomings of\nexisting counter-drone solutions, have been the most engaged and\nsupportive advocates of the proposed project.\nThe objective of this proposal is the successful design, development, and\ntesting of a stand-alone (but scalable) low-cost counter-drone solution that is\neffective against multiple drones simultaneously. Immobileyes aims to\nscale-up its innovative, patented, optical and directed energy HALAR-L\nsolutions, to make a fully integrated multi-target / swarm detect and\ndefeat. HALAR-L uses a shot-gun approach, sending out a “shower” of\nbeams for wide-area sensor denial and will be integrated with detect, track,\ncharacterize, and targeting capability of the Virtual Control Tower (VCT)\ndeveloped by Lighthouse Avionics. VCT is a low-cost scalable airspace\nmanagement solution specifically developed for commercial drones and\nunder evaluation by the Federal Aviation Administration (FAA) to support\nadvanced air mobility (AAM) operations. The integration of these two unique solutions will result in a\nscalable, adaptable, low-cost, and highly effective drone swarm denial solution for the protection of our\nWarfighters and critical infrastructure across the country and abroad. Notably, the proposed effort will\nincorporate the adaptations and improvements recommended by a panel of USAF experts engaged during\nImmobileyes’ Phase I Feasibility Study. In a sign of the potential impact and value to the Air Force, and a\nresult of the team’s direct engagement during Phase I, a collaboration of Air Force Stakeholders, from various\nSecurity Forces Squadrons, the Air Force Security Forces Center (AFSFC), AFRL, AFSOC, AFWERX Prime\nand other Air Force interested parties, has formed to provide direct support, feedback, and guidance for the\ndevelopment and execution of this project.\nSpecifically for this project, Immobileyes has partnered with the 72nd Security Forces Squadron at Tinker\nAFB as the project’s representative signatories and TPOC. The 72nd SFS have established the only known\nDefender Spark Cell in the Air Force and are building a Security Forces directed technology test, evaluation,\nand insertion capability. They have committed to be the representative Customer and End-User for the project\nand provide a TPOC for the project. They are closely aligned with the AFWERX Prime, AFSFC, and the\nAFRL Stakeholders requesting the DAF adaptation of the Immobileyes’ cUAS solution. Together, this group\nof Stakeholders represent the champions who will shepherd this project from an R&D Phase II to an impactful\nAir Force acquisition-ready solution upon completion of the project."},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.927Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.927Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"e4031830-f53c-40bc-a98f-e917323789b8","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 11 of 41 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.927Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.927Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"26fc094f-d931-40ee-97e7-bcf429018ffa","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Page 2 of 15\nTopic Number: AFX23D-TCSO1 Proposal Number: F 2 - 1 7 5 2 8\nProposal Title: “AFSFC Requested Innovative Directed Energy Counter UAS Solution Integrated Into\nProven Detection, Tracking, and Targeting System.”\nSBC: Immobileyes\n2.1.1 Identification and Significance of the Problem or Opportunity\nThere are over 120 Air Force Security Forces Squadrons currently active in the Air Force comprised of over\n38,000 personnel. These Defenders represent the largest career field in the Air Force and are responsible for\nthe security and safety of operations and personnel wherever the Air Force operates globally. They are\nresponsible for the deployment and operation of the Air Force’s counter-UAS systems and, as such, have\nprovided insight into the massive problem facing the DOD.\nDefender Counter-UAS Application: The proliferation of low-cost highly capable drone systems creates a\ncritical challenge for the Air Force and Department of Defense. These autonomous systems, equipped with\ncommercial cameras and infrared sensor systems, pose a significant surveillance and operational security risk\nto our military. The Department of Defense has over 750 bases worldwide and each of them is under threat\nfrom sUAS and drones. This was highlighted when, in early 2021, The Pentagon released a new strategy to\ncounter increasingly complex small drone threats. AFWERX specifically recognized the need for solutions to\nsecure airfield operations against the threats and hazards of small UAS systems, stating there is a need for\n“leveraging automation to strengthen the airfield perimeter security.”\n“The proliferation of unmanned aerial systems and the ability to operate them in swarms poses a\nsignificant threat to the Joint Force. The committee applauds the Department of Defense’s focus on\nrapid development and fielding of counter-UAS capabilities but seeks to accelerate fielding of\ncapabilities.” – Senate Armed Services Committee\nPROBLEM: Despite being aware of the threat of drones for over a decade, to date, most counter drone\nsolutions are ineffective against multiple drones or swarms. Current kinetic and non-kinetic solutions rely on\ntaking on drones at a time, with serious imitations when countering multiple drones or swarms. SOLUTION:\nImmobileyes is developing a new directed energy system that can target multiple drones at a time – by using\noptical elements that can split a single laser beam into a shower of beams, it employes a “shot-gun” approach,\nand thus is able to paint several targets and deny/defeat several sensors simultaneously. This solution has been\ndemonstrated using low power lasers. Counterintuitively, this approach simplifies the operation of the system,\nrequiring less powerful source lasers and less accurate targeting while maintaining effectiveness at range,\nAs such, this system is simpler to operate, lower-cost and requires less power to run, making a potable\nsolution entirely possible.\nThis entirely new approach is currently championed by the Security Forces Center (See LOS) to ensure that the\nDefenders have the most advanced solutions to support their missions. They seek to provide these\nprofessionals with any tactical advantage possible. In an environment where the threat of drones is increasing\nexponentially, the HALAR-L solution provides them with the tool they need to counter those threats.\n2.1.2 Air Force Technology Strategic Capabilities\nImmobileyes’ proposed solution will allow for easy deployment and operation of artificially intelligent\ncomputer vision to support a range of Air Force activities and addresses multiple desired DAF Technology\nCapabilities:\nAir Superiority: Immobileyes is being supported by AFSFC for its potential to provide low-cost and highly\neffective counter-sUAS and drone swarm counter-intelligence capability.\nCommand and Control: The VCT portion of the HALAR-L solution is ideal for unified vision-based\nintelligence generation for monitoring of either sky while looking for threats. It can be an easily leveraged\nlow-cost scalable Command and Control asset.\nResponsibilities to Organize, Train, and Equip the Force: The HALR-L solution will provide the Security\nForces community with the equipment needed to counter the drone intelligence threat.\nAir Force Blue Sky: The HALAR-L solution is a disruptive first-of-its kind solution employing advanced\ndirected energy optics, micro-electronics, edge-based AI software, and other blue-sky innovations.\nSpace Force Technology Strategic Capabilities: While the project is directed toward the Air Force, the\nbenefits highlighted throughout the proposal are applicable to Space Force operations as well."},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.927Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.927Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"7da5ec88-eb23-4510-b634-1731bcab2d98","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 12 of 41 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.927Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.927Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"3d60c291-5f0a-4662-9c58-4a85597fc5b9","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Page 3 of 15\nTopic Number: AFX23D-TCSO1 Proposal Number: F 2 - 1 7 5 2 8\nProposal Title: “AFSFC Requested Innovative Directed Energy Counter UAS Solution Integrated Into\nProven Detection, Tracking, and Targeting System.”\nSBC: Immobileyes\n2.1.3 Non-Defense Commercial Solution\nThis project integrates the non-defense commercial solutions from two innovative technology startups to\nproduce the HALAR-L counter-UAS offering. Separately the commercial solutions\noffered by Immobileyes (LASER STORM) and Lighthouse Avionics (Virtual\nControl Tower) provide significant value-add to their commercial marketplaces.\nIntegrated together the non-defense commercial solutions can be adapted to create\nan Air Force directed and desired counter-UAS capability which addresses the\nchallenges voiced by many USAF Stakeholders.\nThe proposed drone swarm intelligence denial solution is based on Immobileyes’\ninnovative, patented, programmable, scalable directed energy (LED and Laser)\ndisruption and dazzling system called HALAR. The solution takes a new\n“shotgun” approach to laser emission, making it possible to target and “blind”\nseveral UAS sensors at once, making it a force multiplier for security, OPSEC,\ncounter surveillance, counter unmanned aerial systems, and a litany of other value-added commercial\napplications. The Immobileyes HALAR solutions are developed by researchers in the fields of physics,\noptics, and electronics with over a 20-year track record of collaboration with the USAF and represents a first\ntransformative improvement to known existing light-based directed energy technologies.\nA second improvement in the HALAR capability is the development of novel\nliquid crystal (LC)-based optics (in collaboration with our RI partner UCF\nCollege of Optics and Photonics /CREOL). This innovative approach beings\nseveral advantages: (a) it overcomes size limitations of conventional optics\nallowing larger aperture / higher pow er laser sources that increase the effective\nrange of the system; (b) it provides a platform for electronically switchable\noptics that can adjust in real-time to alter the range, coverage, or pattern of the\nlaser beams; and (c) it makes possible the use of one optical element to shape the beam of multiple laser\nwavelengths. Beyond the current solution, the LC-based optical elements have various commercial\napplications including in telecommunications and space.\nThe detect, track, and characterize capabilities of the solution will be delivered\nthrough Lighthouse’s Virtual Control Towers (VCTs). The solution was\ndeveloped to address the FAA’s requirements for active air space management\nand will enable the adoption of commercial autonomous air solutions. It is\nundergoing evaluation and certification by the Federal Aviation Administration\n(FAA) and is positioned to be a critical infrastructure solution required to\nsupport wide scale beyond visual line of sight (BVLOS) operations of\ncommercial drones and air taxis. These commercially available systems\nprovide the perfect solution for persistent airspace monitoring detecting of\nthreats, targeting of threats, and queuing the HALAR solution to deny those\nthreats sensing capability.\n2.1.4 Proposed Adaptation of Non-Defense Commercial Solution\nDuring this Phase II effort, Immobileyes will leverage its existing HALAR\ndesigns to research, design, develop, demonstrate, and evaluate an Air Force adaptation of the HALAR-L\nsolution. Efforts will focus on (I) expanding the ability of the HALAR optical element to split higher power,\nmulti-wavelength beams in a system that can be portable, low-cost and field-adaptable to various conditions,\nand (II) integrating the HALAR-L lasers system with the commercially available VCT for drone detection,\ncharacterization and tracking. During its thorough Air Force customer discovery to analyze the potential\nimpact of the HALAR solution on specific Air Force needs, multiple Air Force customers expressed an interest\nin utilizing the HALAR solutions in support of their operations. With significant feedback received during the\nFeasibility Study and the commitment of engagement from the AFSFC, AFRL, and the 72nd SFS,\nImmobileyes will ensure that the final solution developed will be ready for adoption and use by Air Force\nDefenders across the globe."},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.927Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.927Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"6da75b44-5ebb-4854-a32e-a46adb45f0cd","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 13 of 41 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.927Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.927Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"c89b1b60-73d4-4603-a5fa-f67ce6b4d687","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Page 4 of 15\nTopic Number: AFX23D-TCSO1 Proposal Number: F 2 - 1 7 5 2 8\nProposal Title: “AFSFC Requested Innovative Directed Energy Counter UAS Solution Integrated Into\nProven Detection, Tracking, and Targeting System.”\nSBC: Immobileyes\nAt its core, this project is centered on integrating two known and available\nsolutions into a low-cost, easy-to-deploy, and effective C-UAS detect,\ntrack, characterize, target, and denial solution. The project was\nspecifically requested by Air Force Defenders to fill gaps in existing C-\nUAS and drone swarm systems. By leveraging available commercial\nsolutions developed by Immobileyes and Lighthouse, the project will be\nsignificantly de-risked. As such, the project will focus on the research\nand development required to enable the coupling of the HALAR and VCT\nsolutions to create a prototypical C-UAS solution. The Team will then\nfocus on testing and evaluating the solution’s capabilities to defeat drone\nswarms (via representative electronics and not actual drone flights) at\nvarious distances, in various patterns, and at various movement speeds.\nThis will support the analysis and characterization of both the HALAR\nand VCT operational specifications and capabilities. The final deliverable will provide the Air Force with a\nwell-defined and ready to transition C-UAS and swarm defeat solution.\nBased on multiple customer discovery engagements with both our committed Air Force End-User and\nCustomer, as well as other AF/DOD interested and potential Phase III customers, the primary quantitative\nbenefits desired by the USAF are readily achieved with the HALAR-L solution.\n2.1.5 Phase I ‘Feasibility Study’ Results\nScientific R/R&D effort including research questions, methods, results and relevant lit:\nDuring the Phase I STTR, in collaboration with UTARI (University of Texas at Arlington Research Institute),\nthe following technical objectives were explored and achieved:"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.927Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.927Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"5f6e62b8-ae07-4d78-8757-63fca1ddddc1","type":"bulleted_list","style":{},"content":{"items":[{"text":"Established the feasibility of an AI-enhanced visual camera to detect, identify and track s-UAS."},{"text":"Established ability of HALAR-L to follow s-UAS tracked by camera with multiple beams."},{"text":"Compared combined laser / camera mount in a single device vs. separate camera communicating with"}]},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.928Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.928Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"5ebc42dc-8cca-435b-8d92-3baaaa829bdf","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"laser storm device using geospatial data.\nA lab-bench platform HALAR-L was demoed showing\nability to move and point an array of 100xbeams\ntoward a moving target according to geolocation data\nprovided by a single camera with AI enhanced\nidentification accuracy of 80-90% at 100m range.\nPhase II Activities Based on Phase I Results:\n Camera / Sensor capability: Since the UTARI\nCamera accuracy at longer ranges was limited,\nImmobileyes has teamed with Lighthouse (see key\npersonnel) to use their VCT commercialized systems for drone detection and air space management to provide\naccurate, long-range identify and detect capability.\n Laser and Optical Element Optimization: the key to the Laser Storm technology is the ability to split\nlaser beams to provide a Multi-Beam – Multi-Target capability for swarm defeat.\nFor Phase II, Immobileyes has teamed with UCF College of Optics and Photonics to provide the laser /\noptical element foundation for the fieldable HALAR-L technology. These efforts have more than\naccomplished the requirements for Phase I “Technical Feasibility Study”.\nAir Force Customer Discovery Process: During Phase I, Immobileyes spent several months\nidentifying and engaging with organizations within the Air Force and DOD. Those with identified\nand/or voiced needs associated with tactical and security forces equipment were targeted more\nspecifically. Immobileyes traveled the country attending AFWERX and other Air Force technology\ncommercialization events. The company and its supporters engaged with dozens of Air Force personnel and\nprovided information, demonstrations, and technical specifications to interested organizations when requested."},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.928Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.928Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"b318dd89-c8e9-4030-bc44-b33d4eb93374","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 14 of 41 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.928Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.928Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"81ff7232-62ec-40d8-9849-c49aa1c360fd","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Page 5 of 15\nTopic Number: AFX23D-TCSO1 Proposal Number: F 2 - 1 7 5 2 8\nProposal Title: “AFSFC Requested Innovative Directed Energy Counter UAS Solution Integrated Into\nProven Detection, Tracking, and Targeting System.”\nSBC: Immobileyes\nAir Force Security Forces Center Support: Throughout the initial customer discovery efforts, Immobileyes\nengaged with base-level SFS units and the AFRL. However, the company was often directed to the Air Force\nSecurity Forces Center (AFSFC), located at Joint Base San Antonio-Lackland, Texas. The AFSFC’s mission\nis to develop, deliver, and execute Security Forces capabilities and associated programs for Department of the\nAir Force and Joint mission sets enabling a safe and secure operating environment. Over multiple\nengagements and interactions with the AFSFC’s Concept and Innovations Team (led by LOS provider, Alex\nErb, AFSFC Innovations Program Manager), Immobileyes conducted in-depth “Air Force Feasibility\nStudies” with operational SFS units and researchers at the AFRL. Although not the official “Customer” on\nthe MOU for this project, the AFSFC is the enabler for transition of this this technology from a project to an\nAir Force product and is very much involved in the development of this Phase II project.\nEmpowered Air Force “End-User” and “Customer” and TPOC: In\ncompletion of the Phase I-like feasibility and Air Force customer discovery\nprocess, led to the identification of the 72nd SFS as the ideal MOU sponsor for all\nthree required signatures. As indicated, the AFSFC was heavily involved in that\ndecision and has indicated their support with the LOS provided. For the\nHALAR-L, the typical “Customer” will be the commander of an SFS (in this\ncase; 72nd SFS Commander, Lt. Col. Matthew Stillman). The typical “End-\nUser” will be a field operational Defender within that SFS (in this case; 72nd\nDefender, TSgt. Clay Porter) and the project TPOC would be a senior\ntechnology evaluator and/or trainer within an SFS (72nd Innovation Lead, MSgt. Alejandro Figueroa).\nThese Airmen represent the appropriate triumvirate of representative Air Force professionals outlined by the\nsolicitation. More importantly, they represent the aligned operational functions of Security Forces Squadrons\nglobally who will be most impacted by the adoption of the Halar-Tactical solution. Future customers likely\nfrom any MAJCOM as the 38,000+ Defenders are organized directly under the organizational structure of their\nhost bases.\n2.1.7 Compliance and Regulatory Activities - See Attachment 12 provided in Vol. 5.\n2.2 Phase II Technical Objectives and Key Results\nThe primary goal of this project is to design, develop, deploy, and evaluate the performance of an Air Force\nspecific HALAR-L solution. This technology, singled out by the AFSFC, will be redesigned from its existing\ncommercial embodiment, and integrated into a commercially available airspace management solution to\nprovide the USAF with a novel counter-UAS solution.\nThe goal of this project is to design, develop, deploy, and evaluate the performance of an integrated low-cost,"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.928Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.928Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"b9f2fa54-b3f3-421c-92ea-b81cc67610c6","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 15 of 41 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.928Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.928Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"fc954192-5a02-45c0-9e06-2ef008973b11","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Page 6 of 15\nTopic Number: AFX23D-TCSO1 Proposal Number: F 2 - 1 7 5 2 8\nProposal Title: “AFSFC Requested Innovative Directed Energy Counter UAS Solution Integrated Into\nProven Detection, Tracking, and Targeting System.”\nSBC: Immobileyes\nscalable, highly capable solution to defeat the intelligence collection capabilities of low-altitude s-UAS and\ndrone swarms. The anticipated project time is 21 months, with an additional 3 months for reporting. At the\ncompletion of the project, the Team will work with our Air Force end-users to demonstrate a fully capable and\nready-to-be-deployed drone swarm intelligence collection denial solution.\nProject Objectives and Key Results:\n➢ Effective Multi-Target System: The system will demonstrate the ability to detect, track,\ncharacterize, target, and deny intelligence collection by multiple drones/ swarms and other low-\naltitude threats. This will be demonstrated with surrogate sUAS electronics.\n➢ Characterization of HALAR-LS: The team will successfully characterize the output of the HALAR\nsolution provided. This will include in-depth testing and analysis of the device’s performance under\nvarying operational parameters.\n➢ Scalable: The system should demonstrate the potential to be easily scaled through the deployment of\nadditional stand-alone systems. Phase II will establish the upper limit of the laser/optical element\ncombination.\n➢ Deployable: The system should demonstrate the potential to be easily deployed for operation in\nremote environments given power consumption, cooling & laser source options.\n➢ Dynamic Real-time Adjustable Optical Element: Deliver liquid crystal-based optical elements\ndeveloped by UCF (RI) as the foundation for Next-Gen programmable large-area optical filters\ncapable of real time switching to alter range and area-coverage of the system for AF and Space\nForce target acquisition and Counter-Sensor capabilities.\nTechnical Objective: Immobileyes aims to demonstrate the 3 parameters that define the effectiveness\nof directed energy beams on sensor targets to show the effectiveness of the HALAR-L system against\ndrones:\n(1) Beamlet quality: since using an optical element to split a single laser beam into many\n“Beamlets” will affect the beamlet quality, the system needs to show that each Beamlet has up to 90%\nof the original laser beam quality (profile, characteristics) to be effective at a range of 1Km,\n(2) Exposure time: The integrated system will allow sufficient time on target at the speed of a\nmoving drone (as defined by DAF Customer / End User)\n(3) Area coverage: The coverage area of the Beamlets will be calculated and defined based on\n(1) and (2) above.\n2.2.1 Required Stakeholders\nImmobileyes’ MOU sponsor (Tinker AFB – 72nd SFS) has committed to providing all access and support\nrequired to ensure that the Phase II project is successfully executed. They have also agreed to consult with Air\nForce legal, contracting, SBIR personnel, and other Air Force Stakeholders to formalize cooperation with\nImmobileyes as needed and required. Additionally, they have committed to acting as the Liaison with\nAFSFC, AFGSC, AFIMSC and other interested and involved stakeholders for this initiative.\n2.2.2 Phase II Work Plan\nThe high-level workplan which includes tasking, milestones and deliverables is included in the Memorandum\nof Understanding (MOU) which was executed by the 72nd SFS (in collaboration with other USAF\nStakeholders). Along with this workplan, Immobileyes developed a detailed schedule for the project expressed\nthrough a GANTT chart (which will be delivered to TPOC upon award) which included the critical paths for\nanalysis, development, testing and delivery of the proposed solution.\n2.2.2.1 Scope\nThe Immobileyes Team has worked with our Air Force Customer and End User to scope this project.\nHowever, it has also considered requirements of numerous counter-UAS programs across the military. The\nscope of the current project is the result of information from various relevant RFIs and RFPs for drone defeat"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.928Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.928Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"d92aac55-e4de-4097-b6bb-6f62749c7f22","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 16 of 41 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.928Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.928Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"14883d76-5a11-4b0b-8381-377e4002b606","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Page 7 of 15\nTopic Number: AFX23D-TCSO1 Proposal Number: F 2 - 1 7 5 2 8\nProposal Title: “AFSFC Requested Innovative Directed Energy Counter UAS Solution Integrated Into\nProven Detection, Tracking, and Targeting System.”\nSBC: Immobileyes\nsystems, the information provided by the 72d SFS, Air Force Research Laboratory and the Security Forces\nCenter, the entities most involved in the Air Force C-UAS mission.\n2.2.2.2 Task Outline\nThe major tasks and their associated deliverables are identified below. These tasks were jointly developed by\nImmobileyes, Lighthouse, Air Force stakeholders (led by our DAF Customer/End User) and UCF (RI partner).\nTask 1: Finalize Requirements Documentation; Design Review; Compile Design Documents\nTimeline: Award + 2 months\nSummary: Kick-off - Upon award, the Immobileyes Team will meet with customer virtually to conduct an in-\ndepth requirements analysis, functional use case survey, and operational requirements deep-dive, and to\nreview/revise the SOW with the TPOC. Each application of the solution will be white boarded from start to\nfinish and will focus on desired operational functionality; scalability; integration into the Air Force information\ntechnology systems; form/fit/function; and anticipated development/adoption hurdles.\nDeliverables: Final Version of Statement of Work; Product Requirements Document; Key Stakeholders\nRRAs; Critical Path Document\nTask 2: Optical System Design – Laser / Diffractive Beam-Splitters\nand Secondary Optics requirements\nTimeline: Award + 4 months\nSummary: Optical System- Characterization and Design of Gen-1\noptical system (laser, diffractive optical element, laser wavelength, pulse\nwidth, aperture size, beamlet number, divergence, pattern requirements,\nsecondary optics) to meet single drone and swarm defeat requirements. Identification and testing of diffractive\nmultichannel beam splitter arrays, evaluation of manufacturing process and materials to meet laser power and\nwavelengths needed for sensor defeat at range.\nDeliverables: Digital Design Files; Software Design Plan; Quarterly Status Report #1.\nTask 3: Finalize Designs of Hardware/Software for Integrated Intelligent Counter Drone Solution\nTimeline: Award + 6 months\nSummary: Integrate with Detection and Tracking: The Immobileyes Team will utilize\nthe Product Requirements Document accepted in Task 1 to modify existing HALAR-LS\nand VCT design files to support development of an integrated stand-alone (but\nnetworkable) intelligent drone swarm detect, define, track, interfere, and defeat solution.\nThis will include electronic design, case designs, mounting system designs, and peripheral\ndevices. The completed design files will be locked after acceptance and form the\nfoundation of the Phase II deliverables. A software strawman architecture that includes\nconceptual user interfaces and a data flow diagram will also be developed and delivered.\nDeliverables: Digital Design Files; Software Design Plan; Quarterly Status Report #2.\nTask 4: Hardware / Software Component Development - Alpha Software Production and Testing\nTimeline: Award + 9 months\nSummary: Component Development - The Team will source all required components contemplated in the\ndesign files; fabricate the required printed circuit boards (PCBs); integrate the optical element into the laser\nsystem and embed initial operational software for each solution\napplication specified in the Product Requirements Document. The\nTeam will manufacture alpha prototypes for the physical and\nelectronic peripherals required to demonstrate a baseline HALAR-\nL/VCT integrated and operational capability. The Immobileyes\nTeam will deliver an alpha version of a common user and\nadministrator software package, which will allow for operation and\nmanagement of the system. An initial testing plan will be\ndeveloped, with support from the Air Force TPOC, for future onsite testing. Initial operational testing success\nwill be documented and provided."},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.928Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.928Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"d6a8deef-8cbf-4e5c-859b-51a8a0a02ccc","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 17 of 41 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"3346e5c6-c886-49a8-a7cf-af98aa1d5983","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Page 8 of 15\nTopic Number: AFX23D-TCSO1 Proposal Number: F 2 - 1 7 5 2 8\nProposal Title: “AFSFC Requested Innovative Directed Energy Counter UAS Solution Integrated Into\nProven Detection, Tracking, and Targeting System.”\nSBC: Immobileyes\nDeliverables: Alpha Designs for Hardware and Software; Alpha User and Admin Guides; Test Plan; Quarterly\nStatus Reports #3\nTask 5: Gen 1 System Integration with AI/ML and Embedded Control Software Development\nTimeline: Award + 12 months\nSummary: The Immobileyes Team will incorporate any hardware and software changes required from the\ntesting conducted on the alpha systems previously\ndeveloped. This will include additional training in the\nartificial intelligence software and control software which\nallow the VTC and HALAR-L solutions to operate in a\nsynergistic and seamless manner. The Immobileyes Team\nwill develop the additional control and electronics\nsoftware required to fully integrate the subsystems\ntogether, creating a comprehensive drone swarm detect,\ndefine, track, dazzle, and defeat solution. The Immobileyes Team will complete the alpha control software for\nthe solution allowing for variable and programmable operation.\nDeliverables: Software Architecture and Test Plan; Initial testing report for alpha-level solution. Quarterly\nStatus Report #4\nTask 6: Local Waterfall Hardware/Software Testing and Design Modifications\nTimeline: Award + 15 months\nSummary: The Immobileyes Team will conduct local operational and technical\ncapabilities testing of the threat drone swarm detect, deny, and defeat solution.\nThis task will focus on fully characterizing the operational capabilities of the\nsolution. A comprehensive testing matrix will be developed and executed\nunder various operational conditions. The Team will employ a waterfall test\nand redesign methodology to address capability gaps as they are identified. This\nwill ensure that the hardware and software designs are stable and operate as expected when delivered.\nDeliverables: Final Hardware and Software Designs; Completed Testing Matrix and Operational Performance\nReport; Quarterly Status Report #5\nTask 7: Onsite Delivery and Testing of the HALAR-L Solution\nTimeline: Award + 16 months\nSummary: The Immobileyes Team will travel to the customer site to ensure that\nthe system is fully operational and functioning properly. This will include onsite\ntesting with the Air Force TPOC and other key stakeholders. The team will\nprovide operational training to the Air Force stakeholders and deliver the\ncompleted beta version of the solution. Administrator and user guides will be\nprovided to the Air Force as well.\nDeliverables: 1x Fully Integrated Intelligent Drone Swarm Detect, Track, Dazzle,\nand Defeat Solution; User and System Management Guides\nTask 8: Design and Fabricate Gen2 LC-based Optics for Increased Range/Accuracy Customization\nTimeline: Award + 18 months\nSummary: Collaborate with RI team to design and fabricate Gen-2 Liquid\nCrystal-based two-dimensional laser beam deflectors for large aperture laser-\nbeams to expand range and scalability of overall system. Work on his task will\nbegin at the onset of the award but it is envisioned to take 18 months for\ncompletion and demonstration of LC-based optical elements, demonstration of\ntheir fabrication, test and evaluation of their performance and area, improvement\nand optimization of the fabrication process to increase area coverage, and final delivery for use in the system.\nDeliverables: Demo article large-area optical elements for target designation; Testing and Performance\nReport; Quarterly Status Report #6\nTask 9: Final Report Generation and Transition Plan\nTimeline: Award + 21 months"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"26858965-6f69-4b45-a0f4-630e57c0d5f1","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 18 of 41 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"35f225a1-15ed-4e0d-813d-26fdf4be6af5","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Page 9 of 15\nTopic Number: AFX23D-TCSO1 Proposal Number: F 2 - 1 7 5 2 8\nProposal Title: “AFSFC Requested Innovative Directed Energy Counter UAS Solution Integrated Into\nProven Detection, Tracking, and Targeting System.”\nSBC: Immobileyes\nSummary: The Immobileyes Team will compile the project documentation into a final report which includes a\nfinal version of the Product Requirements Document; Software User’s Guide; Digital Design Files (as PDFs);\nchallenges; and lessons learned. The Immobileyes Team will work with the Air Force to develop a\ntechnology transfer plan which outlines a strategy for acquisition, integration, and adoption of the system into\nthe Air Force ecosystem. This document will provide the roadmap for Phase III contract (or other vehicle)\nexecution and use.\nDeliverables: Final Report; Technology Transfer Plan\n2.2.2.1 Milestone Schedule\nTASK DATE DELIVERABLE ACCEPTANCE PAYMENT\nTask 1: Kick Off: Review of scope and\nrequirements for adaptation, conduct\nImmobileyes Team meets with customer via\nZoom as needed to ensure input document.\nAward +\n2 months\nInitial system overall\noutcome, and component\nhardware and software\nrequirements.\nApproval\nof TPOC.\n$300,000\nTask 2: Optical System: Design of Gen-1\noptical system (laser, diffractive optical\nelement, laser wavelength, pulse width,\naperture size, beamlet number, divergence,\npattern requirements) to meet single drone\nand swarm defeat.\nAward +\n4 months\nSystem hardware design.\nDefinition and acceptance\nof expected system\noperational expectations.\nApproval\nof TPOC.\n$300,000\nTask 3: Integrate with Detection &\ntracking: Design of hardware and software\nfor detection and tracking of the swarm\nincluding the position and elevation.\nAward +\n6 months\nSystem hardware design.\nDefinition and acceptance\nof expected system\noperational expectations.\nApproval\nof TPOC.\n$250,000\nTask 4: Component Development:\nFabrication and characterization of Gen 1\ncomponents. Onboarding of AI/ML and\nsystem control software development and\ntesting for integrated solution.\nAward +\n9 months\nAlpha Designs for\nHardware and Software;\nAlpha User and Admin\nGuides; Test Plan\nApproval\nof TPOC.\n$250,000\nTask 5: Gen 1 System integration:\nIntegrate and operate drone swarm detect,\ndefine, and track system. Alpha prototype\nwith connected components. Initial\noperational testing completed.\nAward +\n12\nmonths\nSoftware Architecture and\nTest Plan; Initial testing\nresult report for\ncompleted alpha-level\nsolution.\nApproval\nof TPOC. $250,000\nTask 6: Iterations waterfall and Integration:\nModification to components, software, and\ntesting for integrated solution.\nAward +\n15\nmonths\nFinal Hardware/Software\nDesigns; Completed\nTesting Matrix,\nOperational Report\nApproval\nof TPOC.\n$150,000\nTask 7: On site testing: System Delivery\nand Onsite Testing of HALAR-LS Drone\nSwarm Detect, Track, and Defeat Solution.\nAward +\n16\nmonths\nPrototype system\ndelivered to AF TPOC.\nImmobileyes will provide\nonsite installation/testing.\nApproval\nof TPOC.\n$150,000\nTask 8: Dynamic Optical System (RI):\nDesign and fabricate Gen-2 LC-based two-\ndimensional laser beam deflectors capable\nof electronic switching\nAward +\n18\nmonths\nDemonstrable large-area\noptical element for target\ndesignation\nApproval\nof TPOC.\n$100,000"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"5dc75bff-0edb-4e27-ab8a-d345bab70e92","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 19 of 41 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"fb97f6af-84f2-4d4d-a4a2-078dbe5a370e","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Page 10 of 15\nTopic Number: AFX23D-TCSO1 Proposal Number: F 2 - 1 7 5 2 8\nProposal Title: “AFSFC Requested Innovative Directed Energy Counter UAS Solution Integrated Into\nProven Detection, Tracking, and Targeting System.”\nSBC: Immobileyes\n2.2.2.2 Deliverables\nAll deliverables for this project are outlined in the Task Outline, Milestone Schedule, and Reports sections of\nthis proposal. Additionally, they are found in the executed MOU.\n2.2.2.3 Unit Item Identification\nImmobileyes does not anticipate any requirements for Unique Item Identification on this project.\n2.2.2.4 Reporting\nIn addition to the reports and digital deliverables highlighted in the Milestone Chart, Immobileyes will, at a\nminimum, deliver the following reports to the TPOC in support of the Phase II effort."},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"364c46fd-d2a6-4169-a029-93da47ce4558","type":"bulleted_list","style":{},"content":{"items":[{"text":"Final Report: Immobileyes will deliver the final report within 30 days of the Phase II technical effort"}]},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"bb1056bb-1631-40fe-95ea-4acc7e35afcd","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"completion. The final report’s first page will be a single-page summary identifying the work’s purpose,\nproviding a brief description of the effort accomplished."},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"8f396124-9254-48e7-a65a-19dae3bc0b32","type":"bulleted_list","style":{},"content":{"items":[{"text":"Status Reports: Immobileyes will deliver Status Reports to the TPOC quarterly, starting at month 3,"}]},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"f2d63921-15f3-41bc-9804-11cd827229b8","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"throughout the period of performance of the contract."},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"93e5269b-1801-4469-adc0-303d77847bfb","type":"bulleted_list","style":{},"content":{"items":[{"text":"Technical Interchange Meetings: Immobileyes will establish a series of project Technical Interchange"}]},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"e2f3873f-f27e-4849-acb3-6a4c6e39ba0d","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Meetings in support of this project. Based on the availability of our Air Force end-user, these meetings\nwill be held bi-weekly (or quarterly at a minimum)."},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"2392e6c4-92cf-4641-af25-a6fe16a8812f","type":"bulleted_list","style":{},"content":{"items":[{"text":"Phase II Summary Report: Immobileyes will submit a < 700-word Phase II Summary Report via the"}]},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"e9c9a097-72cc-4031-b2bf-119b365397d2","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"TPOC at technical effort completion date that will include a technology description and anticipated\napplications/benefits for government/private usage."},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"6eb7dc6a-323d-4218-942b-2c9806dd5052","type":"bulleted_list","style":{},"content":{"items":[{"text":"Additional Reporting: Immobileyes will submit all software documentation, user manuals, engineering"}]},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"7b39ba08-0d96-46a8-b3ef-40bc20512464","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"drawing, operation and maintenance documentation, updated commercialization results and other related\nproject documents.\n2.2.2.5 Safety Related Deliverables\nImmobileyes does not anticipate any requirements for safety related deliverables as the system is not expected\nto pose any hazards to the Warfighters. One of the critical aspects of this project will be the characterization\nand output of the HALAR-L solution when operated with varying power output, focusing, and steering\nparameters achieved during this project. This information will inform the Air Force of any potential EM\nsafety-related concerns which are not expected.\n2.3 Commercialization Strategy\nImmobileyes and its partners have a long history of successfully commercializing innovation and developing\ninnovative and value-added products for customers. The company has a dedicated network of support\norganizations which provide resources, guidance, and connections. Immobileyes have access to committed\nventure and growth capital to allow for rapid growth and expansion upon validation of the solutions by the Air\nForce. In summary, the company has a well thought out and developed commercialization strategy which it is\ncurrently successfully executing.\n2.3.1 Commercialization Plan\nAt a high-level, Immobileyes’ commercialization plan is highlighted below. The company has spent\nconsiderable time and resources developing not only the technology, but also the plan to ensure successful\ncommercialization of the product when it is completed. The company designs and develops solutions against\nISO standards, has established best-practice design and manufacturing processes which will be employed.\n2.3.2 Company Information\nTask 9: Final Report Delivery and Phase III\nTransition Strategy\nAward +\n21\nmonths\nFinal report, hardware /\nsoftware specifications\ndocument, phase III plan\nApproval\nof TPOC.\n$50,000"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"a63fc6fe-7c2e-4075-944a-a6ce839d73f5","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 20 of 41 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"c26c4621-bf0d-4a87-888f-725b37d7d9e7","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Page 11 of 15\nTopic Number: AFX23D-TCSO1 Proposal Number: F 2 - 1 7 5 2 8\nProposal Title: “AFSFC Requested Innovative Directed Energy Counter UAS Solution Integrated Into\nProven Detection, Tracking, and Targeting System.”\nSBC: Immobileyes\nImmobileyes is an innovation and product development company\nlocated in Kent, Ohio. The company was founded, in 2020, to\ncommercialize cutting-edge research associated with optics-based\ninnovations. The invention, and patenting, of the HALAR technology\nplatform provided the strong foundation on which the company is built.\nWith innovative light-control capability, HALAR has applications\nacross multiple industries, including security, law enforcement and\nmilitray and can be embodied in multiple product offerings.\nDuring initial customer discovery and market analysis, the law enforcement and security markets stood above\nall others. The tactical benefits gained though the HALAR solution’s ability to distract, disorient, and delay\nthe actions by potential threats was deemed invaluable by all the law enforcement personnel engaged. The\ngreatest strength of Immobileyes is its team, with:"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"007248d3-9a72-4ca5-935e-ac2b8c958877","type":"bulleted_list","style":{},"content":{"items":[{"text":"25-years of R&D and product development of dynamic,"}]},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"9c92e6a0-a026-43df-a9a2-948ad96bbec1","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"intense light and laser eye protection for the Air Force,"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"9c4369cf-9ce8-4b44-a738-cd3ecdce0894","type":"bulleted_list","style":{},"content":{"items":[{"text":"successful commercialization of SBIR-funded technology"}]},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"c8d62c17-c46e-40d2-9eab-88edd72e3515","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"for consumer and military markets,"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"ff9a66fa-1366-43e0-b002-f9ba9786179f","type":"bulleted_list","style":{},"content":{"items":[{"text":"experience setting up a new manufacturing facility to"}]},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"fb71639e-939f-4a88-aa93-22bedb4fff09","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"meet ISO and MIL-SPEC requirements and providing\nMade in USA products for military customers. In addition, the team has formed several strategic industry\npartnerships to enable continued innovation and expansion into new markets.\n2.3.3 Customer and Competition\nCustomers: In addition to the DoD customers associated with this proposal, namely AFSFC and the 38,000+\nDefenders, Immobileyes is engaging with the commercial sector security integrators and law enforcement\ncustomers. This includes Homeland Security, The Department of Justice, and other Federal agencies.\nCompetitors: In The Immobileyes Team, with the support\nof their Air Force stakeholders, have conducted an\nexhaustive analysis of the C-UAS solutions currently fielded\nor under development in each of the service components.\nAn example table of those solutions are highlighted in the\nimage here. All the competing solutions are focused on\ndisrupting communication links or GPS signals, which are\nrendered useless when drone swarms are operating\nautonomously and navigating visually. Moreover, the\nkinetic or energy defeat solutions have been demonstrated to\nbe easily overmatched when facing more than a single threat\nsUAS. The proposed solution focuses more on the wide-area denial of intelligence gathering capabilities\nby threat drone swarms. This mechanism of action also allows the Air Force to more aggressively engage\nany drone (threat or not) to deny collection of information on critical Air Force operations.\n2.3.4 Market\nThe proposed solution addresses the C-UAS market which size is\ncurrently $1B annually and growing exponentially. It is expected to\nreach $12.6B by 2030. Disabling or “killing” the drone is not always the\ndesired outcome. The largest concern is defeating the drone''s ability to\nimpact security or operational effectiveness. Immobileyes’ HALAR-\nLaser solves the challenge posed by a drone swarm with multiple cameras\nby “blinding” the entire swarm thereby mitigating the threat. Each of\nthe Air Force bases evaluated for HALAR-Laser adoption for c-UAS\nactivities would procure hundreds of low-cost systems."},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"0a2ae04b-cb99-46e1-b958-5e94bf715a56","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 21 of 41 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"a6e44ac8-735e-42c2-841e-c7e2a1841d74","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Page 12 of 15\nTopic Number: AFX23D-TCSO1 Proposal Number: F 2 - 1 7 5 2 8\nProposal Title: “AFSFC Requested Innovative Directed Energy Counter UAS Solution Integrated Into\nProven Detection, Tracking, and Targeting System.”\nSBC: Immobileyes\n2.3.5 Intellectual Property\nImmobileyes owns two broad patents which form the foundation of the\nHALAR solutions and multiple patent applications that cover the\nsecurity and C-UAS visions of the HALAR technology. Lighthouse\nutilizes high-end, commercially available electronics for all components\nassociated with the Virtual Control Tower. The embedded software\ndeveloped by the company and the proprietary video processing and\nonboard artificial intelligence is considered Trade Secret and is owned by\nthe company.\n2.3.6 Financing\nAs outlined in the section below, the Immobileyes team has a set of very supportive venture partners which\ninclude the Entrepreneurs Center, Converge Ventures, the Ohio Gateway Tech Fund, and others who continue\nto support the growth of the company with additional venture funding as needed.\nConverge Ventures is a Venture Capital support partner for Immobileyes and have\ncommitted to supporting the company’s fund raising, as needed. Letter of support\nprovided.\nThe Ohio Gateway Tech Fund is an Ohio based early-stage investment fund committed to\nfunding innovative and transformative companies from the state. They have committed\n$500,000 to Immobileyes for their upcoming capital raise. Letter of support provided.\nAlphaMicron is an Ohio-based technology company and strategic partner and investor in\nImmobileyes technology. It has provided $350,000 of funds and in-kind contributions and\ncontinues to provide support services to bridge any funding gaps. AlphaMicron has pledged\nto mtach future investment efforts Letter of support provided.\n2.3.6 Assistance and Mentoring\nThe Team’s innovative solutions have been funded with millions of dollars funding (internal and external) over\nthe last several years. Immobileyes has successfully spun out of AlphaMicron, a leading Optics and Liquid\nCrystal (LC) R&D and manufacturing facility in Kent, OH and a pioneer of first-in-class flexible LC devices for\nthe US Army and Air Force. The Immobileyes team enjoys full access to the facilities, equipment, and personnel\nof AlphaMicron.\nThe Ohio PTAC, SBDC, and MEP Programs have been engaged by The Team to\nsupport the commercialization of its technologies. Immobileyes and the partner\ncompanies are well supported by these programs.\nThe Entrepreneurs’ Center currently includes both Immobileyes and Lighthouse in its\nportfolio of Ohio supported companies. EC has considerable experience helping startups\nbring their technologies into the military markets (see letter of support).\nDerek VanAllen (Subject Matter Expert) – FORTIS A Marine Corps veteran, Derek\nbrings over 20 years in intelligence and electronic warfare and is a seasoned project\nmanager and SIGINT expert. His career features 13 overseas deployments, contributing\nto his deep understanding of SIGINT, C-UAS, and C-IED operations. He excels in\ntraining and leading teams, briefing high-ranking officials, and developing effective\ndefense strategies. Holding an active Top Secret/SCI clearance, Derek combines tactical\nfield experience with strategic acumen.\nThe Immobileyes Team has extensive experience developing new commercial products and bringing them to\nmarket. This includes expertise in marketing, sales, R&D, engineering support, assembly, fabrication,\nmanufacturing, and other ideas to market expertise."},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"e6673122-b6a6-477b-af2e-ee11941f3a0e","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 22 of 41 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"f9b9d925-ff53-4964-96f2-e6834e2ce944","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Page 13 of 15\nTopic Number: AFX23D-TCSO1 Proposal Number: F 2 - 1 7 5 2 8\nProposal Title: “AFSFC Requested Innovative Directed Energy Counter UAS Solution Integrated Into\nProven Detection, Tracking, and Targeting System.”\nSBC: Immobileyes\n2.4 Military Applications and DoD Customers\nSmall, unmanned aircraft systems (sUAS), commonly called drones, have proliferated rapidly and are\navailable to nation states and to non-state actors and individuals. These systems could provide U.S. adversaries\nwith a low-cost means of conducting intelligence, surveillance, and reconnaissance missions against—or\nattacking—U.S. forces. Furthermore, many smaller UASs cannot be detected by traditional air defense systems\ndue to their size, construction material, and flight altitude. As a result, in FY2023, the Department of Defense\n(DOD) plans to spend at least $668 million on counter-UAS (C-UAS) research and development and at\nleast $78 million on C-UAS procurement. The Team is working directly with the Air Force organizations\nevaluating emerging C-UAS technologies and were specifically sponsored.\n2.5 Foreign Nationals\nImmobileyes does not employee non-US citizens and will ensure that any partners also do not.\n2.6 Key Personnel\nAtossa Alavi, J.D. (Chief Executive Officer) US Citizen - Atossa is an IP attorney\nand entrepreneur with 15 years of experience in strategy, management, and\nimplementation of intellectual property, contracting, team development and\ncommercialization activities. She specializes in making elite technology available to\nthe appropriate parties. She holds a law degree (JD) from Case Western Reserve\nUniversity and an MPhil. degree in Neuroscience from the University of London.\nBahman Taheri, Ph.D. (Chief Technical Officer) US Citizen Bahman is a liquid\ncrystal and laser physicist with over 30 years’ experience conducting research for\nthe Department of Defense and others to understand the effects of lasers on vision.\nHe is named inventor on over 90 US and International patents. Bahman has\ndesigned and manufactured high-tech electronics which are sold around the world\nand are used by the military and commercial consumers. He holds a PhD in physics\nfrom Oklahoma State; Nonlinear Optics and Laser Eye Protection.\nAntonio Munoz, Ph.D. (Chief Science Officer) – AlphaMicron - US Citizen An\naward-winning scientist with over 30 years of experience in the field of optical\nphysics, spectroscopy, and lasers, Antonio uses his knowledge of ANSI optics to\ncharacterize device performance of active light management solutions in mission\ncritical situations. He received his BS, MSc, and Ph.D. in Physics.\nProfessor. Shin-Tson Wu (RI) - US Citizen - is a Trustee Chair Professor at\nCREOL, The UCF College of Optics and Photonics and recipient of multiple\nnational and international awards in Optics and Excellence in Research. He has co-\nauthored 7 books, 670 journal papers and 320 conference papers, and obtained 96\nissued US patents. His publications have accumulated over 48,000 citations. His lab\nspecializes in diffractive optics and lightweight, cost effective, LC polymer flat\noptics. He received his Ph.D. in Physics from University of Southern California and\nBS in Physics from National Taiwan University.\nTyler Bair (Chief Engineer) – Lighthouse - US Citizen - Tyler leads all\ndevelopment and engineering aspects of the Drone First Responder and Virtual\nControl Tower projects at Lighthouse. Tyler was a senior design engineer at\nConverge Technologies, where he led the design and commercial development\nof noise radars, optical sensing systems, infrared detection systems, autonomous\ndrone systems, and a litany of other technologies. Tyler has his BS in ECE,\nhighest honors, from Ohio State."},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"c277a5d2-aecf-4013-966a-209a116c0dd7","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 23 of 41 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"83a57d5c-4990-4463-b73e-681be1ff880e","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Page 14 of 15\nTopic Number: AFX23D-TCSO1 Proposal Number: F 2 - 1 7 5 2 8\nProposal Title: “AFSFC Requested Innovative Directed Energy Counter UAS Solution Integrated Into\nProven Detection, Tracking, and Targeting System.”\nSBC: Immobileyes\n2.7 Non-Defense Commercial Customers\nThe commercial use of drones has been growing exponentially over the last decade.\nThe worldwide markets have consumed $23B in commercial UAV sales in 2020, a\nnumber expected to double by 2025, as researched by BCC. Uncooperative or hostile\ndrone swarms pose a threat to Air Force base operations, airports, critical\ninfrastructure, and the public at large. Drones are being used for illicit package\ndelivery in prisons, at the border and other locations. Most recently, the Lighthouse\nteam has been working with Amentum, a leading federal contractor leading UAS and\nC-UAS operations for many federal customers. Together with the team, discussions have been held with\nthe Ohio Department of Justice who are anxious to pilot the VCT-HALAR solution at prisons. Prisons in\nAmerica have an increasingly large problem with illegal package delivery with sUASs. A system to help\ndetect, track, and classify sUASs in the area and defeat those deemed to be suspect would prevent these\npackages reaching inmates. There are an estimated over 3,000 detention facilities that could utilize the\nVCT-HALAR. If each site requires 2x HALAR-Ls on average, the total market is ~$600 million.\n2.8 Investors and Partners\nUCF - CREOL (RI): Home to world-class research centers and cutting-edge facilities,\nUCF College of Optics & Photonics (CREOL) is renowned as a global leader in optics\nand photonics. Research at the college is enriched by the high-tech industrial ecosystem\nin central Florida that provides synergistic partnership opportunities. Professor Shin-\nTson Wu (see Key Personnel) is known for expanding the capabilities and accuracy of\nnon-mechanical laser beam steerers and is addressing the inherent limitations of current\ntechnologies, from light detection and ranging (LiDAR) to high precision satellite-to-\nsatellite communications, using liquid crystals.\nDorcy is the committed manufacturing and distribution partner for Immobileyes. For over\n55 years, Dorcy has been a global leader in the commercial flashlight market.\nHeadquartered in Columbus, Ohio, Dorcy has validated the value of HALAR solutions.\nDorcy is a strong supporter of Immobileyes and their engagement with the USAF. Letter\nof support provided.\nThe City of Hilliard is a mid-sized municipality in Ohio which has developed an\ninnovative “City Labs” program which incubates and supports emerging solutions which\ncould have a positive impact on cities across the United States.\nKent Police Department (PD) serves the City of Kent with a population of 32,000 and is\nhome to Kent State University and its campus police and a member of North East Ohio\nMetro SWAT. Both police departments have identified Immobileyes as an impactful\ncompany for first responders and are actively supporting the company by testing and\nevaluating effectiveness in the field and developing departmental operational policy.\nLetters of support provided.\nAlphaMicron is an award-winning, global leader in liquid crystal-based light reactive\ntechnologies. For over 22 years ago, AlphaMicron has successfully pursued research and\ndevelopment for the Department of Defense. AlphaMicron is an ardent supporter of\nImmobileyes and provides research, design, and engineering support. Letter of support\nprovided.\nConverge Technologies is an engineering services and business advisory company\ncreated to support efficient commercialization of new and innovative technologies. The\ncompany has developed other commercial electronics which will be leveraged for this\nproject. Letter of support provided."},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"fd4a04e8-b8be-4370-aa4d-ea677ee3614b","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 24 of 41 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"889538ec-2bcc-4d5f-8446-0a4bd10405be","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Page 15 of 15\nTopic Number: AFX23D-TCSO1 Proposal Number: F 2 - 1 7 5 2 8\nProposal Title: “AFSFC Requested Innovative Directed Energy Counter UAS Solution Integrated Into\nProven Detection, Tracking, and Targeting System.”\nSBC: Immobileyes\nFORTIS is a full-service business development consultant firm that empowers federal\ngovernment contractors, both large and small, with on-demand expertise tailored to the\nunique needs of each client. Our approach is data driven and grounded in a deep\nunderstanding of the government contracting landscape, enabling us to provide\ncompetitive advantages by leveraging our clients'' strengths in the most efficient manner.\nFortis'' innovative program, \"Navigate,\" exemplifies our commitment to providing\nconsistent market visibility and contractor/opportunity fit assessments. Letter of support\nprovided.\n2.9 Related Work\nProject Title: “Enhanced OPSEC for Air Force Mission Functions Utilizing a Networkable Nonlethal\nDirected Energy Solution” STTR Phase I Contract # - FA864923P0967 Start Date - May 5, 2023 End\nDate - Aug 7, 2023 RI Partner- The Ohio State University\nSummary: Immobileyes HALAR solutions, in conjunction with computer vision systems, can provide an\nimmediate non-lethal deterrent and counter-intelligence capability in areas deemed sensitive. The system\ndistracts and disrupts visual perception in the affected area. The same system can also be used to mitigate\nthe threat of active shooters or other engagements which would benefit from disrupting visual acuity. The\nsolutions utilize 100% eye-safe certified LEDs which are programmable to allow for patterns of effect.\nTargeted End Users – Any Security Forces organization tasked with controlling sensitive areas and/or\ninformation. Given that Defenders would respond to any access alert, they represent the optimal end-users.\nProject Title: “Throwable Portable HALAR Visual Disrupter for Air Force Rapid Tactical &\nSecurity Operations” Contract # - FA864923P0971 Start Date -May 5, 2023 End Date - Aug 7, 2023\nRI Partner - Kent State University\nSummary: HALAR is an innovative patented platform technology representing an innovative leap forward\nin optical distraction, disorientation, diversion, and dazzling non-lethal threat mitigation solutions. The\nHALAR solutions represent the first non-laser eye-safe non-lethal system designed for Military Security\nForces and Law Enforcement missions. The HALAR utilizes high-power multi-color LEDs that are\nprogramable allowing them to flash in a particular sequence known to cause physiological effects such as:\ndizziness, disorientation, glare, and dazzle/flash blind effects.\nTargeted End Users – Aviano AFB SFS supported customer discovery and would be the ideal candidate.\nProject Title: Small UAS Detection and Classification for Rapid Multiple C-UAS Operations\nusing a Novel Multi-Wavelength Directed Energy Technology Contract # - FA864923P0972 Start Date"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"3dd7a6c6-e90f-4443-a120-9fd841a4160a","type":"bulleted_list","style":{},"content":{"items":[{"text":"May 5, 2023 End Date - Aug 7, 2023 RI Partner - University of Texas at Arlington"}]},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"9a16ad31-34f4-4d59-a5f0-66d0d179976e","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Summary: HALAR Laser Storm is a patented, scalable, and programmable laser-emitting device capable\nof projecting a “shower” of multiple wavelength laser beams adapted to overwhelm multiple sensors. It\noffers a novel approach in the use of multiple laser lights as a force multiplier for counter surveillance,\ncounter unmanned aerial systems, and other value-added Air Force applications. In this project,\nImmobileyes will to test the feasibility of adapting its technology to “Track and Blind” multiple UAVs in\na low C-SWAP system.\nTargeted End Users – Tinker AFB (72nd SFS) supported customer discovery and is the ideal candidate to\nsupport this as well. It appears they are very active cUAS participants.\nAF X21.1A- STTR Phase I - Intelligent Air Laser System for Mission Support w/ Multi UAVs\nEquipped with Laser Beams CSO Proposal #: FX21A TCSO1 0321 Research Partner: Kent State\nUniversity: Under this project, Immobileyes evaluated developing a rapidly deployable UAS-mounted\nnon-lethal Intelligent Air Laser (AIL) System to provide USAF Security Forces with a rapid & safe\ntechnology capable of dazzling, distracting, and disrupting hostile threats utilizing hundreds of multicolored\nmoving laser beams programmed for targeted visual disruption. This project successfully demonstrated the\nability of directed energy optical solutions to provide valuable tactical and operational advantages to the\nAir Force while ensuring that engagement of any potential threats was non-lethal and non-harmful."},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:42:58.929Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"5ccf19ae-cb9