  VALUES ('151a4ffd-07c8-4161-9d4e-7a75c72b8445'::uuid, tid, 'primitive', 'p10 · Page 1 of 15
Topic Number: AFX23D-TCSO1 Proposal Numbe', 'Page 1 of 15
Topic Number: AFX23D-TCSO1 Proposal Number: F 2 - 1 7 5 2 8
Proposal Title: “AFSFC Requested Innovative Directed Energy Counter UAS Solution Integrated Into
Proven Detection, Tracking, and Targeting System.”
SBC: Immobileyes
2.1 Technical Summary
As drone technology advances and swarm tactics become more
sophisticated, the need for effective countermeasures has become
increasingly urgent. Military installations and critical infrastructure are
facing a growing threat from both state and non-state actors employing
drone swarms for reconnaissance, offensive operations, and even acts of
terrorism. In addition to the threat to human life, the potential economic
impact of successful drone swarm attacks on critical infrastructure is
staggering. A 2020 study by Oxford Economics estimated that a single
drone attack on a European airport could cause economic losses of
up to $69 million. While the DoD (and Air Force specifically) have
invested significant resources into C-UAS solutions, currently deployed
systems fall short of the mark and the ability to deny multiple drones and
swarms operating fully autonomously and asymmetrically is identified as
one of the top remaining technology challenges for the DoD.
During robust customer engagement between the Immobileyes Team and
Air Force, the need for a low-cost easy-to-deploy scalable counter-drone
and drone swarm detect, track, characterize, target, and denial solution
continues to be voiced as a critical mission priority. Specifically, the
Security Forces Squadrons tasked with operating the fielded C-UAS
systems, and therefore the most knowledgeable on the shortcomings of
existing counter-drone solutions, have been the most engaged and
supportive advocates of the proposed project.
The objective of this proposal is the successful design, development, and
testing of a stand-alone (but scalable) low-cost counter-drone solution that is
effective against multiple drones simultaneously. Immobileyes aims to
scale-up its innovative, patented, optical and directed energy HALAR-L
solutions, to make a fully integrated multi-target / swarm detect and
defeat. HALAR-L uses a shot-gun approach, sending out a “shower” of
beams for wide-area sensor denial and will be integrated with detect, track,
characterize, and targeting capability of the Virtual Control Tower (VCT)
developed by Lighthouse Avionics. VCT is a low-cost scalable airspace
management solution specifically developed for commercial drones and
under evaluation by the Federal Aviation Administration (FAA) to support
advanced air mobility (AAM) operations. The integration of these two unique solutions will result in a
scalable, adaptable, low-cost, and highly effective drone swarm denial solution for the protection of our
Warfighters and critical infrastructure across the country and abroad. Notably, the proposed effort will
incorporate the adaptations and improvements recommended by a panel of USAF experts engaged during
Immobileyes’ Phase I Feasibility Study. In a sign of the potential impact and value to the Air Force, and a
result of the team’s direct engagement during Phase I, a collaboration of Air Force Stakeholders, from various
Security Forces Squadrons, the Air Force Security Forces Center (AFSFC), AFRL, AFSOC, AFWERX Prime
and other Air Force interested parties, has formed to provide direct support, feedback, and guidance for the
development and execution of this project.
Specifically for this project, Immobileyes has partnered with the 72nd Security Forces Squadron at Tinker
AFB as the project’s representative signatories and TPOC. The 72nd SFS have established the only known
Defender Spark Cell in the Air Force and are building a Security Forces directed technology test, evaluation,
and insertion capability. They have committed to be the representative Customer and End-User for the project
and provide a TPOC for the project. They are closely aligned with the AFWERX Prime, AFSFC, and the
AFRL Stakeholders requesting the DAF adaptation of the Immobileyes’ cUAS solution. Together, this group
of Stakeholders represent the champions who will shepherd this project from an R&D Phase II to an impactful
Air Force acquisition-ready solution upon completion of the project.', '[{"id":"a25963df-e899-4d6a-bed1-935ef9029671","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Page 1 of 15\nTopic Number: AFX23D-TCSO1 Proposal Number: F 2 - 1 7 5 2 8\nProposal Title: “AFSFC Requested Innovative Directed Energy Counter UAS Solution Integrated Into\nProven Detection, Tracking, and Targeting System.”\nSBC: Immobileyes\n2.1 Technical Summary\nAs drone technology advances and swarm tactics become more\nsophisticated, the need for effective countermeasures has become\nincreasingly urgent. Military installations and critical infrastructure are\nfacing a growing threat from both state and non-state actors employing\ndrone swarms for reconnaissance, offensive operations, and even acts of\nterrorism. In addition to the threat to human life, the potential economic\nimpact of successful drone swarm attacks on critical infrastructure is\nstaggering. A 2020 study by Oxford Economics estimated that a single\ndrone attack on a European airport could cause economic losses of\nup to $69 million. While the DoD (and Air Force specifically) have\ninvested significant resources into C-UAS solutions, currently deployed\nsystems fall short of the mark and the ability to deny multiple drones and\nswarms operating fully autonomously and asymmetrically is identified as\none of the top remaining technology challenges for the DoD.\nDuring robust customer engagement between the Immobileyes Team and\nAir Force, the need for a low-cost easy-to-deploy scalable counter-drone\nand drone swarm detect, track, characterize, target, and denial solution\ncontinues to be voiced as a critical mission priority. Specifically, the\nSecurity Forces Squadrons tasked with operating the fielded C-UAS\nsystems, and therefore the most knowledgeable on the shortcomings of\nexisting counter-drone solutions, have been the most engaged and\nsupportive advocates of the proposed project.\nThe objective of this proposal is the successful design, development, and\ntesting of a stand-alone (but scalable) low-cost counter-drone solution that is\neffective against multiple drones simultaneously. Immobileyes aims to\nscale-up its innovative, patented, optical and directed energy HALAR-L\nsolutions, to make a fully integrated multi-target / swarm detect and\ndefeat. HALAR-L uses a shot-gun approach, sending out a “shower” of\nbeams for wide-area sensor denial and will be integrated with detect, track,\ncharacterize, and targeting capability of the Virtual Control Tower (VCT)\ndeveloped by Lighthouse Avionics. VCT is a low-cost scalable airspace\nmanagement solution specifically developed for commercial drones and\nunder evaluation by the Federal Aviation Administration (FAA) to support\nadvanced air mobility (AAM) operations. The integration of these two unique solutions will result in a\nscalable, adaptable, low-cost, and highly effective drone swarm denial solution for the protection of our\nWarfighters and critical infrastructure across the country and abroad. Notably, the proposed effort will\nincorporate the adaptations and improvements recommended by a panel of USAF experts engaged during\nImmobileyes’ Phase I Feasibility Study. In a sign of the potential impact and value to the Air Force, and a\nresult of the team’s direct engagement during Phase I, a collaboration of Air Force Stakeholders, from various\nSecurity Forces Squadrons, the Air Force Security Forces Center (AFSFC), AFRL, AFSOC, AFWERX Prime\nand other Air Force interested parties, has formed to provide direct support, feedback, and guidance for the\ndevelopment and execution of this project.\nSpecifically for this project, Immobileyes has partnered with the 72nd Security Forces Squadron at Tinker\nAFB as the project’s representative signatories and TPOC. The 72nd SFS have established the only known\nDefender Spark Cell in the Air Force and are building a Security Forces directed technology test, evaluation,\nand insertion capability. They have committed to be the representative Customer and End-User for the project\nand provide a TPOC for the project. They are closely aligned with the AFWERX Prime, AFSFC, and the\nAFRL Stakeholders requesting the DAF adaptation of the Immobileyes’ cUAS solution. Together, this group\nof Stakeholders represent the champions who will shepherd this project from an R&D Phase II to an impactful\nAir Force acquisition-ready solution upon completion of the project."},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"imported"},"library_eligible":true}]'::jsonb, NULL, 624, 4248, '{}'::jsonb, 'approved', 0.5, 'pending', 0.5, 0, 'upload', 'f0ef96b8-1375-4baf-887d-c6f031ffa9b4'::uuid, NULL, NULL, '686842de-ae88-44ac-b724-8e550f079e3e'::uuid, 'tenant', '2026-08-18T22:42:59.308Z'::timestamptz, '2026-08-18T22:42:59.308Z'::timestamptz, 'admin', uid, '[{"blockIds":["b10"],"sourceAtomId":"6d9d25e2-3aa0-4ab5-b01c-518e4c043627"}]'::jsonb, NULL)
