  VALUES ('edb35ff8-8132-41c5-84c8-195d1ae3b907'::uuid, tid, 'primitive', 'p11 · Page 2 of 15
Topic Number: AFX23D-TCSO1 Proposal Numbe', 'Page 2 of 15
Topic Number: AFX23D-TCSO1 Proposal Number: F 2 - 1 7 5 2 8
Proposal Title: “AFSFC Requested Innovative Directed Energy Counter UAS Solution Integrated Into
Proven Detection, Tracking, and Targeting System.”
SBC: Immobileyes
2.1.1 Identification and Significance of the Problem or Opportunity
There are over 120 Air Force Security Forces Squadrons currently active in the Air Force comprised of over
38,000 personnel. These Defenders represent the largest career field in the Air Force and are responsible for
the security and safety of operations and personnel wherever the Air Force operates globally. They are
responsible for the deployment and operation of the Air Force’s counter-UAS systems and, as such, have
provided insight into the massive problem facing the DOD.
Defender Counter-UAS Application: The proliferation of low-cost highly capable drone systems creates a
critical challenge for the Air Force and Department of Defense. These autonomous systems, equipped with
commercial cameras and infrared sensor systems, pose a significant surveillance and operational security risk
to our military. The Department of Defense has over 750 bases worldwide and each of them is under threat
from sUAS and drones. This was highlighted when, in early 2021, The Pentagon released a new strategy to
counter increasingly complex small drone threats. AFWERX specifically recognized the need for solutions to
secure airfield operations against the threats and hazards of small UAS systems, stating there is a need for
“leveraging automation to strengthen the airfield perimeter security.”
“The proliferation of unmanned aerial systems and the ability to operate them in swarms poses a
significant threat to the Joint Force. The committee applauds the Department of Defense’s focus on
rapid development and fielding of counter-UAS capabilities but seeks to accelerate fielding of
capabilities.” – Senate Armed Services Committee
PROBLEM: Despite being aware of the threat of drones for over a decade, to date, most counter drone
solutions are ineffective against multiple drones or swarms. Current kinetic and non-kinetic solutions rely on
taking on drones at a time, with serious imitations when countering multiple drones or swarms. SOLUTION:
Immobileyes is developing a new directed energy system that can target multiple drones at a time – by using
optical elements that can split a single laser beam into a shower of beams, it employes a “shot-gun” approach,
and thus is able to paint several targets and deny/defeat several sensors simultaneously. This solution has been
demonstrated using low power lasers. Counterintuitively, this approach simplifies the operation of the system,
requiring less powerful source lasers and less accurate targeting while maintaining effectiveness at range,
As such, this system is simpler to operate, lower-cost and requires less power to run, making a potable
solution entirely possible.
This entirely new approach is currently championed by the Security Forces Center (See LOS) to ensure that the
Defenders have the most advanced solutions to support their missions. They seek to provide these
professionals with any tactical advantage possible. In an environment where the threat of drones is increasing
exponentially, the HALAR-L solution provides them with the tool they need to counter those threats.
2.1.2 Air Force Technology Strategic Capabilities
Immobileyes’ proposed solution will allow for easy deployment and operation of artificially intelligent
computer vision to support a range of Air Force activities and addresses multiple desired DAF Technology
Capabilities:
Air Superiority: Immobileyes is being supported by AFSFC for its potential to provide low-cost and highly
effective counter-sUAS and drone swarm counter-intelligence capability.
Command and Control: The VCT portion of the HALAR-L solution is ideal for unified vision-based
intelligence generation for monitoring of either sky while looking for threats. It can be an easily leveraged
low-cost scalable Command and Control asset.
Responsibilities to Organize, Train, and Equip the Force: The HALR-L solution will provide the Security
Forces community with the equipment needed to counter the drone intelligence threat.
Air Force Blue Sky: The HALAR-L solution is a disruptive first-of-its kind solution employing advanced
directed energy optics, micro-electronics, edge-based AI software, and other blue-sky innovations.
Space Force Technology Strategic Capabilities: While the project is directed toward the Air Force, the
benefits highlighted throughout the proposal are applicable to Space Force operations as well.', '[{"id":"4639af46-67f6-46ab-a0b7-424bfa0224e1","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Page 2 of 15\nTopic Number: AFX23D-TCSO1 Proposal Number: F 2 - 1 7 5 2 8\nProposal Title: “AFSFC Requested Innovative Directed Energy Counter UAS Solution Integrated Into\nProven Detection, Tracking, and Targeting System.”\nSBC: Immobileyes\n2.1.1 Identification and Significance of the Problem or Opportunity\nThere are over 120 Air Force Security Forces Squadrons currently active in the Air Force comprised of over\n38,000 personnel. These Defenders represent the largest career field in the Air Force and are responsible for\nthe security and safety of operations and personnel wherever the Air Force operates globally. They are\nresponsible for the deployment and operation of the Air Force’s counter-UAS systems and, as such, have\nprovided insight into the massive problem facing the DOD.\nDefender Counter-UAS Application: The proliferation of low-cost highly capable drone systems creates a\ncritical challenge for the Air Force and Department of Defense. These autonomous systems, equipped with\ncommercial cameras and infrared sensor systems, pose a significant surveillance and operational security risk\nto our military. The Department of Defense has over 750 bases worldwide and each of them is under threat\nfrom sUAS and drones. This was highlighted when, in early 2021, The Pentagon released a new strategy to\ncounter increasingly complex small drone threats. AFWERX specifically recognized the need for solutions to\nsecure airfield operations against the threats and hazards of small UAS systems, stating there is a need for\n“leveraging automation to strengthen the airfield perimeter security.”\n“The proliferation of unmanned aerial systems and the ability to operate them in swarms poses a\nsignificant threat to the Joint Force. The committee applauds the Department of Defense’s focus on\nrapid development and fielding of counter-UAS capabilities but seeks to accelerate fielding of\ncapabilities.” – Senate Armed Services Committee\nPROBLEM: Despite being aware of the threat of drones for over a decade, to date, most counter drone\nsolutions are ineffective against multiple drones or swarms. Current kinetic and non-kinetic solutions rely on\ntaking on drones at a time, with serious imitations when countering multiple drones or swarms. SOLUTION:\nImmobileyes is developing a new directed energy system that can target multiple drones at a time – by using\noptical elements that can split a single laser beam into a shower of beams, it employes a “shot-gun” approach,\nand thus is able to paint several targets and deny/defeat several sensors simultaneously. This solution has been\ndemonstrated using low power lasers. Counterintuitively, this approach simplifies the operation of the system,\nrequiring less powerful source lasers and less accurate targeting while maintaining effectiveness at range,\nAs such, this system is simpler to operate, lower-cost and requires less power to run, making a potable\nsolution entirely possible.\nThis entirely new approach is currently championed by the Security Forces Center (See LOS) to ensure that the\nDefenders have the most advanced solutions to support their missions. They seek to provide these\nprofessionals with any tactical advantage possible. In an environment where the threat of drones is increasing\nexponentially, the HALAR-L solution provides them with the tool they need to counter those threats.\n2.1.2 Air Force Technology Strategic Capabilities\nImmobileyes’ proposed solution will allow for easy deployment and operation of artificially intelligent\ncomputer vision to support a range of Air Force activities and addresses multiple desired DAF Technology\nCapabilities:\nAir Superiority: Immobileyes is being supported by AFSFC for its potential to provide low-cost and highly\neffective counter-sUAS and drone swarm counter-intelligence capability.\nCommand and Control: The VCT portion of the HALAR-L solution is ideal for unified vision-based\nintelligence generation for monitoring of either sky while looking for threats. It can be an easily leveraged\nlow-cost scalable Command and Control asset.\nResponsibilities to Organize, Train, and Equip the Force: The HALR-L solution will provide the Security\nForces community with the equipment needed to counter the drone intelligence threat.\nAir Force Blue Sky: The HALAR-L solution is a disruptive first-of-its kind solution employing advanced\ndirected energy optics, micro-electronics, edge-based AI software, and other blue-sky innovations.\nSpace Force Technology Strategic Capabilities: While the project is directed toward the Air Force, the\nbenefits highlighted throughout the proposal are applicable to Space Force operations as well."},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"imported"},"library_eligible":true}]'::jsonb, NULL, 686, 4645, '{}'::jsonb, 'approved', 0.5, 'pending', 0.5, 0, 'upload', 'f0ef96b8-1375-4baf-887d-c6f031ffa9b4'::uuid, NULL, NULL, '686842de-ae88-44ac-b724-8e550f079e3e'::uuid, 'tenant', '2026-08-18T22:42:59.327Z'::timestamptz, '2026-08-18T22:42:59.327Z'::timestamptz, 'admin', uid, '[{"blockIds":["b11"],"sourceAtomId":"6d9d25e2-3aa0-4ab5-b01c-518e4c043627"}]'::jsonb, NULL)
