  VALUES ('0f9f41f2-ba46-4568-8ea2-f2f05969268c'::uuid, tid, 'primitive', 'p12 · Page 3 of 15
Topic Number: AFX23D-TCSO1 Proposal Numbe', 'Page 3 of 15
Topic Number: AFX23D-TCSO1 Proposal Number: F 2 - 1 7 5 2 8
Proposal Title: “AFSFC Requested Innovative Directed Energy Counter UAS Solution Integrated Into
Proven Detection, Tracking, and Targeting System.”
SBC: Immobileyes
2.1.3 Non-Defense Commercial Solution
This project integrates the non-defense commercial solutions from two innovative technology startups to
produce the HALAR-L counter-UAS offering. Separately the commercial solutions
offered by Immobileyes (LASER STORM) and Lighthouse Avionics (Virtual
Control Tower) provide significant value-add to their commercial marketplaces.
Integrated together the non-defense commercial solutions can be adapted to create
an Air Force directed and desired counter-UAS capability which addresses the
challenges voiced by many USAF Stakeholders.
The proposed drone swarm intelligence denial solution is based on Immobileyes’
innovative, patented, programmable, scalable directed energy (LED and Laser)
disruption and dazzling system called HALAR. The solution takes a new
“shotgun” approach to laser emission, making it possible to target and “blind”
several UAS sensors at once, making it a force multiplier for security, OPSEC,
counter surveillance, counter unmanned aerial systems, and a litany of other value-added commercial
applications. The Immobileyes HALAR solutions are developed by researchers in the fields of physics,
optics, and electronics with over a 20-year track record of collaboration with the USAF and represents a first
transformative improvement to known existing light-based directed energy technologies.
A second improvement in the HALAR capability is the development of novel
liquid crystal (LC)-based optics (in collaboration with our RI partner UCF
College of Optics and Photonics /CREOL). This innovative approach beings
several advantages: (a) it overcomes size limitations of conventional optics
allowing larger aperture / higher pow er laser sources that increase the effective
range of the system; (b) it provides a platform for electronically switchable
optics that can adjust in real-time to alter the range, coverage, or pattern of the
laser beams; and (c) it makes possible the use of one optical element to shape the beam of multiple laser
wavelengths. Beyond the current solution, the LC-based optical elements have various commercial
applications including in telecommunications and space.
The detect, track, and characterize capabilities of the solution will be delivered
through Lighthouse’s Virtual Control Towers (VCTs). The solution was
developed to address the FAA’s requirements for active air space management
and will enable the adoption of commercial autonomous air solutions. It is
undergoing evaluation and certification by the Federal Aviation Administration
(FAA) and is positioned to be a critical infrastructure solution required to
support wide scale beyond visual line of sight (BVLOS) operations of
commercial drones and air taxis. These commercially available systems
provide the perfect solution for persistent airspace monitoring detecting of
threats, targeting of threats, and queuing the HALAR solution to deny those
threats sensing capability.
2.1.4 Proposed Adaptation of Non-Defense Commercial Solution
During this Phase II effort, Immobileyes will leverage its existing HALAR
designs to research, design, develop, demonstrate, and evaluate an Air Force adaptation of the HALAR-L
solution. Efforts will focus on (I) expanding the ability of the HALAR optical element to split higher power,
multi-wavelength beams in a system that can be portable, low-cost and field-adaptable to various conditions,
and (II) integrating the HALAR-L lasers system with the commercially available VCT for drone detection,
characterization and tracking. During its thorough Air Force customer discovery to analyze the potential
impact of the HALAR solution on specific Air Force needs, multiple Air Force customers expressed an interest
in utilizing the HALAR solutions in support of their operations. With significant feedback received during the
Feasibility Study and the commitment of engagement from the AFSFC, AFRL, and the 72nd SFS,
Immobileyes will ensure that the final solution developed will be ready for adoption and use by Air Force
Defenders across the globe.', '[{"id":"99956076-5867-41ff-a3e9-79e3f077e07a","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Page 3 of 15\nTopic Number: AFX23D-TCSO1 Proposal Number: F 2 - 1 7 5 2 8\nProposal Title: “AFSFC Requested Innovative Directed Energy Counter UAS Solution Integrated Into\nProven Detection, Tracking, and Targeting System.”\nSBC: Immobileyes\n2.1.3 Non-Defense Commercial Solution\nThis project integrates the non-defense commercial solutions from two innovative technology startups to\nproduce the HALAR-L counter-UAS offering. Separately the commercial solutions\noffered by Immobileyes (LASER STORM) and Lighthouse Avionics (Virtual\nControl Tower) provide significant value-add to their commercial marketplaces.\nIntegrated together the non-defense commercial solutions can be adapted to create\nan Air Force directed and desired counter-UAS capability which addresses the\nchallenges voiced by many USAF Stakeholders.\nThe proposed drone swarm intelligence denial solution is based on Immobileyes’\ninnovative, patented, programmable, scalable directed energy (LED and Laser)\ndisruption and dazzling system called HALAR. The solution takes a new\n“shotgun” approach to laser emission, making it possible to target and “blind”\nseveral UAS sensors at once, making it a force multiplier for security, OPSEC,\ncounter surveillance, counter unmanned aerial systems, and a litany of other value-added commercial\napplications. The Immobileyes HALAR solutions are developed by researchers in the fields of physics,\noptics, and electronics with over a 20-year track record of collaboration with the USAF and represents a first\ntransformative improvement to known existing light-based directed energy technologies.\nA second improvement in the HALAR capability is the development of novel\nliquid crystal (LC)-based optics (in collaboration with our RI partner UCF\nCollege of Optics and Photonics /CREOL). This innovative approach beings\nseveral advantages: (a) it overcomes size limitations of conventional optics\nallowing larger aperture / higher pow er laser sources that increase the effective\nrange of the system; (b) it provides a platform for electronically switchable\noptics that can adjust in real-time to alter the range, coverage, or pattern of the\nlaser beams; and (c) it makes possible the use of one optical element to shape the beam of multiple laser\nwavelengths. Beyond the current solution, the LC-based optical elements have various commercial\napplications including in telecommunications and space.\nThe detect, track, and characterize capabilities of the solution will be delivered\nthrough Lighthouse’s Virtual Control Towers (VCTs). The solution was\ndeveloped to address the FAA’s requirements for active air space management\nand will enable the adoption of commercial autonomous air solutions. It is\nundergoing evaluation and certification by the Federal Aviation Administration\n(FAA) and is positioned to be a critical infrastructure solution required to\nsupport wide scale beyond visual line of sight (BVLOS) operations of\ncommercial drones and air taxis. These commercially available systems\nprovide the perfect solution for persistent airspace monitoring detecting of\nthreats, targeting of threats, and queuing the HALAR solution to deny those\nthreats sensing capability.\n2.1.4 Proposed Adaptation of Non-Defense Commercial Solution\nDuring this Phase II effort, Immobileyes will leverage its existing HALAR\ndesigns to research, design, develop, demonstrate, and evaluate an Air Force adaptation of the HALAR-L\nsolution. Efforts will focus on (I) expanding the ability of the HALAR optical element to split higher power,\nmulti-wavelength beams in a system that can be portable, low-cost and field-adaptable to various conditions,\nand (II) integrating the HALAR-L lasers system with the commercially available VCT for drone detection,\ncharacterization and tracking. During its thorough Air Force customer discovery to analyze the potential\nimpact of the HALAR solution on specific Air Force needs, multiple Air Force customers expressed an interest\nin utilizing the HALAR solutions in support of their operations. With significant feedback received during the\nFeasibility Study and the commitment of engagement from the AFSFC, AFRL, and the 72nd SFS,\nImmobileyes will ensure that the final solution developed will be ready for adoption and use by Air Force\nDefenders across the globe."},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"imported"},"library_eligible":true}]'::jsonb, NULL, 615, 4284, '{}'::jsonb, 'approved', 0.5, 'pending', 0.5, 0, 'upload', 'f0ef96b8-1375-4baf-887d-c6f031ffa9b4'::uuid, NULL, NULL, '686842de-ae88-44ac-b724-8e550f079e3e'::uuid, 'tenant', '2026-08-18T22:42:59.349Z'::timestamptz, '2026-08-18T22:42:59.349Z'::timestamptz, 'admin', uid, '[{"blockIds":["b12"],"sourceAtomId":"6d9d25e2-3aa0-4ab5-b01c-518e4c043627"}]'::jsonb, NULL)
