  VALUES ('0c1aea9a-7a4d-4f31-9c33-2ffdfaaa637a'::uuid, tid, 'primitive', 'p15 · Page 6 of 15
Topic Number: AFX23D-TCSO1 Proposal Numbe', 'Page 6 of 15
Topic Number: AFX23D-TCSO1 Proposal Number: F 2 - 1 7 5 2 8
Proposal Title: “AFSFC Requested Innovative Directed Energy Counter UAS Solution Integrated Into
Proven Detection, Tracking, and Targeting System.”
SBC: Immobileyes
scalable, highly capable solution to defeat the intelligence collection capabilities of low-altitude s-UAS and
drone swarms. The anticipated project time is 21 months, with an additional 3 months for reporting. At the
completion of the project, the Team will work with our Air Force end-users to demonstrate a fully capable and
ready-to-be-deployed drone swarm intelligence collection denial solution.
Project Objectives and Key Results:
➢ Effective Multi-Target System: The system will demonstrate the ability to detect, track,
characterize, target, and deny intelligence collection by multiple drones/ swarms and other low-
altitude threats. This will be demonstrated with surrogate sUAS electronics.
➢ Characterization of HALAR-LS: The team will successfully characterize the output of the HALAR
solution provided. This will include in-depth testing and analysis of the device’s performance under
varying operational parameters.
➢ Scalable: The system should demonstrate the potential to be easily scaled through the deployment of
additional stand-alone systems. Phase II will establish the upper limit of the laser/optical element
combination.
➢ Deployable: The system should demonstrate the potential to be easily deployed for operation in
remote environments given power consumption, cooling & laser source options.
➢ Dynamic Real-time Adjustable Optical Element: Deliver liquid crystal-based optical elements
developed by UCF (RI) as the foundation for Next-Gen programmable large-area optical filters
capable of real time switching to alter range and area-coverage of the system for AF and Space
Force target acquisition and Counter-Sensor capabilities.
Technical Objective: Immobileyes aims to demonstrate the 3 parameters that define the effectiveness
of directed energy beams on sensor targets to show the effectiveness of the HALAR-L system against
drones:
(1) Beamlet quality: since using an optical element to split a single laser beam into many
“Beamlets” will affect the beamlet quality, the system needs to show that each Beamlet has up to 90%
of the original laser beam quality (profile, characteristics) to be effective at a range of 1Km,
(2) Exposure time: The integrated system will allow sufficient time on target at the speed of a
moving drone (as defined by DAF Customer / End User)
(3) Area coverage: The coverage area of the Beamlets will be calculated and defined based on
(1) and (2) above.
2.2.1 Required Stakeholders
Immobileyes’ MOU sponsor (Tinker AFB – 72nd SFS) has committed to providing all access and support
required to ensure that the Phase II project is successfully executed. They have also agreed to consult with Air
Force legal, contracting, SBIR personnel, and other Air Force Stakeholders to formalize cooperation with
Immobileyes as needed and required. Additionally, they have committed to acting as the Liaison with
AFSFC, AFGSC, AFIMSC and other interested and involved stakeholders for this initiative.
2.2.2 Phase II Work Plan
The high-level workplan which includes tasking, milestones and deliverables is included in the Memorandum
of Understanding (MOU) which was executed by the 72nd SFS (in collaboration with other USAF
Stakeholders). Along with this workplan, Immobileyes developed a detailed schedule for the project expressed
through a GANTT chart (which will be delivered to TPOC upon award) which included the critical paths for
analysis, development, testing and delivery of the proposed solution.
2.2.2.1 Scope
The Immobileyes Team has worked with our Air Force Customer and End User to scope this project.
However, it has also considered requirements of numerous counter-UAS programs across the military. The
scope of the current project is the result of information from various relevant RFIs and RFPs for drone defeat', '[{"id":"c4ea7539-de53-42f4-9a77-f0954b0dcd90","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Page 6 of 15\nTopic Number: AFX23D-TCSO1 Proposal Number: F 2 - 1 7 5 2 8\nProposal Title: “AFSFC Requested Innovative Directed Energy Counter UAS Solution Integrated Into\nProven Detection, Tracking, and Targeting System.”\nSBC: Immobileyes\nscalable, highly capable solution to defeat the intelligence collection capabilities of low-altitude s-UAS and\ndrone swarms. The anticipated project time is 21 months, with an additional 3 months for reporting. At the\ncompletion of the project, the Team will work with our Air Force end-users to demonstrate a fully capable and\nready-to-be-deployed drone swarm intelligence collection denial solution.\nProject Objectives and Key Results:\n➢ Effective Multi-Target System: The system will demonstrate the ability to detect, track,\ncharacterize, target, and deny intelligence collection by multiple drones/ swarms and other low-\naltitude threats. This will be demonstrated with surrogate sUAS electronics.\n➢ Characterization of HALAR-LS: The team will successfully characterize the output of the HALAR\nsolution provided. This will include in-depth testing and analysis of the device’s performance under\nvarying operational parameters.\n➢ Scalable: The system should demonstrate the potential to be easily scaled through the deployment of\nadditional stand-alone systems. Phase II will establish the upper limit of the laser/optical element\ncombination.\n➢ Deployable: The system should demonstrate the potential to be easily deployed for operation in\nremote environments given power consumption, cooling & laser source options.\n➢ Dynamic Real-time Adjustable Optical Element: Deliver liquid crystal-based optical elements\ndeveloped by UCF (RI) as the foundation for Next-Gen programmable large-area optical filters\ncapable of real time switching to alter range and area-coverage of the system for AF and Space\nForce target acquisition and Counter-Sensor capabilities.\nTechnical Objective: Immobileyes aims to demonstrate the 3 parameters that define the effectiveness\nof directed energy beams on sensor targets to show the effectiveness of the HALAR-L system against\ndrones:\n(1) Beamlet quality: since using an optical element to split a single laser beam into many\n“Beamlets” will affect the beamlet quality, the system needs to show that each Beamlet has up to 90%\nof the original laser beam quality (profile, characteristics) to be effective at a range of 1Km,\n(2) Exposure time: The integrated system will allow sufficient time on target at the speed of a\nmoving drone (as defined by DAF Customer / End User)\n(3) Area coverage: The coverage area of the Beamlets will be calculated and defined based on\n(1) and (2) above.\n2.2.1 Required Stakeholders\nImmobileyes’ MOU sponsor (Tinker AFB – 72nd SFS) has committed to providing all access and support\nrequired to ensure that the Phase II project is successfully executed. They have also agreed to consult with Air\nForce legal, contracting, SBIR personnel, and other Air Force Stakeholders to formalize cooperation with\nImmobileyes as needed and required. Additionally, they have committed to acting as the Liaison with\nAFSFC, AFGSC, AFIMSC and other interested and involved stakeholders for this initiative.\n2.2.2 Phase II Work Plan\nThe high-level workplan which includes tasking, milestones and deliverables is included in the Memorandum\nof Understanding (MOU) which was executed by the 72nd SFS (in collaboration with other USAF\nStakeholders). Along with this workplan, Immobileyes developed a detailed schedule for the project expressed\nthrough a GANTT chart (which will be delivered to TPOC upon award) which included the critical paths for\nanalysis, development, testing and delivery of the proposed solution.\n2.2.2.1 Scope\nThe Immobileyes Team has worked with our Air Force Customer and End User to scope this project.\nHowever, it has also considered requirements of numerous counter-UAS programs across the military. The\nscope of the current project is the result of information from various relevant RFIs and RFPs for drone defeat"},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"imported"},"library_eligible":true}]'::jsonb, NULL, 603, 4021, '{}'::jsonb, 'approved', 0.5, 'pending', 0.5, 0, 'upload', 'f0ef96b8-1375-4baf-887d-c6f031ffa9b4'::uuid, NULL, NULL, '686842de-ae88-44ac-b724-8e550f079e3e'::uuid, 'tenant', '2026-08-18T22:42:59.409Z'::timestamptz, '2026-08-18T22:42:59.409Z'::timestamptz, 'admin', uid, '[{"blockIds":["b15"],"sourceAtomId":"6d9d25e2-3aa0-4ab5-b01c-518e4c043627"}]'::jsonb, NULL)
