  VALUES ('28d02af7-1442-401c-b008-bd7284bf8aa0'::uuid, tid, 'primitive', 'p20 · Page 11 of 15
Topic Number: AFX23D-TCSO1 Proposal Numb', 'Page 11 of 15
Topic Number: AFX23D-TCSO1 Proposal Number: F 2 - 1 7 5 2 8
Proposal Title: “AFSFC Requested Innovative Directed Energy Counter UAS Solution Integrated Into
Proven Detection, Tracking, and Targeting System.”
SBC: Immobileyes
Immobileyes is an innovation and product development company
located in Kent, Ohio. The company was founded, in 2020, to
commercialize cutting-edge research associated with optics-based
innovations. The invention, and patenting, of the HALAR technology
platform provided the strong foundation on which the company is built.
With innovative light-control capability, HALAR has applications
across multiple industries, including security, law enforcement and
militray and can be embodied in multiple product offerings.
During initial customer discovery and market analysis, the law enforcement and security markets stood above
all others. The tactical benefits gained though the HALAR solution’s ability to distract, disorient, and delay
the actions by potential threats was deemed invaluable by all the law enforcement personnel engaged. The
greatest strength of Immobileyes is its team, with:

25-years of R&D and product development of dynamic, intense light and laser eye protection for the Air Force, successful commercialization of SBIR-funded technology for consumer and military markets, experience setting up a new manufacturing facility to meet ISO and MIL-SPEC requirements and providing
Made in USA products for military customers. In addition, the team has formed several strategic industry
partnerships to enable continued innovation and expansion into new markets.
2.3.3 Customer and Competition
Customers: In addition to the DoD customers associated with this proposal, namely AFSFC and the 38,000+
Defenders, Immobileyes is engaging with the commercial sector security integrators and law enforcement
customers. This includes Homeland Security, The Department of Justice, and other Federal agencies.
Competitors: In The Immobileyes Team, with the support
of their Air Force stakeholders, have conducted an
exhaustive analysis of the C-UAS solutions currently fielded
or under development in each of the service components.
An example table of those solutions are highlighted in the
image here. All the competing solutions are focused on
disrupting communication links or GPS signals, which are
rendered useless when drone swarms are operating
autonomously and navigating visually. Moreover, the
kinetic or energy defeat solutions have been demonstrated to
be easily overmatched when facing more than a single threat
sUAS. The proposed solution focuses more on the wide-area denial of intelligence gathering capabilities
by threat drone swarms. This mechanism of action also allows the Air Force to more aggressively engage
any drone (threat or not) to deny collection of information on critical Air Force operations.
2.3.4 Market
The proposed solution addresses the C-UAS market which size is
currently $1B annually and growing exponentially. It is expected to
reach $12.6B by 2030. Disabling or “killing” the drone is not always the
desired outcome. The largest concern is defeating the drone''s ability to
impact security or operational effectiveness. Immobileyes’ HALAR-
Laser solves the challenge posed by a drone swarm with multiple cameras
by “blinding” the entire swarm thereby mitigating the threat. Each of
the Air Force bases evaluated for HALAR-Laser adoption for c-UAS
activities would procure hundreds of low-cost systems.', '[{"id":"61d66411-bacf-4865-90d1-696fc5b56e8e","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Page 11 of 15\nTopic Number: AFX23D-TCSO1 Proposal Number: F 2 - 1 7 5 2 8\nProposal Title: “AFSFC Requested Innovative Directed Energy Counter UAS Solution Integrated Into\nProven Detection, Tracking, and Targeting System.”\nSBC: Immobileyes\nImmobileyes is an innovation and product development company\nlocated in Kent, Ohio. The company was founded, in 2020, to\ncommercialize cutting-edge research associated with optics-based\ninnovations. The invention, and patenting, of the HALAR technology\nplatform provided the strong foundation on which the company is built.\nWith innovative light-control capability, HALAR has applications\nacross multiple industries, including security, law enforcement and\nmilitray and can be embodied in multiple product offerings.\nDuring initial customer discovery and market analysis, the law enforcement and security markets stood above\nall others. The tactical benefits gained though the HALAR solution’s ability to distract, disorient, and delay\nthe actions by potential threats was deemed invaluable by all the law enforcement personnel engaged. The\ngreatest strength of Immobileyes is its team, with:\n\n25-years of R&D and product development of dynamic, intense light and laser eye protection for the Air Force, successful commercialization of SBIR-funded technology for consumer and military markets, experience setting up a new manufacturing facility to meet ISO and MIL-SPEC requirements and providing\nMade in USA products for military customers. In addition, the team has formed several strategic industry\npartnerships to enable continued innovation and expansion into new markets.\n2.3.3 Customer and Competition\nCustomers: In addition to the DoD customers associated with this proposal, namely AFSFC and the 38,000+\nDefenders, Immobileyes is engaging with the commercial sector security integrators and law enforcement\ncustomers. This includes Homeland Security, The Department of Justice, and other Federal agencies.\nCompetitors: In The Immobileyes Team, with the support\nof their Air Force stakeholders, have conducted an\nexhaustive analysis of the C-UAS solutions currently fielded\nor under development in each of the service components.\nAn example table of those solutions are highlighted in the\nimage here. All the competing solutions are focused on\ndisrupting communication links or GPS signals, which are\nrendered useless when drone swarms are operating\nautonomously and navigating visually. Moreover, the\nkinetic or energy defeat solutions have been demonstrated to\nbe easily overmatched when facing more than a single threat\nsUAS. The proposed solution focuses more on the wide-area denial of intelligence gathering capabilities\nby threat drone swarms. This mechanism of action also allows the Air Force to more aggressively engage\nany drone (threat or not) to deny collection of information on critical Air Force operations.\n2.3.4 Market\nThe proposed solution addresses the C-UAS market which size is\ncurrently $1B annually and growing exponentially. It is expected to\nreach $12.6B by 2030. Disabling or “killing” the drone is not always the\ndesired outcome. The largest concern is defeating the drone''s ability to\nimpact security or operational effectiveness. Immobileyes’ HALAR-\nLaser solves the challenge posed by a drone swarm with multiple cameras\nby “blinding” the entire swarm thereby mitigating the threat. Each of\nthe Air Force bases evaluated for HALAR-Laser adoption for c-UAS\nactivities would procure hundreds of low-cost systems."},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"imported"},"library_eligible":true}]'::jsonb, NULL, 507, 3488, '{}'::jsonb, 'approved', 0.5, 'pending', 0.5, 0, 'upload', 'f0ef96b8-1375-4baf-887d-c6f031ffa9b4'::uuid, NULL, NULL, '686842de-ae88-44ac-b724-8e550f079e3e'::uuid, 'tenant', '2026-08-18T22:42:59.501Z'::timestamptz, '2026-08-18T22:42:59.501Z'::timestamptz, 'admin', uid, '[{"blockIds":["b20"],"sourceAtomId":"6d9d25e2-3aa0-4ab5-b01c-518e4c043627"}]'::jsonb, NULL)
  ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

