  VALUES ('e5cfe988-f9bf-45f7-92ff-f21e366edec1'::uuid, tid, 'primitive', 'p23 · Page 14 of 15
Topic Number: AFX23D-TCSO1 Proposal Numb', 'Page 14 of 15
Topic Number: AFX23D-TCSO1 Proposal Number: F 2 - 1 7 5 2 8
Proposal Title: “AFSFC Requested Innovative Directed Energy Counter UAS Solution Integrated Into
Proven Detection, Tracking, and Targeting System.”
SBC: Immobileyes
2.7 Non-Defense Commercial Customers
The commercial use of drones has been growing exponentially over the last decade.
The worldwide markets have consumed $23B in commercial UAV sales in 2020, a
number expected to double by 2025, as researched by BCC. Uncooperative or hostile
drone swarms pose a threat to Air Force base operations, airports, critical
infrastructure, and the public at large. Drones are being used for illicit package
delivery in prisons, at the border and other locations. Most recently, the Lighthouse
team has been working with Amentum, a leading federal contractor leading UAS and
C-UAS operations for many federal customers. Together with the team, discussions have been held with
the Ohio Department of Justice who are anxious to pilot the VCT-HALAR solution at prisons. Prisons in
America have an increasingly large problem with illegal package delivery with sUASs. A system to help
detect, track, and classify sUASs in the area and defeat those deemed to be suspect would prevent these
packages reaching inmates. There are an estimated over 3,000 detention facilities that could utilize the
VCT-HALAR. If each site requires 2x HALAR-Ls on average, the total market is ~$600 million.
2.8 Investors and Partners
UCF - CREOL (RI): Home to world-class research centers and cutting-edge facilities,
UCF College of Optics & Photonics (CREOL) is renowned as a global leader in optics
and photonics. Research at the college is enriched by the high-tech industrial ecosystem
in central Florida that provides synergistic partnership opportunities. Professor Shin-
Tson Wu (see Key Personnel) is known for expanding the capabilities and accuracy of
non-mechanical laser beam steerers and is addressing the inherent limitations of current
technologies, from light detection and ranging (LiDAR) to high precision satellite-to-
satellite communications, using liquid crystals.
Dorcy is the committed manufacturing and distribution partner for Immobileyes. For over
55 years, Dorcy has been a global leader in the commercial flashlight market.
Headquartered in Columbus, Ohio, Dorcy has validated the value of HALAR solutions.
Dorcy is a strong supporter of Immobileyes and their engagement with the USAF. Letter
of support provided.
The City of Hilliard is a mid-sized municipality in Ohio which has developed an
innovative “City Labs” program which incubates and supports emerging solutions which
could have a positive impact on cities across the United States.
Kent Police Department (PD) serves the City of Kent with a population of 32,000 and is
home to Kent State University and its campus police and a member of North East Ohio
Metro SWAT. Both police departments have identified Immobileyes as an impactful
company for first responders and are actively supporting the company by testing and
evaluating effectiveness in the field and developing departmental operational policy.
Letters of support provided.
AlphaMicron is an award-winning, global leader in liquid crystal-based light reactive
technologies. For over 22 years ago, AlphaMicron has successfully pursued research and
development for the Department of Defense. AlphaMicron is an ardent supporter of
Immobileyes and provides research, design, and engineering support. Letter of support
provided.
Converge Technologies is an engineering services and business advisory company
created to support efficient commercialization of new and innovative technologies. The
company has developed other commercial electronics which will be leveraged for this
project. Letter of support provided.', '[{"id":"6156b1eb-530b-4ded-b1fe-3d0c4d5e1faf","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Page 14 of 15\nTopic Number: AFX23D-TCSO1 Proposal Number: F 2 - 1 7 5 2 8\nProposal Title: “AFSFC Requested Innovative Directed Energy Counter UAS Solution Integrated Into\nProven Detection, Tracking, and Targeting System.”\nSBC: Immobileyes\n2.7 Non-Defense Commercial Customers\nThe commercial use of drones has been growing exponentially over the last decade.\nThe worldwide markets have consumed $23B in commercial UAV sales in 2020, a\nnumber expected to double by 2025, as researched by BCC. Uncooperative or hostile\ndrone swarms pose a threat to Air Force base operations, airports, critical\ninfrastructure, and the public at large. Drones are being used for illicit package\ndelivery in prisons, at the border and other locations. Most recently, the Lighthouse\nteam has been working with Amentum, a leading federal contractor leading UAS and\nC-UAS operations for many federal customers. Together with the team, discussions have been held with\nthe Ohio Department of Justice who are anxious to pilot the VCT-HALAR solution at prisons. Prisons in\nAmerica have an increasingly large problem with illegal package delivery with sUASs. A system to help\ndetect, track, and classify sUASs in the area and defeat those deemed to be suspect would prevent these\npackages reaching inmates. There are an estimated over 3,000 detention facilities that could utilize the\nVCT-HALAR. If each site requires 2x HALAR-Ls on average, the total market is ~$600 million.\n2.8 Investors and Partners\nUCF - CREOL (RI): Home to world-class research centers and cutting-edge facilities,\nUCF College of Optics & Photonics (CREOL) is renowned as a global leader in optics\nand photonics. Research at the college is enriched by the high-tech industrial ecosystem\nin central Florida that provides synergistic partnership opportunities. Professor Shin-\nTson Wu (see Key Personnel) is known for expanding the capabilities and accuracy of\nnon-mechanical laser beam steerers and is addressing the inherent limitations of current\ntechnologies, from light detection and ranging (LiDAR) to high precision satellite-to-\nsatellite communications, using liquid crystals.\nDorcy is the committed manufacturing and distribution partner for Immobileyes. For over\n55 years, Dorcy has been a global leader in the commercial flashlight market.\nHeadquartered in Columbus, Ohio, Dorcy has validated the value of HALAR solutions.\nDorcy is a strong supporter of Immobileyes and their engagement with the USAF. Letter\nof support provided.\nThe City of Hilliard is a mid-sized municipality in Ohio which has developed an\ninnovative “City Labs” program which incubates and supports emerging solutions which\ncould have a positive impact on cities across the United States.\nKent Police Department (PD) serves the City of Kent with a population of 32,000 and is\nhome to Kent State University and its campus police and a member of North East Ohio\nMetro SWAT. Both police departments have identified Immobileyes as an impactful\ncompany for first responders and are actively supporting the company by testing and\nevaluating effectiveness in the field and developing departmental operational policy.\nLetters of support provided.\nAlphaMicron is an award-winning, global leader in liquid crystal-based light reactive\ntechnologies. For over 22 years ago, AlphaMicron has successfully pursued research and\ndevelopment for the Department of Defense. AlphaMicron is an ardent supporter of\nImmobileyes and provides research, design, and engineering support. Letter of support\nprovided.\nConverge Technologies is an engineering services and business advisory company\ncreated to support efficient commercialization of new and innovative technologies. The\ncompany has developed other commercial electronics which will be leveraged for this\nproject. Letter of support provided."},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"imported"},"library_eligible":true}]'::jsonb, NULL, 568, 3800, '{}'::jsonb, 'approved', 0.5, 'pending', 0.5, 0, 'upload', 'f0ef96b8-1375-4baf-887d-c6f031ffa9b4'::uuid, NULL, NULL, '686842de-ae88-44ac-b724-8e550f079e3e'::uuid, 'tenant', '2026-08-18T22:42:59.560Z'::timestamptz, '2026-08-18T22:42:59.560Z'::timestamptz, 'admin', uid, '[{"blockIds":["b23"],"sourceAtomId":"6d9d25e2-3aa0-4ab5-b01c-518e4c043627"}]'::jsonb, NULL)
