Open Topic Number: 	', 'Proposal Number: N26BX-NP002-0450
Open Topic Number: 	DON26BX03-NP002 	Immobileyes, Inc.
Page 7 of 10
Atmospheric and seeker variability at
operational range
Bench testing plus modeled range analysis; conservative Phase I
feasibility claims, with the range demonstration deferred to the
Option
Eye-safety and FAA constraints on
CONUS employment
FAA-aware graduated escalation (warn/deter first); OSHA laser-
safety controls; non-kinetic-first doctrine
Integration risk with Navy sensors
and C2
MOSA / detection-agnostic interfaces; reuse of fielded STORM
sensor integrations. Partnership with PerceptView.
1.3 Related Work
GHOST builds directly on a funded, TRL-5 directed-energy C-UAS lineage designed, developed, and
successfully demonstrated by Immobileyes:
Air Force STTR Phase II / TACFI. (SBC: Immobileyes; Research Institutions: University of Central
Florida CREOL) Immobileyes successfully advanced the STORM™ directed-energy C-UAS system
from TRL 3 to TRL 5, incorporating operational feedback from the 72d Security Forces Squadron,
Tinker AFB. Across three Air Force STTR Phase I awards (2021, 2023) and a Phase II/TACFI effort, the
company has secured approximately $2 million in USAF SBIR/STTR funding to mature its adaptive
optical C-UAS technologies.
Intellectual Property. Immobileyes holds two issued patents (U.S. Pat 11,519,701 and 11,686,560 (Light
Shield Device) and Patent App. WO2025122941A1(Multi-Zone Multi-Target System), covering adaptive
optical countermeasure technologies that extend the Navy''s foundational seeker-confusion inventions
(U.S. Patents 8,305,252 and 8,212,709) were made available through the Department of War’s Defense
Patent Holiday initiative, a technology transition program that provides qualified companies with no-fee
Commercial Evaluation Licenses to evaluate and commercialize selected DoW laboratory inventionsand
form the technical basis for the proposed STORM/DEXTER architecture. The foundational patents
supporting this effort
AlphaMicron, Inc. (Strategic Partner – DEXTER / Liquid-Crystal Optics). Partner since 2020 (($600K
in-kind + $1.6M matching), AlphaMicron is a recognized leader in liquid crystal electro-optics with
decades of experience developing advanced optical technologies for the USAF, Army, and Navy through
numerous R&D and technology maturation contracts. Its electronically switchable LC product is the first
of its kind to be included into the U.S. Army''s APEL (Authorized Protective Eyewear List) program,
demonstrating its ability to mature laboratory innovations into fielded military products. Through this
partnership, AlphaMicron provides the DEXTER™ adaptive beam-routing technology, including its
liquid-crystal polarization gratings and software-controlled optical switching architecture, and supports
prototype development, manufacturing, and transition to production. AlphaMicron also maintains a long-
standing relationship with the AFRL Functional Materials Division, providing additional expertise in
advanced electro-optic materials and device development.
PerceptView (Partner) combines decades of machine-vision and target-tracking expertise with ten years
of experience building and delivering end-to-end counter-UAS solutions. Its modular systems integrate
EO/IR cameras, automated target tracking, radar, Remote ID, ADS-B, Cursor-on-Target and other
sensors, with APIs for connection to external command-and-control platforms and effectors.
1.4 Defense Need
End users are NAVAIR and NAVSEA C-UAS for Naval installations, Naval aircraft, and ships, plus
USMC and Joint-Force expeditionary air defense. The most reasonable near-term use case is fixed and
expeditionary point defense of high-value assets against Group 1–2 EO-guided FPV fiber drones that
today''s RF/GPS-centric C-UAS cannot engage. The system is small enough to be mounted to a fast boat
or HMMWV for SEAL, and other special operations AND CAN be scaled and modularized to support
full ship or Naval base defense and denial of drone threats.', '[{"id":"38f1c0f9-1dda-4ce3-ab28-7c1d888bf668","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Proposal Number: N26BX-NP002-0450\nOpen Topic Number: \tDON26BX03-NP002 \tImmobileyes, Inc.\nPage 7 of 10\nAtmospheric and seeker variability at\noperational range\nBench testing plus modeled range analysis; conservative Phase I\nfeasibility claims, with the range demonstration deferred to the\nOption\nEye-safety and FAA constraints on\nCONUS employment\nFAA-aware graduated escalation (warn/deter first); OSHA laser-\nsafety controls; non-kinetic-first doctrine\nIntegration risk with Navy sensors\nand C2\nMOSA / detection-agnostic interfaces; reuse of fielded STORM\nsensor integrations. Partnership with PerceptView.\n1.3 Related Work\nGHOST builds directly on a funded, TRL-5 directed-energy C-UAS lineage designed, developed, and\nsuccessfully demonstrated by Immobileyes:\nAir Force STTR Phase II / TACFI. (SBC: Immobileyes; Research Institutions: University of Central\nFlorida CREOL) Immobileyes successfully advanced the STORM™ directed-energy C-UAS system\nfrom TRL 3 to TRL 5, incorporating operational feedback from the 72d Security Forces Squadron,\nTinker AFB. Across three Air Force STTR Phase I awards (2021, 2023) and a Phase II/TACFI effort, the\ncompany has secured approximately $2 million in USAF SBIR/STTR funding to mature its adaptive\noptical C-UAS technologies.\nIntellectual Property. Immobileyes holds two issued patents (U.S. Pat 11,519,701 and 11,686,560 (Light\nShield Device) and Patent App. WO2025122941A1(Multi-Zone Multi-Target System), covering adaptive\noptical countermeasure technologies that extend the Navy''s foundational seeker-confusion inventions\n(U.S. Patents 8,305,252 and 8,212,709) were made available through the Department of War’s Defense\nPatent Holiday initiative, a technology transition program that provides qualified companies with no-fee\nCommercial Evaluation Licenses to evaluate and commercialize selected DoW laboratory inventionsand\nform the technical basis for the proposed STORM/DEXTER architecture. The foundational patents\nsupporting this effort\nAlphaMicron, Inc. (Strategic Partner – DEXTER / Liquid-Crystal Optics). Partner since 2020 (($600K\nin-kind + $1.6M matching), AlphaMicron is a recognized leader in liquid crystal electro-optics with\ndecades of experience developing advanced optical technologies for the USAF, Army, and Navy through\nnumerous R&D and technology maturation contracts. Its electronically switchable LC product is the first\nof its kind to be included into the U.S. Army''s APEL (Authorized Protective Eyewear List) program,\ndemonstrating its ability to mature laboratory innovations into fielded military products. Through this\npartnership, AlphaMicron provides the DEXTER™ adaptive beam-routing technology, including its\nliquid-crystal polarization gratings and software-controlled optical switching architecture, and supports\nprototype development, manufacturing, and transition to production. AlphaMicron also maintains a long-\nstanding relationship with the AFRL Functional Materials Division, providing additional expertise in\nadvanced electro-optic materials and device development.\nPerceptView (Partner) combines decades of machine-vision and target-tracking expertise with ten years\nof experience building and delivering end-to-end counter-UAS solutions. Its modular systems integrate\nEO/IR cameras, automated target tracking, radar, Remote ID, ADS-B, Cursor-on-Target and other\nsensors, with APIs for connection to external command-and-control platforms and effectors.\n1.4 Defense Need\nEnd users are NAVAIR and NAVSEA C-UAS for Naval installations, Naval aircraft, and ships, plus\nUSMC and Joint-Force expeditionary air defense. The most reasonable near-term use case is fixed and\nexpeditionary point defense of high-value assets against Group 1–2 EO-guided FPV fiber drones that\ntoday''s RF/GPS-centric C-UAS cannot engage. The system is small enough to be mounted to a fast boat\nor HMMWV for SEAL, and other special operations AND CAN be scaled and modularized to support\nfull ship or Naval base defense and denial of drone threats."},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"imported"},"library_eligible":true}]'::jsonb, NULL, 529, 4005, '{}'::jsonb, 'approved', 0.5, 'pending', 0.5, 0, 'upload', '684d8917-6a86-4eeb-806d-4db1cd3a655f'::uuid, NULL, NULL, '686842de-ae88-44ac-b724-8e550f079e3e'::uuid, 'tenant', '2026-08-18T22:46:44.689Z'::timestamptz, '2026-08-18T22:46:44.689Z'::timestamptz, 'admin', uid, '[{"blockIds":["b14"],"sourceAtomId":"836dbf84-0f3f-42e4-bf1a-be16503c676c"}]'::jsonb, NULL)
