palletized or checked luggage -- 10 of 10 --', '[{"id":"4d357180-7327-458c-bc57-58c62f60dc47","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Proposal Number: N26BX-NP002-0450\nOpen Topic Number: \tDON26BX03-NP002 \tImmobileyes, Inc.\nPage 1 of 10\nGuided Hostile Optical Sensor-hijacking (GHOST: Adaptive Optical Countermeasures for\nEO-Guided Fiber-Optic FPV Drones\n1.0 Description of Proposed Phase I Technical Effort\nProblem and Significance\nFirst-person-view (FPV) fiber-optic drones have become one of the\nmost difficult unmanned-air-system (UAS) threats on the modern\nbattlefield. Unlike conventional FPV systems, these aircraft require\nno radiofrequency (RF) link, are immune to RF jamming, and operate\nin GPS-denied environments—relying almost entirely on continuous\nelectro-optical (EO) imagery for terminal guidance. Fielded at scale\nin Ukraine and by non-state actors, they defeat the RF-centric and\nGPS-centric counter-UAS (C-UAS) systems the Navy fields today.\nThe operational consequence is stark. RF-based detection and defeat\n(jammers, spoofers, high-power microwave) have no emission to\nexploit; GPS denial has no effect on a vehicle flown by wire over\nfiber; and kinetic interceptors are cost-prohibitive and collaterally\nconstrained against a sub-$1,000 airframe. A single fiber drone can\nbe produced for a few hundred dollars yet forces the defender to\nexpend a far costlier effect—an unfavorable cost-exchange and\nmagazine-depth problem that is most acute at the exact moment of a\nsaturating swarm attack against a Naval installation, aircraft on the\nramp, or a ship in the littorals.\nExisting C-UAS remains optimized for RF disruption or kinetic\ndefeat. Existing shipboard laser systems are effective on larger\nplatforms but typically require significant size, weight, power,\ncooling, and integration resources, limiting their suitability for\nsmaller, agile vessels. There remains an operational gap for low-\nSWaP, scalable, non-kinetic laser countermeasure systems capable of\ndetecting, tracking, identifying, and defeating both individual and\nswarming UAS across diverse maritime environments.\nTechnical Innovation: Guided Hostile Optical Sensor-hijacking\nImmobileyes proposes a software-defined counter-UAS architecture\nintegrating two Immobileyes technologies:\n● STORM™ (Smart Tracking & Optical Response Mitigation)\nis an AI-enabled, MOSA-based counter-UAS system that combines multi-sensor detection, precision\ntracking, and adaptive optical countermeasures. Through its partnership with PerceptView, a provider of\nEO/IR tracking and C2 solutions, STORM is sensor-agnostic by design, integrating EO/IR, radar, RF,\nand other detection sources.\n● \tImmobileyes has already developed and demonstrated a STORM™ prototype (Fig. 2) integrating\nan optical warn/dazzle payload with a PTZ EO/IR camera, enabling continuous target detection,\ntracking, and engagement. During an FAA-authorized operational demonstration for the 72d Security\nForces Squadron at Tinker AFB, the system achieved Warn (>500 m) and Dazzle (300–350 m)\noptical effects against representative UAS EO cameras while maintaining continuous target tracking,\nsubstantially reducing technical risk for the proposed effort.(Fig. 3)\nFigure 1. Fiber-optic FPV drones with\nno RF link, immune to jamming, now\nproliferating in current conflicts.\nFigure 2. Current AF system: STORM\nintegrated with PerceptView EO Tracking"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:46:45.344Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:46:45.344Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"f6f9b564-0c03-47de-944d-edc9c3754109","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 1 of 10 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:46:45.344Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:46:45.344Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"342db173-6c88-40f8-886a-5a91e6814c94","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Proposal Number: N26BX-NP002-0450\nOpen Topic Number: \tDON26BX03-NP002 \tImmobileyes, Inc.\nPage 2 of 10\nSTORM employs a graduated, human-in-the-loop optical\nresponse tailored to the threat (Fig. 4). Zone 1\n(Warn/Deter) uses low-level visible illumination to\nsignal presence and encourage compliance. Zone 2\n(Dazzle/Disrupt) degrades the drone''s electro-optical\ncamera, reducing image quality and ISR effectiveness.\nZone 3 (Deny/Damage) concentrates optical energy to\ndesignate targets or disable optical sensors, when\nauthorized, providing a scalable, non-kinetic response\nagainst EO-guided UAS.\n● \tDEXTER™ (Directed-Energy Beam RouTER) is a\nlow-SWaP adaptive beam-routing architecture developed by\nImmobileyes in collaboration with its strategic partner,\nAlphaMicron, Inc. (AMI), leveraging AMI''s decades of\nexpertise in defense-grade liquid crystal (LC) optical devices\nfor military and aerospace applications. Using adaptive LC\npolarization gratings, DEXTER electronically routes, splits,\ncombines, shapes, and redirects laser energy with no moving\nparts. The software-defined optical engine dynamically\nswitches a single laser beam among multiple output channels,\nor combines multiple laser inputs into a single output, in\nmilliseconds, enabling simultaneous engagement of multiple\ndrones, rapid beam steering, and scalable swarm defense at\nspeeds unattainable with conventional mechanically steered optical systems.\n● \tIntegration: Immobileyes has partnered with PerceptView to provide a mature, sensor-agnostic\nintegration architecture (Fig. 5). STORM fuses inputs from RF, radar, EO/IR, and other detection\nsystems and can operate either as a standalone C2 platform or as an integrated effector within existing\nNavy and DoW command-and-control architectures, including MEDUSA..\nOptical Seeker-Confusion-Overwhelm Mechanism\nThe effort updates and refines the Navy''s internal research\nand development of IR guided seeker-confusion principle\n(U.S. Pat. 8,305,252 and 8,212,709). The Navy\ndemonstrated that a continuous-wave optical source placed\nwithin an imaging seeker''s field of view generates a\nlocalized high-intensity region that the tracking algorithm\ninterprets as a feature or target; the tracker''s centroid or\nFigure 3. Drone camera field of view before\nand after STORM laser engagement at 400 m,\ndemonstrating effective sensor dazzle.\nFigure 5. PerceptView EX-SUADS\nExpeditionary C-UAS + C2 Sensor\nFigure 4. GHOST Graduated Escalation Framework\nFigure 6. Optical effects to disrupt EO-\nguided drone targeting with DEXTER"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:46:45.344Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:46:45.344Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"add88967-8600-4c59-9314-214005734602","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 2 of 10 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:46:45.344Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:46:45.344Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"a9352d1f-5b9d-4f26-acf7-171a32b1343c","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Proposal Number: N26BX-NP002-0450\nOpen Topic Number: \tDON26BX03-NP002 \tImmobileyes, Inc.\nPage 3 of 10\nfeature-match then migrates toward the induced source, steering the vehicle away from the protected as\nset. Modern EO-guided FPV fiber drones rely on the same class of feature-tracking and optical-flow\nguidance running on uncooled CMOS/CCD cameras. Against those sensors, controlled illumination\nproduces image saturation, blooming, false high-intensity features, contrast collapse, and machine-vision\nconfusion (Fig. 6), thus degrading or breaking terminal lock without physical destruction.\nThe proposed effort investigates the wavelength, dwell, polarization, and beam-geometry parameters that\nmaximize this effect across representative EO camera types and characterizes the transition from\nreversible dazzle (Zone 2) to persistent denial (Zone 3) as a function of delivered irradiance, giving the\noperator a calibrated, proportional response rather than a single binary effect.\nDEXTER Adaptive Beam Routing\nThe enabling technology is DEXTER, built on AlphaMicron''s adaptive polarization gratings\n(Pancharatnam–Berry-phase optics), which electronically switch laser beams between optical paths under\nlow-voltage control, with no moving parts. Cascading these elements creates a software-defined optical\nrouter that electronically routes, splits, combines, and steers laser beams, enabling rapid, reversible\ntransitions from a single focused beam to a multi-beam \"shotgun\" configuration capable of engaging\nmultiple targets simultaneously (Fig. 7). This replaces heavy, slow, failure-prone gimbaled mirror trains\nwith a low-SWaP, solid-state, software-defined aperture, and enables a distributed architecture in which\nmultiple beam directors share one laser source across fixed, mobile, shipboard, and expeditionary\nplatforms.\nGHOST is therefore not a fixed dazzler but a software-defined effector: wavelength real time; a single\nsub-kilowatt laser source is electronically allocated across multiple beam directors for multi-target and\nswarm engagement without duplicating laser hardware.\nSystem Snapshot — Key Attributes\nAttribute \tGHOST / STORM–DEXTER\nLaser output \t< 1 kW total, software-allocated across beams\nEffective range \tBurnout > 500m; Dazzle > 1 km; Warning > 3 km\nWavelengths \tMulti-wavelength — green / blue (450 nm) + IR\nBeam routing \tDEXTER liquid crystal grating: 1 → \ttens–hundreds of beams, millisecond\nswitching; NO MOVING PARTS\nCueing / integration \tDetection-agnostic MOSA — EO/IR, radar, RF/EW, TSPI – PROVEN AT\nTINKER AFB\nEngagement doctrine \tGraduated W arn/Deter → Dazzle/Disrupt → Deny/Damage; human-in-\nthe-loop or autonomous\nMaturity \tBuilds on TRL-5 AF STTR + TACFI prototype lineage\nFigure 7. DEXTER splits a single laser into multiple beams using a low-profile, non-mechanical LC optical element."},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:46:45.344Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:46:45.344Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"4b9ccff8-6dd6-4893-8f52-5f6915ac0e3a","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 3 of 10 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:46:45.344Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:46:45.344Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"1ed2f704-315e-491b-9d33-ec00e01cfa21","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Proposal Number: N26BX-NP002-0450\nOpen Topic Number: \tDON26BX03-NP002 \tImmobileyes, Inc.\nPage 4 of 10\n1.1 Phase I Technical Objectives\nPhase I will establish the technical feasibility of the STORM/DEXTER optical counter-UAS approach\nagainst EO-guided Group 1–2 UAS. Specific objectives include the following:\n● Objective 1: Demonstrate technical feasibility. Evaluate extending the Navy''s previously researched\nseeker-confusion concept from IR seekers to EO-guided fiber-optic drones; characterize the\nsusceptibility of representative EO cameras to optical perturbation.\n● Objective 2: Define the STORM/DEXTER system architecture and integration approach for Navy C-\nUAS applications, including interface requirements, MOSA compatibility, and the hardware and\nsoftware modifications necessary to support a shipboard Phase II prototype.\n● Objective 3: Quantify performance improvements. Model and measure improvements over existing\nnon-kinetic C-UAS: tracking disruption, multi-target engagement, reduced SWaP, elimination of\nmechanical beam steering, and effectiveness against RF-jamming-resistant fiber-optic drones.\n● Objective 4: Develop a Phase II transition plan. Define the roadmap for a prototype able to recognize,\ntrack, and non-kinetically defeat <Group 2 UAS and identify NAVAIR/NAVSEA transition.\nPhase I Feasibility Metrics (Go / No-Go)\nMetric \tPhase I Target \tGo / No-Go Threshold\nEO-camera susceptibility\n(measurable degradation of\nterminal tracking)\nDemonstrated on ≥ 2 representative\nEO/FPV cameras\n≥ 1 camera\nOptical effect \tRepeatable saturation / blooming at\nbench range\nQualitative confirmation\nDEXTER multi-beam routing \t≥ 3 simultaneously addressable output\nchannels (model/bench)\n≥ 2 channels\nPredicted SWaP vs. mechanically\nsteered baseline\n≥ 30% reduction (analysis) \tNet reduction\nArchitecture \tSTORM↔DEXTER interface + Navy\nC-UAS integration defined\nInterface defined\nAnticipated Phase I Outcomes\nAt the conclusion of Phase I, the Immobileyes team will have delivered:\n● \tDemonstrated feasibility of applying the Navy seeker-confusion principle to EO-guided FPV\nfiber-optic drones, evidenced by measured optical degradation of EO cameras.\n● \tA defined preliminary STORM/DEXTER architecture for Navy C - UAS applications, with\nthe STORMڸ DEXTER interface and MOSA specification.\n● \tIdentification of the hardware and software modifications required for an Option Phase benchtop\ndemonstration and a full Phase II developed prototype.\n● \tQuantified improvements over existing non-kinetic C-UAS system under development by\nImmobileyes and AlphaMicron: multi-target engagement, reduced SWaP, elimination of\nmechanical beam steering, and effectiveness against visual nav or fiber-optic drones.\n● \tAn established technical baseline and transition plan for a for Phase II developed prototype with a\nfull demonstration for NAVAIR/NAVSEA personnel.\nPhase I Deliverables:\n● \tKick-Off Briefing"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:46:45.344Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:46:45.344Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"6e473e42-00bd-440f-ac21-3b525cffca2a","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 4 of 10 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:46:45.344Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:46:45.344Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"3710ff24-cb5f-4826-9e10-a100591bb9cc","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Proposal Number: N26BX-NP002-0450\nOpen Topic Number: \tDON26BX03-NP002 \tImmobileyes, Inc.\nPage 5 of 10\n● \tProgress Report\n● \tFinal Technical Report documenting the STORM and DEXTER baseline, the Navy C-UAS\ncapability gaps addressed, required modifications, the feasibility assessment, and expected\nperformance improvements.\n● \tInitial Phase II Proposal and initial transition and commercialization framework.\n1.2 Phase I (Base and Option) Statement of Work\nThe Base effort (6 months) establishes feasibility and system architecture. The Option (6 months), funded\nseparately, furthers the effort toward Phase II with a breadboard optical-effects demonstration and a\nprototype/transition package. All work is performed at Immobileyes'' Kent, OH facility; no foreign\nnationals perform on this ITAR-restricted effort.\nTask No. \tTitle and Description \tMonths \tPerformer and\nDeliverable\n1 (Base) \tSystem Definition & Feasibility Assessment: Assess\nthe STORM/DEXTER architecture; review Group 1–2\nUAS threats, EO cameras, and current Navy C-UAS to\ndefine requirements and gaps.\n1 \tImmobileyes - Kick-Off\nBriefing\n2 (Base) \tSystem Architecture & Design: Preliminary\narchitecture integrating STORM detect-track with\nDEXTER beam routing; STORM/DEXTER interface;\noptical-engagement concept; control architecture.\n1–3 \tImmobileyes + AMI -\nProgress Report\n3 (Base) \tOptical Countermeasure Feasibility Evaluation:\nLaboratory test and modeling of saturation, blooming,\nfalse-feature generation, and contrast degradation\nagainst representative EO cameras; compare vs.\nexisting non-kinetic C-UAS.\n2–5 \tImmobileyes - Test Data\n& Analysis Report\n4 (Base) \tTransition Assessment & Phase I Reporting:\nconsolidate results; document performance\nimprovements and residual risk.\n5–6 \tImmobileyes - Final\nTechnical Report; Option\nPeriod and Initial Phase II\nProposal\n5 (Option) \tMaritime Platform Integration and Motion Analysis:\nEvaluate deployment on Navy platforms, define\nrequirements for stabilization, inertial compensation,\nand predictive tracking\n7–8 \tImmobileyes – Analysis &\nTechnical Report\n6 (Option) \tClosed-Loop Tracking and Breadboard Demo:\nIntegrating STORM detection/tracking with DEXTER\nbeam routing, Validate tracking performance, software-\ncontrol and optical effects.\n8-11 \tImmobileyes +AMI -\nBreadboard Demo &\nPerformance Data\nAnalysis and Report\n7 (Option) \tPhase II Prototype Definition & NAVAIR/NAVSEA\nTransition Package: prototype design baseline, test\nplan, and integration/transition plan for a Navy C-UAS\ndemonstration.\n10–12 \tImmobileyes - Prototype\nplan & Transition Planning\nPackage for Phase II\nBase Period — Task Approach\nTask 1 (Month 1) - System Definition & Feasibility Assessment Establish the Group 1–2 EO-guided\nfiber-optic threat set, catalogs representative FPV/EO camera technologies and current Navy C-UAS\ncapability gaps, and converts them into system requirements, operational use cases, performance metrics,\nand evaluation criteria presented at the Kick-Off Briefing."},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:46:45.344Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:46:45.344Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"d7d6c637-32f4-4e94-b95e-aafab8f6aa7e","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 5 of 10 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:46:45.344Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:46:45.344Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"08285311-be51-4d84-8520-04b82642db96","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Proposal Number: N26BX-NP002-0450\nOpen Topic Number: \tDON26BX03-NP002 \tImmobileyes, Inc.\nPage 6 of 10\nTask 2 (Months 1–3) - System Architecture & Design Develop the preliminary STORM/DEXTER\narchitecture, including the STORM↔DEXTER interface, integration with the PerceptView EO tracking\nsystem, the optical engagement concept, MOSA interfaces, and the preliminary software architecture.\nDevelop engagement algorithms that determine the appropriate optical response based on target range,\naspect angle, camera orientation, closing velocity, observed drone behavior, beam dwell time, and\ntransitions between Warn, Dazzle, Deny, single-beam, and multi-beam operating modes.\nTask 3 (Months 2–5) - Optical Countermeasure Feasibility Evaluation Perform analytical modeling\nand laboratory characterization of representative EO cameras to quantify laser-induced saturation,\nblooming, false-feature generation, contrast degradation, tracking instability, beam divergence, irradiance,\nspot size, wavelength, and engagement geometry. Determine the optical conditions and engagement\nenvelope required to misguide EO-guided fiber-optic FPV drones while minimizing laser power\nrequirements for various modalities of operation.\nTask 4 (Months 5–6) - Transition Assessment & Phase I Reporting integrate the Phase I analytical,\nsoftware, and laboratory results into the Final Technical Report, defines the preliminary Phase II\nprototype architecture, verification and validation approach, and manufacturing strategy, and prepares the\nInitial Phase II Proposal and NAVAIR/NAVSEA transition plan with manufacturing and commercial\ndevelopment pathways contemplated.\nOption Period — Task Approach\nTask 5 (Months 7–8) – Maritime Platform Integration and Motion Analysis\nEvaluate deployment of STORM on moving Navy platforms (RHIBs, Combatant Craft Assault,\nexpeditionary craft, and tactical vehicles). Develop preliminary closed-loop tracking architecture and\nassess the effects of platform roll, pitch, yaw, heave, vibration, target maneuvering, and relative motion\non tracking accuracy, laser pointing stability, beam jitter, optical dwell time, and overall countermeasure\neffectiveness. Define stabilization, inertial compensation, predictive tracking, and adaptive beam-control\nrequirements for maritime operation.\nTask 6 (Months 8–11) – Closed-Loop Tracking and Breadboard Demonstration\nDevelop a laboratory breadboard integrating STORM''s AI-enabled detection and tracking with\nDEXTER''s adaptive beam-routing engine to demonstrate the complete engagement chain from target\ndetection through electronic beam routing and measured optical effects on representative EO-guided FPV\ncameras. Validate tracking performance, software-controlled beam routing, single- and multi-beam\noperation, engagement geometry, and beam stability while verifying the feasibility of the proposed\nmaritime stabilization and control approach.\nTask 7 (Months 10–12) – Phase II Prototype Definition and Transition Planning\nDefine the Phase II prototype baseline, including the preliminary system design, software and hardware\narchitecture, verification and validation plan, and performance metrics. Prepare the NAVAIR/NAVSEA\nintegration package, manufacturing approach, technology transition strategy, and Phase II work plan to\nsupport a Navy C-UAS prototype demonstration.\nKey Technical Risks and Mitigations\nTechnical Risk \tMitigation\nEO-camera diversity limits\ngeneralization of the optical effect\nTest a representative camera set; parameterize wavelength, dwell,\nand beam geometry via DEXTER software control"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:46:45.344Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:46:45.344Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"3c379340-b26d-4f65-bd6a-0adfd684f023","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 6 of 10 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:46:45.344Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:46:45.344Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"babf2b0c-49db-478a-872b-4c25f624e1cb","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Proposal Number: N26BX-NP002-0450\nOpen Topic Number: \tDON26BX03-NP002 \tImmobileyes, Inc.\nPage 7 of 10\nAtmospheric and seeker variability at\noperational range\nBench testing plus modeled range analysis; conservative Phase I\nfeasibility claims, with the range demonstration deferred to the\nOption\nEye-safety and FAA constraints on\nCONUS employment\nFAA-aware graduated escalation (warn/deter first); OSHA laser-\nsafety controls; non-kinetic-first doctrine\nIntegration risk with Navy sensors\nand C2\nMOSA / detection-agnostic interfaces; reuse of fielded STORM\nsensor integrations. Partnership with PerceptView.\n1.3 Related Work\nGHOST builds directly on a funded, TRL-5 directed-energy C-UAS lineage designed, developed, and\nsuccessfully demonstrated by Immobileyes:\nAir Force STTR Phase II / TACFI. (SBC: Immobileyes; Research Institutions: University of Central\nFlorida CREOL) Immobileyes successfully advanced the STORM™ directed-energy C-UAS system\nfrom TRL 3 to TRL 5, incorporating operational feedback from the 72d Security Forces Squadron,\nTinker AFB. Across three Air Force STTR Phase I awards (2021, 2023) and a Phase II/TACFI effort, the\ncompany has secured approximately $2 million in USAF SBIR/STTR funding to mature its adaptive\noptical C-UAS technologies.\nIntellectual Property. Immobileyes holds two issued patents (U.S. Pat 11,519,701 and 11,686,560 (Light\nShield Device) and Patent App. WO2025122941A1(Multi-Zone Multi-Target System), covering adaptive\noptical countermeasure technologies that extend the Navy''s foundational seeker-confusion inventions\n(U.S. Patents 8,305,252 and 8,212,709) were made available through the Department of War’s Defense\nPatent Holiday initiative, a technology transition program that provides qualified companies with no-fee\nCommercial Evaluation Licenses to evaluate and commercialize selected DoW laboratory inventionsand\nform the technical basis for the proposed STORM/DEXTER architecture. The foundational patents\nsupporting this effort\nAlphaMicron, Inc. (Strategic Partner – DEXTER / Liquid-Crystal Optics). Partner since 2020 (($600K\nin-kind + $1.6M matching), AlphaMicron is a recognized leader in liquid crystal electro-optics with\ndecades of experience developing advanced optical technologies for the USAF, Army, and Navy through\nnumerous R&D and technology maturation contracts. Its electronically switchable LC product is the first\nof its kind to be included into the U.S. Army''s APEL (Authorized Protective Eyewear List) program,\ndemonstrating its ability to mature laboratory innovations into fielded military products. Through this\npartnership, AlphaMicron provides the DEXTER™ adaptive beam-routing technology, including its\nliquid-crystal polarization gratings and software-controlled optical switching architecture, and supports\nprototype development, manufacturing, and transition to production. AlphaMicron also maintains a long-\nstanding relationship with the AFRL Functional Materials Division, providing additional expertise in\nadvanced electro-optic materials and device development.\nPerceptView (Partner) combines decades of machine-vision and target-tracking expertise with ten years\nof experience building and delivering end-to-end counter-UAS solutions. Its modular systems integrate\nEO/IR cameras, automated target tracking, radar, Remote ID, ADS-B, Cursor-on-Target and other\nsensors, with APIs for connection to external command-and-control platforms and effectors.\n1.4 Defense Need\nEnd users are NAVAIR and NAVSEA C-UAS for Naval installations, Naval aircraft, and ships, plus\nUSMC and Joint-Force expeditionary air defense. The most reasonable near-term use case is fixed and\nexpeditionary point defense of high-value assets against Group 1–2 EO-guided FPV fiber drones that\ntoday''s RF/GPS-centric C-UAS cannot engage. The system is small enough to be mounted to a fast boat\nor HMMWV for SEAL, and other special operations AND CAN be scaled and modularized to support\nfull ship or Naval base defense and denial of drone threats."},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:46:45.344Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:46:45.344Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"17be1cc3-db2a-4d42-a580-976384a9d225","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 7 of 10 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:46:45.344Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:46:45.344Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"1791c749-4e89-4bf9-9cee-d01460ceafce","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Proposal Number: N26BX-NP002-0450\nOpen Topic Number: \tDON26BX03-NP002 \tImmobileyes, Inc.\nPage 8 of 10\nConcept of Employment\nGHOST deploys as a modular effector cued by existing Navy sensors over MOSA interfaces, in three\nprimary modes: (1) fixed-site installation defense of magazines, flight lines, and command-and-control\nnodes; (2) shipboard self-defense against EO-guided threats in the littorals; and (3) expeditionary or\nvehicle-mounted defense of maneuvering forces. In every mode the graduated escalation framework lets\nthe operator apply the minimum necessary effect (warn, dazzle, or deny) preserving rules-of-engagement\nlatitude and minimizing collateral and airspace risk.\nDEXTER enables a single laser source to be electronically routed and distributed across multiple targets,\nallowing one STORM effector to engage successive or simultaneous EO-guided FPV drones without the\nreload or magazine limitations of kinetic systems. By targeting the drone''s optical guidance rather than\nthe airframe, STORM offers a scalable, low-SWaP countermeasure optimized for emerging fiber-optic\nFPV threats. Open MOSA interfaces allow the system to integrate with existing Navy sensors and combat\nsystems, minimizing integration risk and accelerating transition.\n2.0 Key Personnel\nAll proposed Immobileyes personnel are U.S. Citizens.\nName and Title \tEmployer \tQualifications \tForeign\nPerson\n(Y/N)\nRelevant Experience\nAtossa Alavi, CEO\nPrincipal Investigator\nProgram Manager /\nBusiness & IP Lead\nImmobileyes \tMPhil (Neuroscience)\nJD (Case Western);\nUSPTO-admitted;\nN \tProgram management,\nDoD transition, and IP\nstrategy for the\nAlphaMicron/Immobileyes\ntechnology base.\nDr. Bahman Taheri,\nCEO/CTO\nPrincipal Scientist /\nElectro-Optics Lead\nAlphaMicron\n(Existing Partner)\nPhD/MS Physics\n(Oklahoma State); BS\nPhysics (Cal Poly\nSLO)\nN \t120+ patents; PI on Air\nForce, Navy (ONR),\nArmy, DOE, and DARPA\nprograms in liquid-crystal\nelectro-optics, adaptive\nlaser & flash protection;\nSecret clearance.\nDr. Christopher\nLukowski\nOptical Engineer\nImmobileyes \tPhD Optics/Physics\n(University of\nMaryland)\nN \tLiquid-crystal beam-\nsteering, laser sources, and\nEO camera\ncharacterization.\nElectrical / Software\nEngineers\nImmobileyes \tBS/MS Electronic\nEngineering,\nComputer Science\nN \tReal-time detect-track,\ncontrol electronics, and\ndata pipelines for the\nfeasibility campaign.\nDr.Ross Lamm, CEO\nPerceptView Integration\nExpert\nPerceptView\n(Existing Partner)\nPhD (UC Davis)\nspecializes in\ncomputer vision and\npattern recognition.\nN \tSupported organizations\nincluding NASA’s Jet\nPropulsion Laboratory,\nMIT Lincoln Laboratory,\nU.S. Navy / Coast Guard.\nThe Phase I team combines a nationally recognized electro-optics research group with in-house\nengineering and machining staff, all under one roof at the Kent, OH facility. AlphaMicron and\nPerceptView will support Phase I effort by contributing their expertise in adaptive optical beam routing"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:46:45.344Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:46:45.344Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"5052f72a-704f-4525-b09f-3a53716dd197","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 8 of 10 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:46:45.344Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:46:45.344Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"8ff82eba-9d92-483e-aeb0-6c46a986bc07","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Proposal Number: N26BX-NP002-0450\nOpen Topic Number: \tDON26BX03-NP002 \tImmobileyes, Inc.\nPage 9 of 10\nand AI-enabled EO tracking, respectively, and will continue as key partners during Phase II prototype\ndevelopment, system integration, testing, and manufacturing.\nThis depth lets the team run the Phase I laboratory campaign without new hires or subcontracted labor\nand provides direct continuity into the Option phase benchtop prototype, the Phase II full operational\nprototype, and the Phase III translational and fieldable solution for the Navy.\nRestriction on performance by foreign persons: this topic is ITAR-restricted (22 CFR 120–130). No\nforeign nationals are proposed to perform on this effort; all personnel are U.S. Citizens.\n3.0 Commercialization / Transition Plan Summary\nDoW transition targets NAVAIR and NAVSEA (installation and ship C-UAS), the USMC, Army\nRCCTO, and Air Force Security Forces, building on the established Tinker AFB 72d SFS relationship.\nNon-DoW markets include critical-infrastructure and homeland defense, airports, nuclear facilities, and\nborder security, where non-kinetic, FAA-aware defeat is essential.\nTransition Path and Timeline\nPhase I establishes feasibility; Phase II delivers a TRL-6 prototype for a Navy operational\ndemonstration, building directly on the TRL-5 STORM baseline and the ~$2M of non-dilutive USAF\nSBIR/STTR investment already made in the underlying technology; Phase III targets production effectors\nand integration onto Navy C-UAS programs of record. The established AF Security Forces relationships\nwill be leveraged to provide operational and security guidance, as needed, to ensure the system is\ndemonstratable and fieldable by the Navy.\nCombined with NAVAIR and NAVSEA transition sponsors identified in Task 6, de-risks the path from\nprototype to record program. Immobileyes'' AlphaMicron partnership (US-based LC manufacturing) and\nPreceptive partnership (US-based sensor integration /manufacturing) provide a scalable manufacturing\nand integration base.\nTo accelerate technology maturation, manufacturing readiness, and transition to operational use,\nImmobileyes has established strategic partnerships with the following organizations:\nYoungstown Innovation Hub (YIH)- a regional defense-innovation\nand technology-commercialization organization with the mission of\naccelerating maturation of advanced manufacturing and dual-use\ntechnologies by providing technology maturation, customer\ndiscovery, business development, and transition support that connects\ninnovative companies with DoW stakeholders and industry partners.\nNational Center for Defense Manufacturing and Machining (NCDMM) - a national nonprofit\ndedicated to strengthening the U.S. defense industrial base through\nadvanced manufacturing, digital engineering, technology transition,\nsupply chain development, and manufacturing readiness programs\nsupporting the Department of Defense.\nOhio Defense Innovation OnRamp Hub (ODIH)- a DOW-\nsupported innovation hub that connects nontraditional defense\ncompanies with military customers, prime contractors, investors, and\nfederal funding opportunities, accelerating technology transition through customer discovery, pilot\nprograms, and partnerships across the defense innovation ecosystem..\nBeyond Navy applications, the common STORM/DEXTER architecture is equally applicable to Army\nand Air Force missions, supporting layered counter-UAS protection of military installations,\nexpeditionary bases, ports, airfields, critical infrastructure, energy facilities, and commercial maritime"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:46:45.344Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:46:45.344Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"3a535f41-7c2b-4b38-8f0c-17cc1a47d81d","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 9 of 10 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:46:45.344Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:46:45.344Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"5e5631b1-8189-4d44-b475-0f5757165aad","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Proposal Number: N26BX-NP002-0450\nOpen Topic Number: \tDON26BX03-NP002 \tImmobileyes, Inc.\nPage 10 of 10\nassets. This broad dual-use applicability enables manufacturing investments to be leveraged across\nmultiple defense and commercial markets, reducing production costs while accelerating transition and\ncommercialization.\n4.0 Facilities / Equipment\nImmobileyes shares its facilities with AlphaMicron, an ISO 9001-certified manufacturer headquartered\nin a 30,000-square-foot facility in Kent, Ohio. The facility houses seven specialized laboratories,\nincluding two electro-optics and laser laboratories, liquid-crystal fabrication clean rooms, precision\noptical assembly areas, a MIL-STD environmental test suite, electronics integration and rapid-prototyping\ncapabilities, in-house 3D printing for accelerated prototype development and system integration.\nThe facility maintains an active DoD Facility Clearance (FCL), implements NIST SP 800-171 security\ncontrols, has completed a CMMC Level 2 self-assessment, and maintains OSHA-compliant laser safety\nprocedures.\nThe laser labs feature a wide variety of continuous wave and pulsed lasers, spectrometers and control\nelectronics, amplifiers, wave form generators and detectors. The laser team, led by Dr. Bahman Taheri,\nhas expertise in optics, photonics, and two decades of research on Laser Eye-Protection.\nThe Immobileyes / AlphaMicron scientific team is composed of\ndistinguished cross-disciplinary optics and liquid crystal physicists,\nchemists and engineers with diverse scientific and technical\nbackgrounds, collaborating to solve specific light related problems in\nnew ways. Immobileyes CTO Dr. Bahman Taheri has 30+ years in the\nfields of laser optics and liquid crystal research and bring great depth of\nknowledge to help develop new and innovative devices with new\napplications..\nPerceptView: An Idaho-based designer and manufacturer of advanced\nelectro-optical and infrared (EO/IR) imaging systems with extensive\nexperience supporting defense, aerospace, and homeland security\napplications. The company develops rugged camera payloads, stabilized\npan-tilt systems, embedded vision processing, and\nintelligent tracking solutions for government customers.\nIts U.S. engineering and manufacturing facility provides\nmechanical, electrical, software, and optical integration;\nrapid prototyping; environmental and field testing; and\nlow-rate production, providing STORM with a secure,\ndomestic supply chain and an established path from\nprototype to deployable systems.\nTogether, the two companies provide an end-to-end,\nentirely U.S.-based capability spanning optical\ncomponent fabrication, laser integration, system\nassembly, environmental testing, and production,\nsupporting a secure domestic supply chain.\nFigure 7. Immobileyes electro-optics/laser lab, 3D printing suite and LC manufacturing clean rooms\nLaser Lab\nFig 8: AlphaMicron MIL-\nSpec optical testing\nFigure 9. PerceptView\nfacilities and deployed\nsystems in Fly-Away\nTransport ready for\npalletized or checked luggage"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:46:45.344Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:46:45.344Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"806cedb7-4ecb-430e-bc01-0085b772b5ad","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 10 of 10 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:46:45.344Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:46:45.344Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true}]'::jsonb, 'Technical volume (Volume 2 sidecar) · Uploaded pdf · 5 objects · N26BX-NP002 GHOST — Navy SBIR Phase I (DSIP package)', 4197, 31748, '{}'::jsonb, 'approved', 0.5, 'pending', 0.5, 0, 'upload', '684d8917-6a86-4eeb-806d-4db1cd3a655f'::uuid, NULL, NULL, '686842de-ae88-44ac-b724-8e550f079e3e'::uuid, 'tenant', '2026-08-18T22:46:45.352Z'::timestamptz, '2026-08-18T22:46:45.352Z'::timestamptz, 'admin', uid, NULL, NULL)
