  VALUES ('eb2e8560-af03-4c09-8e2c-b906c89e1807'::uuid, tid, 'primitive', 'Proposal Number: N26BX-NP002-0450
Open Topic Number: 	DON26B', 'Proposal Number: N26BX-NP002-0450
Open Topic Number: 	DON26BX03-NP002 	Immobileyes, Inc.
Page 1 of 10
Guided Hostile Optical Sensor-hijacking (GHOST: Adaptive Optical Countermeasures for
EO-Guided Fiber-Optic FPV Drones
1.0 Description of Proposed Phase I Technical Effort
Problem and Significance
First-person-view (FPV) fiber-optic drones have become one of the
most difficult unmanned-air-system (UAS) threats on the modern
battlefield. Unlike conventional FPV systems, these aircraft require
no radiofrequency (RF) link, are immune to RF jamming, and operate
in GPS-denied environments—relying almost entirely on continuous
electro-optical (EO) imagery for terminal guidance. Fielded at scale
in Ukraine and by non-state actors, they defeat the RF-centric and
GPS-centric counter-UAS (C-UAS) systems the Navy fields today.
The operational consequence is stark. RF-based detection and defeat
(jammers, spoofers, high-power microwave) have no emission to
exploit; GPS denial has no effect on a vehicle flown by wire over
fiber; and kinetic interceptors are cost-prohibitive and collaterally
constrained against a sub-$1,000 airframe. A single fiber drone can
be produced for a few hundred dollars yet forces the defender to
expend a far costlier effect—an unfavorable cost-exchange and
magazine-depth problem that is most acute at the exact moment of a
saturating swarm attack against a Naval installation, aircraft on the
ramp, or a ship in the littorals.
Existing C-UAS remains optimized for RF disruption or kinetic
defeat. Existing shipboard laser systems are effective on larger
platforms but typically require significant size, weight, power,
cooling, and integration resources, limiting their suitability for
smaller, agile vessels. There remains an operational gap for low-
SWaP, scalable, non-kinetic laser countermeasure systems capable of
detecting, tracking, identifying, and defeating both individual and
swarming UAS across diverse maritime environments.
Technical Innovation: Guided Hostile Optical Sensor-hijacking
Immobileyes proposes a software-defined counter-UAS architecture
integrating two Immobileyes technologies:
● STORM™ (Smart Tracking & Optical Response Mitigation)
is an AI-enabled, MOSA-based counter-UAS system that combines multi-sensor detection, precision
tracking, and adaptive optical countermeasures. Through its partnership with PerceptView, a provider of
EO/IR tracking and C2 solutions, STORM is sensor-agnostic by design, integrating EO/IR, radar, RF,
and other detection sources.
● 	Immobileyes has already developed and demonstrated a STORM™ prototype (Fig. 2) integrating
an optical warn/dazzle payload with a PTZ EO/IR camera, enabling continuous target detection,
tracking, and engagement. During an FAA-authorized operational demonstration for the 72d Security
Forces Squadron at Tinker AFB, the system achieved Warn (>500 m) and Dazzle (300–350 m)
optical effects against representative UAS EO cameras while maintaining continuous target tracking,
substantially reducing technical risk for the proposed effort.(Fig. 3)
Figure 1. Fiber-optic FPV drones with
no RF link, immune to jamming, now
proliferating in current conflicts.
Figure 2. Current AF system: STORM
integrated with PerceptView EO Tracking -- 1 of 10 -- Proposal Number: N26BX-NP002-0450
Open Topic Number: 	DON26BX03-NP002 	Immobileyes, Inc.
Page 2 of 10
STORM employs a graduated, human-in-the-loop optical
response tailored to the threat (Fig. 4). Zone 1
(Warn/Deter) uses low-level visible illumination to
signal presence and encourage compliance. Zone 2
(Dazzle/Disrupt) degrades the drone''s electro-optical
camera, reducing image quality and ISR effectiveness.
Zone 3 (Deny/Damage) concentrates optical energy to
designate targets or disable optical sensors, when
authorized, providing a scalable, non-kinetic response
against EO-guided UAS.
● 	DEXTER™ (Directed-Energy Beam RouTER) is a
low-SWaP adaptive beam-routing architecture developed by
Immobileyes in collaboration with its strategic partner,
AlphaMicron, Inc. (AMI), leveraging AMI''s decades of
expertise in defense-grade liquid crystal (LC) optical devices
for military and aerospace applications. Using adaptive LC
polarization gratings, DEXTER electronically routes, splits,
combines, shapes, and redirects laser energy with no moving
parts. The software-defined optical engine dynamically
switches a single laser beam among multiple output channels,
or combines multiple laser inputs into a single output, in
milliseconds, enabling simultaneous engagement of multiple
drones, rapid beam steering, and scalable swarm defense at
speeds unattainable with conventional mechanically steered optical systems.
● 	Integration: Immobileyes has partnered with PerceptView to provide a mature, sensor-agnostic
integration architecture (Fig. 5). STORM fuses inputs from RF, radar, EO/IR, and other detection
systems and can operate either as a standalone C2 platform or as an integrated effector within existing
Navy and DoW command-and-control architectures, including MEDUSA..
Optical Seeker-Confusion-Overwhelm Mechanism
The effort updates and refines the Navy''s internal research
and development of IR guided seeker-confusion principle
(U.S. Pat. 8,305,252 and 8,212,709). The Navy
demonstrated that a continuous-wave optical source placed
within an imaging seeker''s field of view generates a
localized high-intensity region that the tracking algorithm
interprets as a feature or target; the tracker''s centroid or
Figure 3. Drone camera field of view before
and after STORM laser engagement at 400 m,
demonstrating effective sensor dazzle.
Figure 5. PerceptView EX-SUADS
Expeditionary C-UAS + C2 Sensor
Figure 4. GHOST Graduated Escalation Framework
Figure 6. Optical effects to disrupt EO-
guided drone targeting with DEXTER -- 2 of 10 --', '[{"id":"4d357180-7327-458c-bc57-58c62f60dc47","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Proposal Number: N26BX-NP002-0450\nOpen Topic Number: \tDON26BX03-NP002 \tImmobileyes, Inc.\nPage 1 of 10\nGuided Hostile Optical Sensor-hijacking (GHOST: Adaptive Optical Countermeasures for\nEO-Guided Fiber-Optic FPV Drones\n1.0 Description of Proposed Phase I Technical Effort\nProblem and Significance\nFirst-person-view (FPV) fiber-optic drones have become one of the\nmost difficult unmanned-air-system (UAS) threats on the modern\nbattlefield. Unlike conventional FPV systems, these aircraft require\nno radiofrequency (RF) link, are immune to RF jamming, and operate\nin GPS-denied environments—relying almost entirely on continuous\nelectro-optical (EO) imagery for terminal guidance. Fielded at scale\nin Ukraine and by non-state actors, they defeat the RF-centric and\nGPS-centric counter-UAS (C-UAS) systems the Navy fields today.\nThe operational consequence is stark. RF-based detection and defeat\n(jammers, spoofers, high-power microwave) have no emission to\nexploit; GPS denial has no effect on a vehicle flown by wire over\nfiber; and kinetic interceptors are cost-prohibitive and collaterally\nconstrained against a sub-$1,000 airframe. A single fiber drone can\nbe produced for a few hundred dollars yet forces the defender to\nexpend a far costlier effect—an unfavorable cost-exchange and\nmagazine-depth problem that is most acute at the exact moment of a\nsaturating swarm attack against a Naval installation, aircraft on the\nramp, or a ship in the littorals.\nExisting C-UAS remains optimized for RF disruption or kinetic\ndefeat. Existing shipboard laser systems are effective on larger\nplatforms but typically require significant size, weight, power,\ncooling, and integration resources, limiting their suitability for\nsmaller, agile vessels. There remains an operational gap for low-\nSWaP, scalable, non-kinetic laser countermeasure systems capable of\ndetecting, tracking, identifying, and defeating both individual and\nswarming UAS across diverse maritime environments.\nTechnical Innovation: Guided Hostile Optical Sensor-hijacking\nImmobileyes proposes a software-defined counter-UAS architecture\nintegrating two Immobileyes technologies:\n● STORM™ (Smart Tracking & Optical Response Mitigation)\nis an AI-enabled, MOSA-based counter-UAS system that combines multi-sensor detection, precision\ntracking, and adaptive optical countermeasures. Through its partnership with PerceptView, a provider of\nEO/IR tracking and C2 solutions, STORM is sensor-agnostic by design, integrating EO/IR, radar, RF,\nand other detection sources.\n● \tImmobileyes has already developed and demonstrated a STORM™ prototype (Fig. 2) integrating\nan optical warn/dazzle payload with a PTZ EO/IR camera, enabling continuous target detection,\ntracking, and engagement. During an FAA-authorized operational demonstration for the 72d Security\nForces Squadron at Tinker AFB, the system achieved Warn (>500 m) and Dazzle (300–350 m)\noptical effects against representative UAS EO cameras while maintaining continuous target tracking,\nsubstantially reducing technical risk for the proposed effort.(Fig. 3)\nFigure 1. Fiber-optic FPV drones with\nno RF link, immune to jamming, now\nproliferating in current conflicts.\nFigure 2. Current AF system: STORM\nintegrated with PerceptView EO Tracking"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:46:45.344Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:46:45.344Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"f6f9b564-0c03-47de-944d-edc9c3754109","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 1 of 10 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:46:45.344Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:46:45.344Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"342db173-6c88-40f8-886a-5a91e6814c94","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Proposal Number: N26BX-NP002-0450\nOpen Topic Number: \tDON26BX03-NP002 \tImmobileyes, Inc.\nPage 2 of 10\nSTORM employs a graduated, human-in-the-loop optical\nresponse tailored to the threat (Fig. 4). Zone 1\n(Warn/Deter) uses low-level visible illumination to\nsignal presence and encourage compliance. Zone 2\n(Dazzle/Disrupt) degrades the drone''s electro-optical\ncamera, reducing image quality and ISR effectiveness.\nZone 3 (Deny/Damage) concentrates optical energy to\ndesignate targets or disable optical sensors, when\nauthorized, providing a scalable, non-kinetic response\nagainst EO-guided UAS.\n● \tDEXTER™ (Directed-Energy Beam RouTER) is a\nlow-SWaP adaptive beam-routing architecture developed by\nImmobileyes in collaboration with its strategic partner,\nAlphaMicron, Inc. (AMI), leveraging AMI''s decades of\nexpertise in defense-grade liquid crystal (LC) optical devices\nfor military and aerospace applications. Using adaptive LC\npolarization gratings, DEXTER electronically routes, splits,\ncombines, shapes, and redirects laser energy with no moving\nparts. The software-defined optical engine dynamically\nswitches a single laser beam among multiple output channels,\nor combines multiple laser inputs into a single output, in\nmilliseconds, enabling simultaneous engagement of multiple\ndrones, rapid beam steering, and scalable swarm defense at\nspeeds unattainable with conventional mechanically steered optical systems.\n● \tIntegration: Immobileyes has partnered with PerceptView to provide a mature, sensor-agnostic\nintegration architecture (Fig. 5). STORM fuses inputs from RF, radar, EO/IR, and other detection\nsystems and can operate either as a standalone C2 platform or as an integrated effector within existing\nNavy and DoW command-and-control architectures, including MEDUSA..\nOptical Seeker-Confusion-Overwhelm Mechanism\nThe effort updates and refines the Navy''s internal research\nand development of IR guided seeker-confusion principle\n(U.S. Pat. 8,305,252 and 8,212,709). The Navy\ndemonstrated that a continuous-wave optical source placed\nwithin an imaging seeker''s field of view generates a\nlocalized high-intensity region that the tracking algorithm\ninterprets as a feature or target; the tracker''s centroid or\nFigure 3. Drone camera field of view before\nand after STORM laser engagement at 400 m,\ndemonstrating effective sensor dazzle.\nFigure 5. PerceptView EX-SUADS\nExpeditionary C-UAS + C2 Sensor\nFigure 4. GHOST Graduated Escalation Framework\nFigure 6. Optical effects to disrupt EO-\nguided drone targeting with DEXTER"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:46:45.344Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:46:45.344Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"add88967-8600-4c59-9314-214005734602","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 2 of 10 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:46:45.344Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:46:45.344Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true}]'::jsonb, NULL, 787, 5814, '{}'::jsonb, 'approved', 0.5, 'pending', 0.5, 0, 'upload', '684d8917-6a86-4eeb-806d-4db1cd3a655f'::uuid, NULL, NULL, '686842de-ae88-44ac-b724-8e550f079e3e'::uuid, 'tenant', '2026-08-18T22:46:45.369Z'::timestamptz, '2026-08-18T22:46:45.369Z'::timestamptz, 'admin', uid, '[{"blockIds":["b0"],"sourceAtomId":"60fb833a-2e35-4bda-9770-0072df73df53"}]'::jsonb, NULL)
