  VALUES ('67c5e58d-d348-408b-996c-5637711d7ea3'::uuid, tid, 'primitive', 'Proposal Number: N26BX-NP002-0450
Open Topic Number: 	DON26B', 'Proposal Number: N26BX-NP002-0450
Open Topic Number: 	DON26BX03-NP002 	Immobileyes, Inc.
Page 3 of 10
feature-match then migrates toward the induced source, steering the vehicle away from the protected as
set. Modern EO-guided FPV fiber drones rely on the same class of feature-tracking and optical-flow
guidance running on uncooled CMOS/CCD cameras. Against those sensors, controlled illumination
produces image saturation, blooming, false high-intensity features, contrast collapse, and machine-vision
confusion (Fig. 6), thus degrading or breaking terminal lock without physical destruction.
The proposed effort investigates the wavelength, dwell, polarization, and beam-geometry parameters that
maximize this effect across representative EO camera types and characterizes the transition from
reversible dazzle (Zone 2) to persistent denial (Zone 3) as a function of delivered irradiance, giving the
operator a calibrated, proportional response rather than a single binary effect.
DEXTER Adaptive Beam Routing
The enabling technology is DEXTER, built on AlphaMicron''s adaptive polarization gratings
(Pancharatnam–Berry-phase optics), which electronically switch laser beams between optical paths under
low-voltage control, with no moving parts. Cascading these elements creates a software-defined optical
router that electronically routes, splits, combines, and steers laser beams, enabling rapid, reversible
transitions from a single focused beam to a multi-beam "shotgun" configuration capable of engaging
multiple targets simultaneously (Fig. 7). This replaces heavy, slow, failure-prone gimbaled mirror trains
with a low-SWaP, solid-state, software-defined aperture, and enables a distributed architecture in which
multiple beam directors share one laser source across fixed, mobile, shipboard, and expeditionary
platforms.
GHOST is therefore not a fixed dazzler but a software-defined effector: wavelength real time; a single
sub-kilowatt laser source is electronically allocated across multiple beam directors for multi-target and
swarm engagement without duplicating laser hardware.
System Snapshot — Key Attributes
Attribute 	GHOST / STORM–DEXTER
Laser output 	< 1 kW total, software-allocated across beams
Effective range 	Burnout > 500m; Dazzle > 1 km; Warning > 3 km
Wavelengths 	Multi-wavelength — green / blue (450 nm) + IR
Beam routing 	DEXTER liquid crystal grating: 1 → 	tens–hundreds of beams, millisecond
switching; NO MOVING PARTS
Cueing / integration 	Detection-agnostic MOSA — EO/IR, radar, RF/EW, TSPI – PROVEN AT
TINKER AFB
Engagement doctrine 	Graduated W arn/Deter → Dazzle/Disrupt → Deny/Damage; human-in-
the-loop or autonomous
Maturity 	Builds on TRL-5 AF STTR + TACFI prototype lineage
Figure 7. DEXTER splits a single laser into multiple beams using a low-profile, non-mechanical LC optical element. -- 3 of 10 -- Proposal Number: N26BX-NP002-0450
Open Topic Number: 	DON26BX03-NP002 	Immobileyes, Inc.
Page 4 of 10
1.1 Phase I Technical Objectives
Phase I will establish the technical feasibility of the STORM/DEXTER optical counter-UAS approach
against EO-guided Group 1–2 UAS. Specific objectives include the following:
● Objective 1: Demonstrate technical feasibility. Evaluate extending the Navy''s previously researched
seeker-confusion concept from IR seekers to EO-guided fiber-optic drones; characterize the
susceptibility of representative EO cameras to optical perturbation.
● Objective 2: Define the STORM/DEXTER system architecture and integration approach for Navy C-
UAS applications, including interface requirements, MOSA compatibility, and the hardware and
software modifications necessary to support a shipboard Phase II prototype.
● Objective 3: Quantify performance improvements. Model and measure improvements over existing
non-kinetic C-UAS: tracking disruption, multi-target engagement, reduced SWaP, elimination of
mechanical beam steering, and effectiveness against RF-jamming-resistant fiber-optic drones.
● Objective 4: Develop a Phase II transition plan. Define the roadmap for a prototype able to recognize,
track, and non-kinetically defeat <Group 2 UAS and identify NAVAIR/NAVSEA transition.
Phase I Feasibility Metrics (Go / No-Go)
Metric 	Phase I Target 	Go / No-Go Threshold
EO-camera susceptibility
(measurable degradation of
terminal tracking)
Demonstrated on ≥ 2 representative
EO/FPV cameras
≥ 1 camera
Optical effect 	Repeatable saturation / blooming at
bench range
Qualitative confirmation
DEXTER multi-beam routing 	≥ 3 simultaneously addressable output
channels (model/bench)
≥ 2 channels
Predicted SWaP vs. mechanically
steered baseline
≥ 30% reduction (analysis) 	Net reduction
Architecture 	STORM↔DEXTER interface + Navy
C-UAS integration defined
Interface defined
Anticipated Phase I Outcomes
At the conclusion of Phase I, the Immobileyes team will have delivered:
● 	Demonstrated feasibility of applying the Navy seeker-confusion principle to EO-guided FPV
fiber-optic drones, evidenced by measured optical degradation of EO cameras.
● 	A defined preliminary STORM/DEXTER architecture for Navy C - UAS applications, with
the STORMڸ DEXTER interface and MOSA specification.
● 	Identification of the hardware and software modifications required for an Option Phase benchtop
demonstration and a full Phase II developed prototype.
● 	Quantified improvements over existing non-kinetic C-UAS system under development by
Immobileyes and AlphaMicron: multi-target engagement, reduced SWaP, elimination of
mechanical beam steering, and effectiveness against visual nav or fiber-optic drones.
● 	An established technical baseline and transition plan for a for Phase II developed prototype with a
full demonstration for NAVAIR/NAVSEA personnel.
Phase I Deliverables:
● 	Kick-Off Briefing -- 4 of 10 --', '[{"id":"a9352d1f-5b9d-4f26-acf7-171a32b1343c","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Proposal Number: N26BX-NP002-0450\nOpen Topic Number: \tDON26BX03-NP002 \tImmobileyes, Inc.\nPage 3 of 10\nfeature-match then migrates toward the induced source, steering the vehicle away from the protected as\nset. Modern EO-guided FPV fiber drones rely on the same class of feature-tracking and optical-flow\nguidance running on uncooled CMOS/CCD cameras. Against those sensors, controlled illumination\nproduces image saturation, blooming, false high-intensity features, contrast collapse, and machine-vision\nconfusion (Fig. 6), thus degrading or breaking terminal lock without physical destruction.\nThe proposed effort investigates the wavelength, dwell, polarization, and beam-geometry parameters that\nmaximize this effect across representative EO camera types and characterizes the transition from\nreversible dazzle (Zone 2) to persistent denial (Zone 3) as a function of delivered irradiance, giving the\noperator a calibrated, proportional response rather than a single binary effect.\nDEXTER Adaptive Beam Routing\nThe enabling technology is DEXTER, built on AlphaMicron''s adaptive polarization gratings\n(Pancharatnam–Berry-phase optics), which electronically switch laser beams between optical paths under\nlow-voltage control, with no moving parts. Cascading these elements creates a software-defined optical\nrouter that electronically routes, splits, combines, and steers laser beams, enabling rapid, reversible\ntransitions from a single focused beam to a multi-beam \"shotgun\" configuration capable of engaging\nmultiple targets simultaneously (Fig. 7). This replaces heavy, slow, failure-prone gimbaled mirror trains\nwith a low-SWaP, solid-state, software-defined aperture, and enables a distributed architecture in which\nmultiple beam directors share one laser source across fixed, mobile, shipboard, and expeditionary\nplatforms.\nGHOST is