  VALUES ('9eb9ad60-661a-4fa6-bc1a-cac36a7fb9b9'::uuid, tid, 'primitive', 'Proposal Number: N26BX-NP002-0450
Open Topic Number: 	DON26B', 'Proposal Number: N26BX-NP002-0450
Open Topic Number: 	DON26BX03-NP002 	Immobileyes, Inc.
Page 5 of 10
● 	Progress Report
● 	Final Technical Report documenting the STORM and DEXTER baseline, the Navy C-UAS
capability gaps addressed, required modifications, the feasibility assessment, and expected
performance improvements.
● 	Initial Phase II Proposal and initial transition and commercialization framework.
1.2 Phase I (Base and Option) Statement of Work
The Base effort (6 months) establishes feasibility and system architecture. The Option (6 months), funded
separately, furthers the effort toward Phase II with a breadboard optical-effects demonstration and a
prototype/transition package. All work is performed at Immobileyes'' Kent, OH facility; no foreign
nationals perform on this ITAR-restricted effort.
Task No. 	Title and Description 	Months 	Performer and
Deliverable
1 (Base) 	System Definition & Feasibility Assessment: Assess
the STORM/DEXTER architecture; review Group 1–2
UAS threats, EO cameras, and current Navy C-UAS to
define requirements and gaps.
1 	Immobileyes - Kick-Off
Briefing
2 (Base) 	System Architecture & Design: Preliminary
architecture integrating STORM detect-track with
DEXTER beam routing; STORM/DEXTER interface;
optical-engagement concept; control architecture.
1–3 	Immobileyes + AMI -
Progress Report
3 (Base) 	Optical Countermeasure Feasibility Evaluation:
Laboratory test and modeling of saturation, blooming,
false-feature generation, and contrast degradation
against representative EO cameras; compare vs.
existing non-kinetic C-UAS.
2–5 	Immobileyes - Test Data
& Analysis Report
4 (Base) 	Transition Assessment & Phase I Reporting:
consolidate results; document performance
improvements and residual risk.
5–6 	Immobileyes - Final
Technical Report; Option
Period and Initial Phase II
Proposal
5 (Option) 	Maritime Platform Integration and Motion Analysis:
Evaluate deployment on Navy platforms, define
requirements for stabilization, inertial compensation,
and predictive tracking
7–8 	Immobileyes – Analysis &
Technical Report
6 (Option) 	Closed-Loop Tracking and Breadboard Demo:
Integrating STORM detection/tracking with DEXTER
beam routing, Validate tracking performance, software-
control and optical effects.
8-11 	Immobileyes +AMI -
Breadboard Demo &
Performance Data
Analysis and Report
7 (Option) 	Phase II Prototype Definition & NAVAIR/NAVSEA
Transition Package: prototype design baseline, test
plan, and integration/transition plan for a Navy C-UAS
demonstration.
10–12 	Immobileyes - Prototype
plan & Transition Planning
Package for Phase II
Base Period — Task Approach
Task 1 (Month 1) - System Definition & Feasibility Assessment Establish the Group 1–2 EO-guided
fiber-optic threat set, catalogs representative FPV/EO camera technologies and current Navy C-UAS
capability gaps, and converts them into system requirements, operational use cases, performance metrics,
and evaluation criteria presented at the Kick-Off Briefing. -- 5 of 10 -- Proposal Number: N26BX-NP002-0450
Open Topic Number: 	DON26BX03-NP002 	Immobileyes, Inc.
Page 6 of 10
Task 2 (Months 1–3) - System Architecture & Design Develop the preliminary STORM/DEXTER
architecture, including the STORM↔DEXTER interface, integration with the PerceptView EO tracking
system, the optical engagement concept, MOSA interfaces, and the preliminary software architecture.
Develop engagement algorithms that determine the appropriate optical response based on target range,
aspect angle, camera orientation, closing velocity, observed drone behavior, beam dwell time, and
transitions between Warn, Dazzle, Deny, single-beam, and multi-beam operating modes.
Task 3 (Months 2–5) - Optical Countermeasure Feasibility Evaluation Perform analytical modeling
and laboratory characterization of representative EO cameras to quantify laser-induced saturation,
blooming, false-feature generation, contrast degradation, tracking instability, beam divergence, irradiance,
spot size, wavelength, and engagement geometry. Determine the optical conditions and engagement
envelope required to misguide EO-guided fiber-optic FPV drones while minimizing laser power
requirements for various modalities of operation.
Task 4 (Months 5–6) - Transition Assessment & Phase I Reporting integrate the Phase I analytical,
software, and laboratory results into the Final Technical Report, defines the preliminary Phase II
prototype architecture, verification and validation approach, and manufacturing strategy, and prepares the
Initial Phase II Proposal and NAVAIR/NAVSEA transition plan with manufacturing and commercial
development pathways contemplated.
Option Period — Task Approach
Task 5 (Months 7–8) – Maritime Platform Integration and Motion Analysis
Evaluate deployment of STORM on moving Navy platforms (RHIBs, Combatant Craft Assault,
expeditionary craft, and tactical vehicles). Develop preliminary closed-loop tracking architecture and
assess the effects of platform roll, pitch, yaw, heave, vibration, target maneuvering, and relative motion
on tracking accuracy, laser pointing stability, beam jitter, optical dwell time, and overall countermeasure
effectiveness. Define stabilization, inertial compensation, predictive tracking, and adaptive beam-control
requirements for maritime operation.
Task 6 (Months 8–11) – Closed-Loop Tracking and Breadboard Demonstration
Develop a laboratory breadboard integrating STORM''s AI-enabled detection and tracking with
DEXTER''s adaptive beam-routing engine to demonstrate the complete engagement chain from target
detection through electronic beam routing and measured optical effects on representative EO-guided FPV
cameras. Validate tracking performance, software-controlled beam routing, single- and multi-beam
operation, engagement geometry, and beam stability while verifying the feasibility of the proposed
maritime stabilization and control approach.
Task 7 (Months 10–12) – Phase II Prototype Definition and Transition Planning
Define the Phase II prototype baseline, including the preliminary system design, software and hardware
architecture, verification and validation plan, and performance metrics. Prepare the NAVAIR/NAVSEA
integration package, manufacturing approach, technology transition strategy, and Phase II work plan to
support a Navy C-UAS prototype demonstration.
Key Technical Risks and Mitigations
Technical Risk 	Mitigation
EO-camera diversity limits
generalization of the optical effect
Test a representative camera set; parameterize wavelength, dwell,
and beam geometry via DEXTER software control -- 6 of 10 --', '[{"id":"3710ff24-cb5f-4826-9e10-a100591bb9cc","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Proposal Number: N26BX-NP002-0450\nOpen Topic Number: \tDON26BX03-NP002 \tImmobileyes, Inc.\nPage 5 of 10\n● \tProgress Report\n● \tFinal Technical Report documenting the STORM and DEXTER baseline, the Navy C-UAS\ncapability gaps addressed, required modifications, the feasibility assessment, and expected\nperformance improvements.\n● \tInitial Phase II Proposal and initial transition and commercialization framework.\n1.2 Phase I (Base and Option) Statement of Work\nThe Base effort (6 months) establishes feasibility and system architecture. The Option (6 months), funded\nseparately, furthers the effort toward Phase II with a breadboard optical-effects demonstration and a\nprototype/transition package. All work is performed at Immobileyes'' Kent, OH facility; no foreign\nnationals perform on this ITAR-restricted effort.\nTask No. \tTitle and Description \tMonths \tPerformer and\nDeliverable\n1 (Base) \tSystem Definition & Feasibility Assessment: Assess\nthe STORM/DEXTER architecture; review Group 1–2\nUAS threats, EO cameras, and current Navy C-UAS to\ndefine requirements and gaps.\n1 \tImmobileyes - Kick-Off\nBriefing\n2 (Base) \tSystem Architecture & Design: Preliminary\narchitecture integrating STORM detect-track with\nDEXTER beam routing; STORM/DEXTER interface;\noptical-engagement concept; control architecture.\n1–3 \tImmobileyes + AMI -\nProgress Report\n3 (Base) \tOptical Countermeasure Feasibility Evaluation:\nLaboratory test and modeling of saturation, blooming,\nfalse-feature generation, and contrast degradation\nagainst representative EO cameras; compare vs.\nexisting non-kinetic C-UAS.\n2–5 \tImmobileyes - Test Data\n& Analysis Report\n4 (Base) \tTransition Assessment & Phase I Reporting:\nconsolidate results; document performance\nimprovements and residual risk.\n5–6 \tImmobileyes - Final\nTechnical Report; Option\nPeriod and Initial Phase II\nProposal\n5 (Option) \tMaritime Platform Integration and Motion Analysis:\nEvaluate deployment on Navy platforms, define\nrequirements for stabilization, inertial compensation,\nand predictive tracking\n7–8 \tImmobileyes – Analysis &\nTechnical Report\n6 (Option) \tClosed-Loop Tracking and Breadboard Demo:\nIntegrating STORM detection/tracking with DEXTER\nbeam routing, Validate tracking performance, software-\ncontrol and optical effects.\n8-11 \tImmobileyes +AMI -\nBreadboard Demo &\nPerformance Data\nAnalysis and Report\n7 (Option) \tPhase II Prototype Definition & NAVAIR/NAVSEA\nTransition Package: prototype design baseline, test\nplan, and integration/transition plan for a Navy C-UAS\ndemonstration.\n10–12 \tImmobileyes - Prototype\nplan & Transition Planning\nPackage for Phase II\nBase Period — Task Approach\nTask 1 (Month 1) - System Definition & Feasibility Assessment Establish the Group 1–2 EO-guided\nfiber-optic threat set, catalogs representative FPV/EO camera technologies and current Navy C-UAS\ncapability gaps, and converts them into system requirements, operational use cases, performance metrics,\nand evaluation criteria presented at the Kick-Off Briefing."},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:46:45.344Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:46:45.344Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"d7d6c637-32f4-4e94-b95e-aafab8f6aa7e","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 5 of 10 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:46:45.344Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:46:45.344Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"08285311-be51-4d84-8520-04b82642db96","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Proposal Number: N26BX-NP002-0450\nOpen Topic Number: \tDON26BX03-NP002 \tImmobileyes, Inc.\nPage 6 of 10\nTask 2 (Months 1–3) - System Architecture & Design Develop the preliminary STORM/DEXTER\narchitecture, including the STORM↔DEXTER interface, integration with the PerceptView EO tracking\nsystem, the optical engagement concept, MOSA interfaces, and the preliminary software architecture.\nDevelop engagement algorithms that determine the appropriate optical response based on target range,\naspect angle, camera orientation, closing velocity, observed drone behavior, beam dwell time, and\ntransitions between Warn, Dazzle, Deny, single-beam, and multi-beam operating modes.\nTask 3 (Months 2–5) - Optical Countermeasure Feasibility Evaluation Perform analytical modeling\nand laboratory characterization of representative EO cameras to quantify laser-induced saturation,\nblooming, false-feature generation, contrast degradation, tracking instability, beam divergence, irradiance,\nspot size, wavelength, and engagement geometry. Determine the optical conditions and engagement\nenvelope required to misguide EO-guided fiber-optic FPV drones while minimizing laser power\nrequirements for various modalities of operation.\nTask 4 (Months 5–6) - Transition Assessment & Phase I Reporting integrate the Phase I analytical,\nsoftware, and laboratory results into the Final Technical Report, defines the preliminary Phase II\nprototype architecture, verification and validation approach, and manufacturing strategy, and prepares the\nInitial Phase II Proposal and NAVAIR/NAVSEA transition plan with manufacturing and commercial\ndevelopment pathways contemplated.\nOption Period — Task Approach\nTask 5 (Months 7–8) – Maritime Platform Integration and Motion Analysis\nEvaluate deployment of STORM on moving Navy platforms (RHIBs, Combatant Craft Assault,\nexpeditionary craft, and tactical vehicles). Develop preliminary closed-loop tracking architecture and\nassess the effects of platform roll, pitch, yaw, heave, vibration, target maneuvering, and relative motion\non tracking accuracy, laser pointing stability, beam jitter, optical dwell time, and overall countermeasure\neffectiveness. Define stabilization, inertial compensation, predictive tracking, and adaptive beam-control\nrequirements for maritime operation.\nTask 6 (Months 8–11) – Closed-Loop Tracking and Breadboard Demonstration\nDevelop a laboratory breadboard integrating STORM''s AI-enabled detection and tracking with\nDEXTER''s adaptive beam-routing engine to demonstrate the complete engagement chain from target\ndetection through electronic beam routing and measured optical effects on representative EO-guided FPV\ncameras. Validate tracking performance, software-controlled beam routing, single- and multi-beam\noperation, engagement geometry, and beam stability while verifying the feasibility of the proposed\nmaritime stabilization and control approach.\nTask 7 (Months 10–12) – Phase II Prototype Definition and Transition Planning\nDefine the Phase II prototype baseline, including the preliminary system design, software and hardware\narchitecture, verification and validation plan, and performance metrics. Prepare the NAVAIR/NAVSEA\nintegration package, manufacturing approach, technology transition strategy, and Phase II work plan to\nsupport a Navy C-UAS prototype demonstration.\nKey Technical Risks and Mitigations\nTechnical Risk \tMitigation\nEO-camera diversity limits\ngeneralization of the optical effect\nTest a representative camera set; parameterize wavelength, dwell,\nand beam geometry via DEXTER software control"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:46:45.344Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:46:45.344Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"3c379340-b26d-4f65-bd6a-0adfd684f023","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"-- 6 of 10 --"},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-18T22:46:45.344Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-18T22:46:45.344Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true}]'::jsonb, NULL, 851, 6582, '{}'::jsonb, 'approved', 0.5, 'pending', 0.5, 0, 'upload', '684d8917-6a86-4eeb-806d-4db1cd3a655f'::uuid, NULL, NULL, '686842de-ae88-44ac-b724-8e550f079e3e'::uuid, 'tenant', '2026-08-18T22:46:45.395Z'::timestamptz, '2026-08-18T22:46:45.395Z'::timestamptz, 'admin', uid, '[{"blockIds":["b2"],"sourceAtomId":"60fb833a-2e35-4bda-9770-0072df73df53"}]'::jsonb, NULL)
