  VALUES ('ff730ec0-36fc-4b42-ad08-bc88ce9622ee'::uuid, tid, 'primitive', 'Proposal Number: N26BX-NP002-0450
Open Topic Number: 	DON26B', 'Proposal Number: N26BX-NP002-0450
Open Topic Number: 	DON26BX03-NP002 	Immobileyes, Inc.
Page 9 of 10
and AI-enabled EO tracking, respectively, and will continue as key partners during Phase II prototype
development, system integration, testing, and manufacturing.
This depth lets the team run the Phase I laboratory campaign without new hires or subcontracted labor
and provides direct continuity into the Option phase benchtop prototype, the Phase II full operational
prototype, and the Phase III translational and fieldable solution for the Navy.
Restriction on performance by foreign persons: this topic is ITAR-restricted (22 CFR 120–130). No
foreign nationals are proposed to perform on this effort; all personnel are U.S. Citizens.
3.0 Commercialization / Transition Plan Summary
DoW transition targets NAVAIR and NAVSEA (installation and ship C-UAS), the USMC, Army
RCCTO, and Air Force Security Forces, building on the established Tinker AFB 72d SFS relationship.
Non-DoW markets include critical-infrastructure and homeland defense, airports, nuclear facilities, and
border security, where non-kinetic, FAA-aware defeat is essential.
Transition Path and Timeline
Phase I establishes feasibility; Phase II delivers a TRL-6 prototype for a Navy operational
demonstration, building directly on the TRL-5 STORM baseline and the ~$2M of non-dilutive USAF
SBIR/STTR investment already made in the underlying technology; Phase III targets production effectors
and integration onto Navy C-UAS programs of record. The established AF Security Forces relationships
will be leveraged to provide operational and security guidance, as needed, to ensure the system is
demonstratable and fieldable by the Navy.
Combined with NAVAIR and NAVSEA transition sponsors identified in Task 6, de-risks the path from
prototype to record program. Immobileyes'' AlphaMicron partnership (US-based LC manufacturing) and
Preceptive partnership (US-based sensor integration /manufacturing) provide a scalable manufacturing
and integration base.
To accelerate technology maturation, manufacturing readiness, and transition to operational use,
Immobileyes has established strategic partnerships with the following organizations:
Youngstown Innovation Hub (YIH)- a regional defense-innovation
and technology-commercialization organization with the mission of
accelerating maturation of advanced manufacturing and dual-use
technologies by providing technology maturation, customer
discovery, business development, and transition support that connects
innovative companies with DoW stakeholders and industry partners.
National Center for Defense Manufacturing and Machining (NCDMM) - a national nonprofit
dedicated to strengthening the U.S. defense industrial base through
advanced manufacturing, digital engineering, technology transition,
supply chain development, and manufacturing readiness programs
supporting the Department of Defense.
Ohio Defense Innovation OnRamp Hub (ODIH)- a DOW-
supported innovation hub that connects nontraditional defense
companies with military customers, prime contractors, investors, and
federal funding opportunities, accelerating technology transition through customer discovery, pilot
programs, and partnerships across the defense innovation ecosystem..
Beyond Navy applications, the common STORM/DEXTER architecture is equally applicable to Army
and Air Force missions, supporting layered counter-UAS protection of military installations,
expeditionary bases, ports, airfields, critical infrastructure, energy facilities, and commercial maritime -- 9 of 10 -- Proposal Number: N26BX-NP002-0450
Open Topic Number: 	DON26BX03-NP002 	Immobileyes, Inc.
Page 10 of 10
assets. This broad dual-use applicability enables manufacturing investments to be leveraged across
multiple defense and commercial markets, reducing production costs while accelerating transition and
commercialization.
4.0 Facilities / Equipment
Immobileyes shares its facilities with AlphaMicron, an ISO 9001-certified manufacturer headquartered
in a 30,000-square-foot facility in Kent, Ohio. The facility houses seven specialized laboratories,
including two electro-optics and laser laboratories, liquid-crystal fabrication clean rooms, precision
optical assembly areas, a MIL-STD environmental test suite, electronics integration and rapid-prototyping
capabilities, in-house 3D printing for accelerated prototype development and system integration.
The facility maintains an active DoD Facility Clearance (FCL), implements NIST SP 800-171 security
controls, has completed a CMMC Level 2 self-assessment, and maintains OSHA-compliant laser safety
procedures.
The laser labs feature a wide variety of continuous wave and pulsed lasers, spectrometers and control
electronics, amplifiers, wave form generators and detectors. The laser team, led by Dr. Bahman Taheri,
has expertise in optics, photonics, and two decades of research on Laser Eye-Protection.
The Immobileyes / AlphaMicron scientific team is composed of
distinguished cross-disciplinary optics and liquid crystal physicists,
chemists and engineers with diverse scientific and technical
backgrounds, collaborating to solve specific light related problems in
new ways. Immobileyes CTO Dr. Bahman Taheri has 30+ years in the
fields of laser optics and liquid crystal research and bring great depth of
knowledge to help develop new and innovative devices with new
applications..
PerceptView: An Idaho-based designer and manufacturer of advanced
electro-optical and infrared (EO/IR) imaging systems with extensive
experience supporting defense, aerospace, and homeland security
applications. The company develops rugged camera payloads, stabilized
pan-tilt systems, embedded vision processing, and
intelligent tracking solutions for government customers.
Its U.S. engineering and manufacturing facility provides
mechanical, electrical, software, and optical integration;
rapid prototyping; environmental and field testing; and
low-rate production, providing STORM with a secure,
domestic supply chain and an established path from
prototype to deployable systems.
Together, the two companies provide an end-to-end,
entirely U.S.-based capability spanning optical
component fabrication, laser integration, system
assembly, environmental testing, and production,
supporting a secure domestic supply chain.
